/* Quiz questions for each flashcard set: one question per card of the matching Physics & Maths Tutor set, in the same order.
   Written in our own words for IAL Revise. PMT's own cards are linked from each quiz and are not copied here.
   "UNIT/deck-slug": [[question, answer], ...] */
const DECK_QUIZ = {
"WPH11/mechanics":[
["Which term describes a physical quantity defined only by its size?","A scalar. It has magnitude only, with no direction."],
["What defines a vector, compared with a scalar?","A vector has both magnitude and direction."],
["Is acceleration a vector or a scalar?","Acceleration is a vector. It has a direction as well as a size."],
["Is mass a scalar or a vector?","Mass is a scalar. It has size but no direction."],
["How do mass and weight differ?","Mass is a scalar that does not depend on gravity. Weight is a vector that depends on gravitational field strength: W = mg."],
["In equilibrium, how do the total clockwise and anticlockwise moments compare?","They are equal. This is the principle of moments."],
["When an object is in equilibrium, what is its state of motion?","It has zero acceleration. It is at rest or moving with constant velocity."],
["Give two ways to check that several forces on an object balance.","Resolve the forces horizontally and vertically and check each total is zero. For three forces, a scale drawing should form a closed triangle."],
["Define the moment of a force.","The turning effect of a force. Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force."],
["What is a couple?","Two equal and opposite forces in the same plane. Their lines of action do not coincide, so they cause rotation only."],
["What is the centre of mass of an object?","The single point where the whole mass of the object can be considered to act."],
["Where is the centre of mass of a uniform solid?","At its geometric centre, because the mass is evenly spread."],
["What is the rate of change of displacement, and how is it found from a graph?","Velocity. It is the gradient of a displacement–time graph (the tangent for instantaneous velocity)."],
["What do the areas under velocity–time and acceleration–time graphs represent?","Under a velocity–time graph: displacement. Under an acceleration–time graph: change in velocity."],
["How does air resistance change as an object’s speed increases?","It increases. Air resistance is roughly proportional to speed squared."],
["A ball is launched horizontally from a castle at 6 m/s. How does its horizontal velocity change until it lands?","It stays constant at 6 m/s. No horizontal force acts, ignoring air resistance."],
["How do the suvat equations show that heavy and light objects fall at the same rate?","Mass does not appear in the suvat equations. So mass does not affect the motion of a freely falling object."],
["What is the vertical acceleration of a projectile?","It is g, the gravitational field strength, acting downwards."],
["Define terminal velocity.","The maximum constant speed of a falling object. The forces on it balance, so its acceleration is zero."],
["What is friction?","A force that opposes motion between surfaces, or of an object through a fluid. It acts opposite to the direction of motion."],
["Which of Newton’s laws describes pairs of equal and opposite forces?","Newton’s third law. Every force has an equal and opposite force acting on a different object."],
["What does Newton’s second law say about force?","F = ma for constant mass. Resultant force equals mass × acceleration."],
["What does Newton’s first law say?","An object stays at rest or moves at constant velocity unless a resultant force acts on it."],
["How do elastic and inelastic collisions differ?","Kinetic energy is conserved in an elastic collision. In an inelastic collision some kinetic energy is transferred to other forms."],
["Which equation links momentum, mass and velocity?","p = mv. Momentum equals mass × velocity."],
["True or false: linear momentum is conserved only in elastic collisions.","False. Momentum is conserved in all collisions, provided no external forces act."],
["What quantity equals the rate of change of momentum?","The resultant force. F = Δp/Δt."],
["What is impulse and how is it calculated?","The change in momentum caused by a force. Impulse = FΔt = Δ(mv)."],
["What does the area under a force–time graph give?","Impulse, which equals the change in momentum."],
["What does Fs cos θ represent?","Work done: the energy transferred by a force. θ is the angle between the force and the displacement."],
["What is power, in terms of work done?","Power is the rate of doing work. P = W/t."],
["How is efficiency calculated?","Efficiency = useful output power ÷ total input power (or useful energy out ÷ energy in)."],
["State the principle of conservation of energy.","Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred between forms. The total energy of a closed system stays constant."],
["A boat is pushed 19 N sideways by a current while its engine gives 45 N at right angles. Find the resultant force and its direction.","Resultant = √(45² + 19²) ≈ 49 N. Direction: about 67° from the current’s direction (tan⁻¹ 45/19)."],
["A ball leaves at 10 m/s at 30° above the horizontal. What are its horizontal and vertical velocity components?","Horizontal: 10 cos 30° ≈ 8.7 m/s. Vertical: 10 sin 30° = 5 m/s."],
["What force acts on an object that changes the direction of a fluid flowing past it?","Lift. It acts perpendicular to the direction of the fluid flow."]],
"WPH11/materials":[
["What is the difference between extension and compression of a spring?","Extension: forces pull outwards and the spring gets longer. Compression: forces push inwards and it gets shorter."],
["What types of deformation do stretching and squashing cause?","Stretching causes tensile deformation. Squashing causes compressive deformation."],
["State Hooke’s law and its limit.","Force is directly proportional to extension: F = kΔx. This holds only up to the limit of proportionality."],
["Define tensile stress and give its equation.","Force per unit cross-sectional area. σ = F/A, in pascals."],
["Define tensile strain. Does it have units?","Extension ÷ original length. It is a ratio, so it has no units."],
["Compare elastic and plastic deformation.","Elastic: the material returns to its original shape when the load is removed. Plastic: it stays permanently deformed."],
["Where does the work done in deforming a material elastically go?","It is stored as elastic potential (strain) energy."],
["What happens to the energy supplied during plastic deformation?","It is used to break bonds and move atoms past each other. The material is left permanently deformed and some energy is released as heat."],
["Define breaking stress.","The maximum stress a material can withstand before it breaks."],
["What does it mean for a material to be brittle?","It shows very little plastic deformation. It snaps suddenly rather than stretching."],
["What is the elastic limit?","The point beyond which the material deforms plastically and will not return to its original shape."],
["What does the area under a force–extension graph give?","The work done, which is the energy stored in the material (if it is elastic)."],
["Write the equation for elastic strain energy in terms of spring constant and extension.","E = ½kΔx². (Equivalently E = ½FΔx.)"],
["Define the Young modulus.","Young modulus = tensile stress ÷ tensile strain."],
["How do you find the Young modulus from a stress–strain graph?","Find the gradient of the straight-line (linear) part of the graph."],
["How can you tell from a loading–unloading graph that a wire has deformed plastically?","The unloading line does not return to the origin. The wire has a permanent extension."],
["Which measurements are needed for a Young modulus experiment with a wire?","Any three of: original length; extension (from initial and final lengths); diameter of the wire; the load (mass × g)."],
["What equipment is used in a Young modulus experiment with a wire, and what for?","Any four of: micrometer for the wire diameter; metre rule for the original length; vernier scale or travelling microscope for the extension; masses or a balance for the load; clamp and pulley to hold the wire."],
["How is the Young modulus worked out from wire measurements?","Stress = force ÷ cross-sectional area. Strain = extension ÷ original length. Young modulus = stress ÷ strain, the gradient of the stress–strain graph."]],
"WPH12/waves-and-particle-nature-of-light":[
["Define amplitude, frequency, period, wave speed and wavelength.","Any four of: amplitude is the maximum displacement from equilibrium; frequency is the number of oscillations per second; period is the time for one oscillation; wave speed is the distance travelled per second; wavelength is the length of one complete cycle."],
["Which equation links wave speed, frequency and wavelength?","v = fλ. Wave speed equals frequency × wavelength."],
["Compare transverse and longitudinal waves.","Transverse waves oscillate perpendicular to the energy transfer, have peaks and troughs, and can be polarised. Longitudinal waves oscillate parallel to it, have compressions and rarefactions, and cannot be polarised."],
["How do you read amplitude and wavelength from a displacement–distance graph?","Amplitude is the height from the equilibrium line to a peak. Wavelength is the horizontal distance between two neighbouring peaks."],
["Define wavefront, coherence, path difference, superposition and interference.","Any four of: a wavefront is a line of points oscillating in phase; coherent sources have the same frequency and a constant phase difference; path difference is the difference in distance travelled by two waves; superposition means the resultant displacement is the sum of the individual displacements; interference is the reinforcement or cancellation of overlapping waves."],
["How are phase difference and path difference related?","Phase difference = 2π × path difference ÷ λ (in radians). Phase difference decides whether interference is constructive or destructive."],
["What is a standing wave and how is one produced?","A wave that stores energy rather than transferring it. It forms when two progressive waves of the same frequency travel in opposite directions, often a wave and its reflection."],
["What are nodes and antinodes?","Nodes are points of zero displacement. Antinodes are points of maximum amplitude."],
["Give the equation for the speed of a wave on a string, and how length, mass per unit length and tension affect the fundamental frequency.","v = √(T/μ). A longer string, a heavier string or a lower tension each lower the fundamental frequency."],
["Define intensity.","Power per unit area. Unit: W m⁻²."],
["State Snell’s law and the critical angle formula.","n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. At the critical angle the refracted ray is at 90°, so sin C = 1/n (for a boundary with air)."],
["What is total internal reflection and when does it happen?","All the light is reflected back inside the denser medium. It happens when the angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle."],
["What is the principal axis of a lens?","A line through the centre of the lens, perpendicular to it."],
["Define focal length.","The distance from the centre of the lens to the principal focus. The principal focus is where rays parallel to the principal axis meet after the lens."],
["How is lens power related to focal length, and how do powers combine?","P = 1/f, with f in metres giving dioptres. For thin lenses in contact, the total power is the sum of the powers."],
["How do real and virtual images differ?","A real image forms where rays actually meet, so it can be shown on a screen. A virtual image is where rays only appear to come from, so it cannot be projected."],
["Which equation links focal length, object distance and image distance?","1/f = 1/u + 1/v."],
["Give two ways to calculate the magnification of a lens.","Image height ÷ object height. Image distance ÷ object distance."],
["What is plane polarisation and how can it be produced?","Oscillations are restricted to one plane. A polarising filter lets through only oscillations in one plane."],
["What is diffraction, and what does Huygens’ principle say?","Diffraction is the spreading of a wave through a gap or around an obstacle. Huygens: every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets that spread out at the wave speed."],
["Give the diffraction grating equation and define the symbols.","d sin θ = nλ. d is the slit spacing, θ the angle from the normal, n the order and λ the wavelength."],
["How does electron diffraction show that electrons behave as waves?","Electrons passing through a crystal produce a diffraction ring pattern. Diffraction is a wave property."],
["Find the de Broglie wavelength of an electron with momentum 5.4 × 10⁻²⁵ kg m s⁻¹.","λ = h/p = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ ÷ 5.4 × 10⁻²⁵ ≈ 1.2 × 10⁻⁹ m."],
["Find the energy of a photon of frequency 3 × 10⁶ Hz.","E = hf = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁶ ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻²⁷ J."],
["Which effect is evidence that light behaves as particles?","The photoelectric effect."],
["Describe the photoelectric effect.","Light above a threshold frequency shining on a metal releases electrons from its surface. The emitted electrons are called photoelectrons."],
["What is the threshold frequency?","The minimum frequency of light that releases electrons from a particular metal."],
["Which equations give a photon’s energy?","E = hf. E = hc/λ."],
["Why must a photon be above a certain frequency to release an electron?","A photon’s energy depends on its frequency. It must be at least the work function for an electron to escape, and one photon interacts with one electron."],
["What is the work function?","The minimum energy needed to remove an electron from the surface of a metal. Symbol φ."],
["What happens when a photon’s frequency is above the threshold frequency?","An electron is released. The energy left over becomes the electron’s kinetic energy."],
["If no electrons are emitted, what happens when the light intensity is increased?","Nothing. More photons arrive per second but each still has too little energy to release an electron."],
["State the photoelectric equation.","hf = φ + Ek(max). Photon energy = work function + maximum kinetic energy of the electron."],
["What is an electronvolt?","The energy gained by an electron accelerated through a potential difference of 1 V. 1 eV = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."],
["How do you convert between electronvolts and joules?","eV to J: multiply by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹. J to eV: divide by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹."],
["How does a fluorescent tube produce visible light?","Electrons accelerated by a high voltage excite mercury atoms, which emit UV photons when they de-excite. The phosphor coating absorbs the UV and emits visible light."],
["What is the evidence for discrete energy levels in atoms?","Line emission and absorption spectra. Atoms emit or absorb only specific wavelengths, matching exact energy differences between levels."],
["What is wave–particle duality?","Matter and light show both wave and particle behaviour. Light acts as particles in the photoelectric effect and as waves in diffraction."]],
"WPH12/electric-circuits":[
["Define current, potential difference and resistance, with equations.","Current is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t. Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge: V = W/Q. Resistance is opposition to current: R = V/I."],
["State Ohm’s law.","Current through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it. This holds only if temperature and other conditions stay constant."],
["How does current behave in series and in parallel circuits?","Series: the current is the same everywhere. Parallel: the current splits between the branches, and the branch currents add up to the total."],
["State Kirchhoff’s two laws and what each conserves.","First law: current into a junction equals current out (conservation of charge). Second law: the sum of e.m.f.s round a closed loop equals the sum of the p.d.s (conservation of energy)."],
["Give three equations for electrical power.","P = IV. P = I²R. P = V²/R."],
["Describe the I–V graphs of an ohmic conductor, a filament lamp, a thermistor and a diode.","Ohmic conductor: straight line through the origin. Filament lamp: curve that flattens as V increases. Thermistor: curve that gets steeper as V increases. Diode: almost no current until the threshold voltage in the forward direction, then a steep rise."],
["Define resistivity.","The resistance of a 1 m length of material with 1 m² cross-sectional area. It depends only on the material: ρ = RA/l."],
["What do the symbols in I = nAvq stand for?","n is the number of charge carriers per unit volume; A the cross-sectional area; v the drift velocity; q the charge on each carrier."],
["How can the resistance of a wire be increased?","Any two of: use a material with higher resistivity; make the wire longer; make it thinner (smaller cross-sectional area)."],
["Give the output voltage of a potential divider taken across R₁.","Vout = Vin × R₁ ÷ (R₁ + R₂). Across R₂ instead, R₂ goes on the top."],
["How can a thermistor in parallel with a heater act as a thermostat?","As the room warms, the thermistor’s resistance falls and more current flows through its branch. Less current flows through the heater, so it heats less."],
["Define e.m.f., internal resistance and lost volts.","E.m.f. is the energy given to each coulomb of charge by the source. Internal resistance is the resistance inside the source. Lost volts is the p.d. across the internal resistance, Ir."],
["What is terminal potential difference?","The p.d. across the terminals of a source, delivered to the external circuit. It equals the e.m.f. minus the lost volts."],
["Write two equations linking e.m.f., terminal p.d., current and internal resistance.","ε = IR + Ir. ε = V + Ir, where V is the terminal p.d."],
["Explain why the resistance of a metal increases as it gets hotter.","The lattice ions vibrate more. Electrons collide with them more often, so resistance increases and current falls for a fixed p.d."],
["Explain why the resistance of an LDR falls as light intensity increases.","More light releases more charge carriers, so n increases. For fixed p.d. the current increases, so resistance falls."]],
"WPH14/further-mechanics":[
["In a collision, which component of momentum is transferred between the objects?","Only the component along the line of impact is transferred. The component perpendicular to the line of impact is unchanged."],
["Write the impulse equation in terms of force, time and velocities.","FΔt = Δ(mv) = mv − mu. Impulse equals the change in momentum."],
["Name two pairs of perpendicular directions you can use to resolve momentum.","Horizontal and vertical. Parallel and perpendicular to the line of impact."],
["How do elastic and inelastic collisions differ?","In an elastic collision both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved. In an inelastic collision kinetic energy is not conserved."],
["Show how kinetic energy can be written in terms of momentum.","Ek = ½mv² and p = mv, so v = p/m. Substituting gives Ek = p²/2m."],
["What is angular displacement and what is its unit?","The angle an object turns through about a point. Measured in radians, where angle = arc length ÷ radius."],
["Define angular velocity and link it to linear speed.","ω = θ/t, the angle turned per unit time. Linear speed v = ωr."],
["Link frequency and period to angular velocity.","ω = 2πf, where f is revolutions per second. T = 2π/ω, the time for one revolution."],
["What is needed to keep an object moving in a circle at constant speed?","A resultant (centripetal) force of constant size, always directed towards the centre of the circle."],
["What causes centripetal acceleration, and what equations follow from F = ma?","A centripetal force towards the centre. F = mv²/r and F = mrω²."],
["Outline how a = v²/r is derived.","The direction of velocity changes continuously, so Δv points towards the centre. Similar triangles give Δv/v = s/r with s = vΔt. So Δv/Δt = v²/r."],
["Give all forms of the centripetal force equation.","F = ma = mv²/r = mrω²."]],
"WPH14/electric-and-magnetic-fields":[
["What is an electric field and how is field strength defined?","A region where a charged object experiences a force. E = F/Q, the force per unit positive charge."],
["Give Coulomb’s law and define the symbols.","F = kQq/r², with k = 1/4πε₀ ≈ 8.99 × 10⁹ N m² C⁻². Q and q are the charges and r is their separation."],
["How does the field strength of a point charge vary with distance?","It follows an inverse square law. E = kQ/r²."],
["What is electric potential at a point? Give the formula for a point charge.","The work done per unit positive charge in bringing it from infinity to that point. V = kQ/r."],
["How are p.d., field strength and separation related for parallel plates?","V = Ed. The field between the plates is uniform: E = V/d."],
["Describe the field lines around an isolated point charge.","They are radial: outwards from a positive charge, inwards to a negative charge."],
["What are equipotentials, and how much work is done moving a charge along one?","Lines or surfaces joining points at the same potential. No work is done moving a charge along an equipotential."],
["What is a capacitor and how is capacitance defined?","Two conducting plates separated by an insulator, which stores charge. C = Q/V."],
["Give three expressions for the energy stored in a capacitor.","W = ½QV. W = ½CV². W = Q²/2C."],
["What two factors control how quickly a capacitor discharges?","The capacitance and the resistance. The time constant is RC."],
["Give the equation for charge on a discharging capacitor.","Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). Current and p.d. follow the same pattern: I = I₀e^(−t/RC) and V = V₀e^(−t/RC)."],
["What is the time constant and what fraction of charge is left after one time constant?","τ = RC. About 37% (1/e) of the original charge remains."],
["How do you find RC from a graph of ln Q against time?","The gradient is −1/RC. So RC = −1 ÷ gradient."],
["What is a magnetic field?","A region where a force acts on magnetic materials, moving charges or current-carrying wires."],
["Describe the magnetic field around a straight current-carrying wire.","Concentric circles centred on the wire. The direction is given by the right-hand grip rule."],
["How does the right-hand grip rule work?","Point your thumb along the conventional current. Your curled fingers show the direction of the field."],
["Why does a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field feel a force?","The wire’s own magnetic field interacts with the external field, producing a force."],
["In Fleming’s left-hand rule, what do the thumb, first finger and second finger show?","Thumb: force (motion). First finger: field. Second finger: current."],
["Define magnetic flux density.","The force per unit length per unit current on a wire at right angles to the field. Unit: tesla."],
["Give the force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field. What is θ?","F = BIl sin θ. θ is the angle between the wire (current) and the field."],
["Give the force on a charge moving in a magnetic field. What is θ?","F = Bqv sin θ. θ is the angle between the velocity and the field."],
["When is an e.m.f. induced in a conductor?","When it cuts magnetic field lines, or the flux linking it changes, e.g. a magnet moves relative to a coil."],
["How can the induced e.m.f. in a coil be increased?","Any two of: move the magnet or coil faster; use more turns; use a stronger magnetic field; use a larger coil area."],
["Define magnetic flux.","Flux density × the area perpendicular to the field. Φ = BA."],
["State Faraday’s law.","The induced e.m.f. is proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linkage."],
["State Lenz’s law.","The induced e.m.f. acts in a direction that opposes the change that produced it."],
["How is r.m.s. voltage found from peak voltage, and what does it mean?","Vrms = V₀/√2. It is the steady (d.c.) voltage that would deliver the same power."]],
"WPH14/nuclear-and-particle-physics":[
["What did the alpha-particle scattering experiment show, and why did it disprove the plum-pudding model?","Most alpha particles passed straight through, so the atom is mostly empty space. A few were deflected through large angles by a tiny, dense, positive nucleus, which the plum-pudding model could not explain."],
["What do the proton number and nucleon number count?","Proton number: the number of protons in the nucleus. Nucleon number: the total number of protons and neutrons."],
["How does a cyclotron accelerate charged particles?","A magnetic field bends the particles into a circular path inside two D-shaped electrodes. An alternating p.d. across the gap accelerates them each time they cross, so the radius grows until they leave."],
["Show that the radius of a charged particle’s path in a magnetic field is r = p/Bq.","Magnetic force Bqv provides the centripetal force mv²/r. So r = mv/Bq = p/Bq."],
["Define the electronvolt.","The kinetic energy gained by an electron accelerated through a potential difference of 1 V."],
["How does a linear accelerator speed up particles?","Alternating p.d. across a series of tubes. The particle is accelerated across each gap, and the tubes get longer because it moves faster while the frequency is constant."],
["How does an electron gun produce a beam of electrons?","A heated cathode releases electrons by thermionic emission. A positive anode accelerates them into a beam."],
["What are hadrons made of?","Quarks."],
["Name the two common baryons. Which is the only stable baryon?","Protons and neutrons. The proton is the only stable baryon."],
["Name the six leptons.","Electron, muon, tau, and the electron neutrino, muon neutrino and tau neutrino."],
["How is an antiparticle related to its particle?","Same mass. Opposite charge, baryon number and lepton number."],
["Which equation links energy and mass in pair production and annihilation?","ΔE = c²Δm (E = mc²)."],
["What are quarks?","Fundamental particles that make up hadrons, such as protons, neutrons and mesons."],
["Give the charges of the up, down and strange quarks.","Up +⅔. Down −⅓. Strange −⅓."],
["Give the quark make-up of a proton, antiproton, neutron and antineutron.","Proton uud. Antiproton ūūd̄. Neutron udd. Antineutron ūd̄d̄."],
["Which quantities are conserved in particle interactions?","Any four of: energy; momentum; charge; baryon number; lepton number."],
["How do cloud chambers and bubble chambers show particle tracks?","Cloud chamber: supersaturated vapour condenses on the ions along a particle’s path. Bubble chamber: superheated liquid boils into bubbles along the ion track when the pressure is lowered."]],
"WPH15/thermodynamics":[
["Which equation gives the energy needed to change the temperature of a substance?","ΔE = mcΔθ. m is mass in kg, c the specific heat capacity in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, Δθ the temperature change."],
["Which equation gives the energy needed to change the state of a substance?","ΔE = LΔm. L is the specific latent heat in J kg⁻¹."],
["What is internal energy?","The sum of the random kinetic and potential energies of all the molecules in a system."],
["What affects the kinetic and potential energies of molecules?","Kinetic energy depends on their speed, which increases with temperature. Potential energy depends on the forces and separation between molecules, which change with state."],
["How do the kelvin and Celsius scales differ?","The kelvin scale is absolute, starting at absolute zero, 0 K ≈ −273 °C. A change of 1 K equals a change of 1 °C."],
["Define specific heat capacity.","The energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K."],
["Describe the arrangement and motion of molecules in solids, liquids and gases.","Solids: regular lattice, vibrating about fixed positions, strong forces. Liquids: close together but free to move past each other. Gases: far apart, moving randomly at high speed."],
["What happens to internal energy during a change of state?","Internal energy changes but temperature stays constant. The potential energy of the molecules changes, not their kinetic energy."],
["Define specific latent heat.","The energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without changing its temperature."],
["How are pressure and volume related at constant temperature, and why?","Inversely proportional: pV = constant (Boyle’s law). In a bigger volume molecules hit the walls less often, so pressure falls."],
["How does volume depend on temperature at constant pressure?","V is directly proportional to absolute temperature: V/T = constant. Faster molecules push the walls out until pressure is restored."],
["How does pressure depend on temperature at constant volume?","p is directly proportional to absolute temperature. Faster molecules hit the walls harder and more often."],
["Write the ideal gas equation with number of molecules.","pV = NkT. N is the number of molecules, k the Boltzmann constant, T in kelvin."],
["Write the average kinetic energy of a gas molecule in terms of temperature.","½m⟨c²⟩ = (3/2)kT. It is proportional to absolute temperature."]],
"WPH15/nuclear-decay":[
["Which four types of radiation can an unstable nucleus emit?","Alpha, beta-minus, beta-plus and gamma."],
["What makes a nucleus unstable?","Too many neutrons, too many protons, too many nucleons overall, or too much energy."],
["Describe alpha radiation: ionising power, speed, penetration and effect of a magnetic field.","Strongly ionising and slow. Stopped by a few cm of air or a sheet of paper. Deflected by magnetic fields because it is charged."],
["Describe beta-minus radiation: ionising power, speed, penetration and effect of a magnetic field.","Weakly ionising and fast. Stopped by a few mm of aluminium. Deflected by magnetic fields, in the opposite direction to alpha."],
["Describe gamma radiation: ionising power, speed, penetration and effect of a magnetic field.","Very weakly ionising and travels at the speed of light. Needs many cm of lead or metres of concrete to stop it. Not deflected by magnetic fields."],
["What is the decay constant, and how is activity related to it?","The probability that a nucleus decays per second. Activity A = λN, the number of decays per second."],
["Define half-life and say how it is measured in practice.","The average time for the number of undecayed nuclei to halve. In practice, the time for the activity or count rate to halve."],
["Which equation links decay constant and half-life?","λ = ln 2 ÷ t½."],
["What is mass deficit and how is it linked to binding energy?","The difference between the mass of the separate nucleons and the mass of the nucleus. It is equivalent to the binding energy by E = mc²."],
["How do you calculate binding energy per nucleon?","Divide the total binding energy by the nucleon number."],
["Why does uranium-235 split after absorbing a thermal neutron?","The nucleus becomes very unstable. It splits into two daughter nuclei and releases more neutrons and energy."],
["Define the atomic mass unit and give its values.","1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. 1 u = 1.661 × 10⁻²⁷ kg, equivalent to 931.5 MeV."],
["What happens in nuclear fusion, and how does binding energy per nucleon change?","Two light nuclei join to make a heavier nucleus. The binding energy per nucleon increases, so energy is released."],
["Why is fusion hard to achieve on Earth?","Nuclei are positive and repel strongly. Very high temperatures and densities are needed to get them close enough for the strong force to act."],
["A student records a count rate of 14 s⁻¹ from a source. Why might this be too high?","Background radiation has not been subtracted."],
["Give the equations for exponential decay of undecayed nuclei and of activity.","N = N₀e^(−λt). A = A₀e^(−λt)."]],
"WPH15/astrophysics-and-cosmology":[
["What is a gravitational field?","A region where a mass experiences a force without contact."],
["Which objects experience a gravitational force?","Any object with mass. The force is always attractive."],
["Apart from separation, what decides the size of the gravitational force between two bodies?","Their masses. F = GMm/r²."],
["What is a point mass?","An idealised body with mass but no size, with all its mass acting at one point."],
["How does the spacing of field lines show field strength?","Closely spaced lines: strong field. Widely spaced lines: weak field."],
["How does gravitational force vary with separation?","It follows an inverse square law: F ∝ 1/r²."],
["Define gravitational field strength.","Force per unit mass. g = F/m."],
["Derive the field strength of a radial gravitational field.","Divide F = GMm/r² by m. This gives g = GM/r²."],
["Define gravitational potential and give its equation.","The work done per unit mass bringing a mass from infinity to that point. It is zero at infinity and negative near a mass: V = −GM/r."],
["What work is done moving a mass through a change in potential?","W = mΔV."],
["What is gravitational potential difference?","The difference in gravitational potential between two points in a field."],
["Give similarities between gravitational and electric fields.","Any two of: field strength is force per unit mass or per unit charge; both follow inverse square laws for point sources; potential is zero at infinity in both; both use field lines."],
["Give differences between gravitational and electric fields.","Any two of: gravity is always attractive but electric forces can repel; gravitational fields cannot be shielded; the medium affects electric force but not gravity."],
["What is a black body radiator?","An ideal object that absorbs all wavelengths of radiation that fall on it. It emits radiation at all wavelengths, depending only on its temperature."],
["State Wien’s law.","λmax T = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m K. λmax is the wavelength at which the intensity is greatest."],
["Write the Stefan–Boltzmann law.","L = σAT⁴. L is luminosity in W, σ the Stefan–Boltzmann constant, A the surface area, T the temperature in K."],
["What is an astronomical unit?","The mean distance from the Earth to the Sun, about 1.5 × 10¹¹ m."],
["What is a light-year?","The distance light travels in one year, about 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m (about 63 000 AU)."],
["How is trigonometric parallax used, and what limits it?","Measure a nearby star’s apparent shift against distant stars from opposite sides of Earth’s orbit, then use trigonometry. Very distant stars give angles too small to measure."],
["How can a star’s surface temperature be found from its luminosity and radius?","Rearrange L = 4πr²σT⁴ to find T."],
["How are Cepheid variables used as standard candles?","Their luminosity is known from their period. Comparing it with the measured intensity using I = L/4πd² gives the distance to their galaxy."],
["What does a Hertzsprung–Russell diagram show?","Luminosity against surface temperature. Stars group into the main sequence, red giants and white dwarfs."],
["Describe the first stages in the life of a star.","A cloud of gas and dust collapses under gravity to form a protostar. The core heats until hydrogen fusion into helium begins."],
["What happens after the protostar stage, and what causes the next change?","The star is in equilibrium on the main sequence. When core hydrogen runs out it becomes a red giant (or a red supergiant if massive)."],
["How does a low-mass star end its life?","The red giant sheds its outer layers. The core contracts into a hot, dim white dwarf."],
["How does a high-mass star end its life?","The red supergiant fuses elements up to iron, then explodes as a supernova. The core becomes a neutron star, or a black hole if massive enough."],
["How do neutron stars and black holes differ?","A neutron star is about 10 km across and spins rapidly, often emitting radio beams. A black hole is so dense that its escape velocity exceeds the speed of light."],
["How can an H–R diagram estimate the age of a star cluster?","Find where the stars turn off the main sequence. Massive stars leave first, so the lower the turn-off point, the older the cluster."],
["Define the Doppler effect.","The change in observed frequency and wavelength when the source moves relative to the observer."],
["What does the size of the Doppler shift depend on?","The velocity of the source relative to the observer."],
["Write the red shift equation.","z = Δλ/λ ≈ Δf/f ≈ v/c."],
["State Hubble’s law.","Recession velocity is proportional to distance: v = H₀d. H₀ is often given in km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹."],
["What does red shift tell us about the universe?","The universe is expanding. More distant galaxies are receding faster."],
["How does the Hubble constant give the age of the universe?","Age ≈ 1/H₀, with H₀ converted to s⁻¹. For H₀ ≈ 65 km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹ this is about 1.5 × 10¹⁰ years."],
["Summarise the Big Bang model.","The universe began in an extremely hot, dense state and has been expanding and cooling ever since."],
["What is the critical density of the universe?","The density at which the universe would expand forever but at an ever-slowing rate, approaching a constant size."],
["Describe the fate of the universe if its density is above, equal to or below the critical density.","Above: expansion stops and it collapses (big crunch). Equal: expansion slows towards zero. Below: it expands forever."],
["Why is it hard to measure the average density of the universe?","Dark matter does not emit light, so its mass is hard to measure."]],
"WPH15/oscillations":[
["What kind of motion has a restoring force proportional to displacement and in the opposite direction?","Simple harmonic motion. F = −kx."],
["Define displacement, amplitude, period and frequency for an oscillation.","Displacement: distance from equilibrium. Amplitude: maximum displacement. Period: time for one oscillation. Frequency: oscillations per second."],
["Which equation links angular frequency and period?","ω = 2π/T (and ω = 2πf)."],
["What are the two conditions for simple harmonic motion?","Acceleration is proportional to displacement from equilibrium. It is always directed towards the equilibrium position: a = −ω²x."],
["Name two systems that perform simple harmonic motion.","A mass on a spring. A simple pendulum (small swings)."],
["In a = (constant) × x for SHM, what is the constant?","−ω²."],
["True or false: velocity is greatest when displacement is greatest.","False. Velocity is zero at maximum displacement. It is greatest at the equilibrium position."],
["How do you find the maximum speed of an oscillator?","vmax = ωA."],
["Give displacement, velocity and acceleration as functions of time in SHM.","x = A cos ωt. v = −Aω sin ωt. a = −Aω² cos ωt."],
["Give the period of a mass–spring system and of a simple pendulum.","Mass–spring: T = 2π√(m/k). Pendulum: T = 2π√(l/g)."],
["What is damping and what causes it?","The decrease in amplitude of an oscillation over time. Energy is lost to resistive forces such as friction or drag."],
["Compare light, heavy and critical damping.","Light: amplitude falls gradually while it keeps oscillating. Heavy: returns to equilibrium slowly without oscillating. Critical: returns to equilibrium in the shortest time without oscillating."],
["How do free and forced oscillations differ?","Free: no driving force, oscillates at its natural frequency. Forced: a periodic driving force makes it oscillate at the driving frequency."],
["What happens at resonance, and how does damping change the resonance peak?","When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency, the amplitude becomes very large. More damping gives a lower, broader peak at a slightly lower frequency."],
["Outline how to investigate resonance of a mass between two springs.","Drive the system with a vibration generator. Increase the frequency gradually and measure the amplitude against a ruler. Filming and reading frames improves accuracy."]],
"WCH11/formulae-equations-and-amount-of-substance":[
["What is the smallest particle of an element that keeps its identity?","An atom."],
["What kind of atoms does a pure element contain?","Only one type of atom."],
["What is an ion?","An atom or group of atoms with an overall charge, from gaining or losing electrons."],
["What is a molecule?","Two or more atoms joined by chemical (covalent) bonds."],
["How does a compound differ from an element?","A compound contains atoms of two or more different elements chemically bonded together."],
["What is an empirical formula?","The simplest whole-number ratio of atoms of each element in a compound."],
["What is a molecular formula?","The actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule."],
["What is the empirical formula of Fe₂O₄?","FeO₂. Divide both numbers by 2."],
["A compound has empirical formula C₄H₃O₂ and Mr 166. What is its molecular formula?","C₈H₆O₄. The empirical mass is 83, and 166 ÷ 83 = 2."],
["What is the SI unit for amount of substance?","The mole (mol)."],
["Define the mole.","The amount of substance containing as many particles as there are atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12."],
["What is the Avogadro constant?","The number of particles in one mole: 6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹."],
["Which equation links number of particles, moles and the Avogadro constant?","Number of particles = moles × Avogadro constant."],
["How many moles are in 4.816 × 10²⁴ iron atoms?","8 mol. 4.816 × 10²⁴ ÷ 6.02 × 10²³."],
["What do the state symbols (g), (aq), (s) and (l) mean?","Gas; aqueous (dissolved in water); solid; liquid."],
["What is an ionic equation?","An equation showing only the ions that react, leaving out spectator ions, e.g. Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s)."],
["Define relative atomic mass.","The weighted mean mass of an atom of an element compared with 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom."],
["What is relative formula mass?","The sum of the relative atomic masses in one formula unit, used for ionic compounds."],
["What is relative molecular mass?","The sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms in a molecule."],
["Define molar mass and give its unit.","The mass of one mole of a substance. Unit: g mol⁻¹."],
["What does ppm mean?","Parts per million: the amount of one component per million parts of the whole."],
["What is 1 ppm in mass/volume and mass/mass units?","1 ppm = 1 mg dm⁻³ (1 μg cm⁻³). 1 ppm = 1 mg kg⁻¹."],
["Convert 2 mg cm⁻³ to ppm.","2000 ppm. 2 mg cm⁻³ = 2000 μg cm⁻³."],
["Convert 0.75 g kg⁻¹ to ppm.","750 ppm. 0.75 g kg⁻¹ = 750 mg kg⁻¹."],
["What does concentration measure?","The amount of solute dissolved in 1 dm³ of solution."],
["How do you calculate concentration in g dm⁻³?","Mass in g ÷ volume in dm³."],
["How do you calculate concentration in mol dm⁻³?","Moles ÷ volume in dm³."],
["In C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O, what is the mole ratio of propane to water?","1 : 4."],
["How do you calculate moles from mass?","Moles = mass ÷ molar mass (Mr)."],
["What volume does one mole of any gas occupy at r.t.p.?","24 dm³."],
["What are room temperature and pressure?","About 20 °C and 1 atm (101 kPa)."],
["Which equation links gas volume at r.t.p. and moles?","Volume (dm³) = moles × 24."],
["How many moles of oxygen are in 72 dm³ at r.t.p.?","3 mol. 72 ÷ 24."],
["Write the ideal gas equation with units.","pV = nRT. p in Pa, V in m³, T in K, R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹."],
["A gas at 55.0 °C and 275 kPa occupies 1.10 dm³. How many moles are there?","About 0.11 mol. n = pV/RT = 275 000 × 0.00110 ÷ (8.31 × 328)."],
["How is percentage yield calculated?","Actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100."],
["20.0 mol H₂ reacts with excess N₂ (N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃) and 40.5 g NH₃ forms. Find the percentage yield.","About 17.8%. Theoretical NH₃ = 13.3 mol × 17 ≈ 227 g, and 40.5 ÷ 227 × 100 ≈ 17.8%."],
["Give three reasons why actual yield is less than expected.","Any three of: the reaction is incomplete or reversible; side reactions; product lost during transfer or purification; impure reactants."],
["What does atom economy measure?","How much of the mass of the reactants ends up in the desired product."],
["How is atom economy calculated?","Molar mass of desired product ÷ total molar mass of all products × 100."],
["Does a higher or lower atom economy mean a more sustainable process?","Higher. Less waste is produced."],
["What happens in a displacement reaction?","A more reactive element replaces a less reactive one in a compound: A + BC → AC + B."],
["Write the equation for bromine reacting with potassium iodide.","Br₂ + 2KI → 2KBr + I₂."],
["What forms when an acid reacts with a metal?","A salt and hydrogen."],
["What forms when an acid reacts with a metal carbonate?","A salt, water and carbon dioxide."],
["What forms when an acid reacts with a metal oxide?","A salt and water."],
["Write the equation for hydrochloric acid reacting with sodium.","2Na + 2HCl → 2NaCl + H₂."],
["Write the equation for sulfuric acid reacting with magnesium oxide.","MgO + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + H₂O."],
["What is a precipitation reaction?","Two soluble substances react in solution to form an insoluble solid, the precipitate."]],
"WCH11/atomic-structure-and-the-periodic-table":[
["Which three subatomic particles make up an atom?","Protons, neutrons and electrons."],
["Describe the structure of an atom.","A small dense nucleus contains protons (positive) and neutrons (neutral). Electrons (negative) occupy shells around the nucleus."],
["Where is almost all the mass of an atom?","In the nucleus."],
["Give the relative masses of a proton, neutron and electron.","Proton 1, neutron 1, electron 1/1836."],
["Give the relative charges of a proton, neutron and electron.","Proton +1, neutron 0, electron −1."],
["What is the atomic number?","The number of protons in the nucleus. It is unique to each element."],
["What is the mass number?","The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus."],
["What are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons called?","Isotopes."],
["How do isotopes compare in atomic number and mass number?","Same atomic number (same protons). Different mass number (different neutrons)."],
["Why does a neutral atom have equal numbers of protons and electrons?","Their charges are equal and opposite, so they cancel to give zero overall charge."],
["How do you find the number of neutrons?","Mass number − atomic number."],
["Boron has atomic number 5 and mass number 11. How many protons, electrons and neutrons?","5 protons, 5 electrons, 6 neutrons."],
["How many protons, electrons and neutrons are in Na⁺ (atomic number 11, mass number 23)?","11 protons, 10 electrons, 12 neutrons."],
["Chlorine is 75% Cl-35 and 25% Cl-37. What peaks appear for Cl₂ in a mass spectrum?","Molecular ion peaks at m/z 70, 72 and 74 in the ratio 9 : 6 : 1. Peaks at 35 and 37 from Cl atoms."],
["Can ions in a mass spectrometer have a 2+ charge?","Yes, but 1+ ions are much more common."],
["What does the peak with the highest m/z (the molecular ion) tell you?","Its m/z value equals the relative molecular mass of the compound."],
["Define first ionisation energy.","The energy needed to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms, forming one mole of gaseous 1+ ions."],
["Define second ionisation energy.","The energy needed to remove one electron from each ion in one mole of gaseous 1+ ions, forming gaseous 2+ ions."],
["Is ionisation endothermic or exothermic?","Endothermic: energy is needed."],
["What affects the size of ionisation energy?","Nuclear charge, distance of the electron from the nucleus (and which sub-shell), and shielding."],
["How do successive ionisation energies show the group of an element?","There is a big jump when an electron is removed from a new, inner shell. The number of electrons removed before the jump is the group number."],
["What does the pattern of first ionisation energies across a period provide evidence for?","Sub-shells (s and p) within the electron shells."],
["What is an orbital?","A region that can hold up to two electrons with opposite spins."],
["What is a sub-shell, and what are they called?","A group of orbitals with the same energy within a shell. They are s, p, d and f."],
["What rules do electrons follow when filling orbitals?","They fill the lowest energy orbitals first. They occupy orbitals of a sub-shell singly before pairing."],
["How many orbitals and electrons do s, p, d and f sub-shells hold?","s: 1 orbital, 2 electrons. p: 3, 6. d: 5, 10. f: 7, 14."],
["How many electrons can shells 1 to 4 hold?","2, 8, 18 and 32."],
["Why does 4s fill before 3d?","The 4s sub-shell is lower in energy than 3d."],
["Write the electron configuration of calcium.","1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 4s²."],
["Which two elements break the usual filling order, and what are their configurations?","Chromium [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ and copper [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹. A half-full or full 3d sub-shell is more stable."],
["What decides the chemical properties of an element?","Its electron configuration, especially its outer electrons."],
["How is the periodic table divided into blocks?","s, p, d and f blocks, according to the sub-shell holding the outermost electrons."],
["What is periodicity?","A repeating pattern of properties across each period when elements are arranged by atomic number."],
["How does first ionisation energy change across Periods 2 and 3?","It generally increases from left to right."],
["Why does first ionisation energy fall from nitrogen to oxygen?","In oxygen the electron is removed from a paired p orbital. Repulsion between the paired electrons makes it easier to remove."],
["Why is aluminium’s first ionisation energy lower than magnesium’s?","Aluminium’s outer electron is in a 3p sub-shell, higher in energy than 3s. It is also slightly shielded by the 3s electrons."],
["Why does first ionisation energy increase across a period?","Nuclear charge increases. Atomic radius decreases. Shielding stays about the same."],
["Why does first ionisation energy decrease down a group?","The outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is held less strongly."]],
"WCH11/bonding-and-structure":[
["How does an atom become an ion?","By gaining or losing one or more electrons."],
["What happens to Cu²⁺ and CrO₄²⁻ ions when a current passes, and what does it show?","Blue Cu²⁺ moves to the negative electrode and yellow CrO₄²⁻ moves to the positive electrode. This shows ions exist and are charged."],
["What do electron density maps of sodium chloride show?","A regular arrangement of separate ions, with almost zero electron density between them. Chloride ions are larger than sodium ions."],
["What is an ionic bond?","The strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions."],
["What is a giant ionic lattice?","A three-dimensional regular arrangement of ions held together by electrostatic attraction."],
["How does ionic radius change down a group, and why?","It increases, because each ion has an extra shell of electrons."],
["How do ionic charge and radius affect the strength of ionic bonding?","Higher charge and smaller radius give higher charge density and stronger ionic bonding."],
["What are isoelectronic species?","Ions or atoms with the same electron configuration."],
["How does ionic radius change from N³⁻ to Al³⁺, and why?","It decreases. The number of electrons stays the same but the nuclear charge increases, pulling them closer."],
["What is polarisation in ionic bonding?","A cation distorts the electron cloud of a nearby anion. A lot of distortion gives the bond some covalent character."],
["What decides the polarising power of a cation?","Its charge density: high charge and small radius mean high polarising power."],
["What decides how easily an anion is polarised?","Its size (and charge): large anions are polarised more easily."],
["What is a covalent bond?","The electrostatic attraction between two nuclei and a shared pair of electrons."],
["Give two properties of giant covalent structures.","Any two of: very high melting and boiling points; do not conduct electricity (except graphite); insoluble in water; hard."],
["Give two properties of simple molecular substances.","Any two of: low melting and boiling points due to weak intermolecular forces; do not conduct electricity; often gases or liquids at room temperature."],
["What is a dative covalent bond?","A covalent bond in which both electrons come from the same atom."],
["Describe the structure of graphite.","Each carbon is bonded to three others in hexagonal layers. There are weak forces between the layers and one delocalised electron per carbon."],
["Why is graphite soft and why does it conduct?","The layers slide over each other easily. Delocalised electrons can move and carry charge."],
["Describe the structure of diamond.","Each carbon is bonded to four others in a rigid three-dimensional tetrahedral network. There are no delocalised electrons."],
["Give two properties of diamond.","Any two of: very hard; very high melting point; does not conduct electricity."],
["What is graphite used for and why?","Electrodes, because it conducts and has a high melting point. Lubricant, because the layers slide."],
["Why is diamond used in cutting tools?","It is extremely hard because of its network of strong covalent bonds."],
["Give two properties of graphene.","Any two of: very strong; high melting point; conducts electricity because of delocalised electrons; one atom thick and transparent."],
["Why is graphene useful in electronics?","It conducts electricity well and is very strong, light and thin (a single layer of graphite)."],
["Define electronegativity.","The ability of an atom to attract the bonding pair of electrons in a covalent bond."],
["How does the electronegativity difference affect bonding?","A large difference gives ionic bonding or a polar bond. Similar values give non-polar covalent bonding. Bonding is a continuum between the two."],
["How does electron-pair repulsion theory predict shapes?","Electron pairs around the central atom repel and get as far apart as possible. Lone pair–lone pair repulsion is greatest, then lone pair–bond pair, then bond pair–bond pair."],
["Define bond length.","The distance between the nuclei of two bonded atoms."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of BeCl₂.","Linear, 180°."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of BCl₃.","Trigonal planar, 120°."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of CH₄.","Tetrahedral, 109.5°."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of NH₃.","Trigonal pyramidal, about 107°."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of NH₄⁺.","Tetrahedral, 109.5°."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of H₂O.","Bent (V-shaped), about 104.5°."],
["Give the shape and bond angles of PCl₅.","Trigonal bipyramidal, 90° and 120°."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of SF₆.","Octahedral, 90°."],
["What is metallic bonding?","The electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons in a giant lattice."],
["Why do metals conduct electricity?","Delocalised electrons can move through the structure and carry charge."],
["Why do metals have high melting points?","There is strong attraction between the positive ions and the delocalised electrons, which needs a lot of energy to overcome."]],
"WCH11/introductory-organic-chemistry-and-alkanes":[
["What is a hazard?","Anything that could cause harm, such as a chemical or a Bunsen burner."],
["What is a risk?","The chance of harm happening from a hazard, and how bad it could be."],
["Why is a risk assessment carried out?","To identify hazards and risks, and to plan precautions and what to do if something goes wrong."],
["Give two ways to reduce risk in a reaction.","Any two of: work on a smaller scale; use precautions suited to each hazard; use less hazardous chemicals or a safer method."],
["What is a homologous series?","A family of compounds with the same functional group and general formula, each member differing by CH₂."],
["What is a functional group?","The atom or group of atoms responsible for a compound’s characteristic reactions."],
["What does a structural formula show?","The arrangement of atoms in a molecule without drawing every bond, e.g. CH₃CH(OH)CH₃."],
["What does a displayed formula show?","Every atom and every bond."],
["What is a skeletal formula?","Carbon chains are drawn as lines, with only functional groups shown as symbols."],
["What is an addition reaction?","Two molecules join to form one product, with nothing else formed."],
["What is a substitution reaction?","An atom or group in a molecule is replaced by a different atom or group."],
["What happens in oxidation?","Loss of electrons."],
["What happens in reduction?","Gain of electrons."],
["What is polymerisation?","Many small monomer molecules join to form a long polymer chain."],
["Name the two types of bond breaking and what each forms.","Homolytic fission forms two radicals. Heterolytic fission forms a positive and a negative ion."],
["What is an electrophile?","A species that accepts a pair of electrons."],
["What is a free radical?","A species with an unpaired electron."],
["What is an alkane?","A saturated hydrocarbon with general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂."],
["What is the general formula and structure of cycloalkanes?","A ring of carbon atoms joined by single bonds. CₙH₂ₙ."],
["What does saturated mean?","The compound contains only single carbon–carbon bonds."],
["What is structural isomerism?","Same molecular formula but different structural formula."],
["Name the three types of structural isomerism.","Chain, position and functional group isomerism."],
["Name the three structural isomers of C₅H₁₂.","Pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane."],
["Name the first four alkanes and their formulae.","Methane CH₄, ethane C₂H₆, propane C₃H₈, butane C₄H₁₀."],
["What type of bonds are in alkanes?","Covalent bonds (C–C and C–H single bonds)."],
["Name three processes used to obtain fuels from crude oil.","Fractional distillation, cracking and reforming."],
["How does fractional distillation separate crude oil?","Crude oil is vaporised into a column that is hot at the bottom and cooler at the top. Fractions condense at different heights according to their boiling points."],
["What is cracking and what does it produce?","Breaking long-chain alkanes into shorter, more useful molecules. It gives shorter alkanes and alkenes."],
["Why is cracking needed?","Demand for short-chain alkanes and alkenes is greater than for long-chain alkanes."],
["What are the products of complete combustion of a hydrocarbon?","Carbon dioxide and water."],
["Write the equation for the complete combustion of ethane.","2C₂H₆ + 7O₂ → 4CO₂ + 6H₂O."],
["When does incomplete combustion happen, and how is it avoided?","When there is not enough oxygen. Use excess oxygen (air)."],
["Name the possible products of incomplete combustion.","Carbon monoxide, carbon (soot) and water (with some carbon dioxide)."],
["Write the equation for incomplete combustion of methane to carbon monoxide.","2CH₄ + 3O₂ → 2CO + 4H₂O."],
["Write the equation for incomplete combustion of methane to soot.","CH₄ + O₂ → C + 2H₂O."],
["What problems does incomplete combustion cause?","Toxic carbon monoxide. Soot (particulates) causes breathing problems and global dimming."],
["Why is carbon monoxide dangerous?","It is colourless, odourless and toxic. It binds to haemoglobin and stops blood carrying oxygen."],
["Why can burning fuels release sulfur dioxide?","Some fuels contain sulfur impurities, which burn to form SO₂."],
["How does acid rain form?","Sulfur dioxide (and nitrogen oxides) dissolve in water in clouds, forming acids."],
["What damage does acid rain cause?","Any two of: erodes limestone buildings and statues; damages trees and plants; acidifies lakes and kills aquatic life."],
["How are oxides of nitrogen formed in car engines?","The high temperature makes nitrogen and oxygen from the air react."],
["What problems do oxides of nitrogen cause?","Acid rain, breathing problems and photochemical smog."],
["Why should alternative fuels replace fossil fuels?","Any two of: fossil fuels are non-renewable; burning them releases CO₂ that causes climate change; they cause acid rain and smog."],
["What does carbon neutral mean?","No net carbon dioxide is added to the atmosphere. For example, crops for bioethanol absorb CO₂ as they grow."],
["Give advantages of hydrogen as a car fuel.","Any two of: high energy per kg; water is the only product; can be made from water, so it is renewable."],
["What is free-radical substitution?","A substitution reaction that goes through radicals, usually started by UV light causing homolytic fission."],
["Name and describe the three stages of free-radical substitution.","Initiation forms radicals. Propagation uses and remakes radicals. Termination removes radicals by joining two together."],
["Give the mechanism for ethane reacting with chlorine.","Initiation: Cl₂ → 2Cl•. Propagation: C₂H₆ + Cl• → C₂H₅• + HCl, then C₂H₅• + Cl₂ → C₂H₅Cl + Cl•. Termination: two radicals combine, e.g. C₂H₅• + Cl• → C₂H₅Cl."]],
"WCH11/alkenes":[
["What is an alkene?","An unsaturated hydrocarbon with a C=C double bond. General formula CₙH₂ₙ."],
["What does unsaturated mean?","The compound contains at least one C=C double bond."],
["Describe the structure of a C=C double bond.","One σ bond from end-on overlap of orbitals. One π bond from sideways overlap of p orbitals."],
["What is E–Z stereoisomerism and what causes it?","Same structural formula but different arrangement in space. It is caused by restricted rotation about the C=C bond."],
["How do you decide whether an isomer is E or Z?","E: the higher-priority groups are on opposite sides. Z: they are on the same side."],
["How do you decide which group has higher priority?","The atom attached to the C=C with the higher atomic number has priority. If they are the same, compare the next atoms along."],
["Give an example of E–Z isomers.","E- and Z-2-chloropent-2-ene, or E- and Z-but-2-ene."],
["What is cis–trans isomerism?","A type of E–Z isomerism where each carbon of the C=C carries the same group (usually H) and one different group."],
["How do cis and trans relate to E and Z?","Usually trans = E and cis = Z."],
["How is an alkene converted to an alkane?","Hydrogenation: H₂ with a nickel catalyst at about 150 °C."],
["How is an alkene converted to a dihalogenoalkane?","Add a halogen such as bromine."],
["How is an alkene converted to a halogenoalkane with one halogen?","Add a hydrogen halide such as HBr."],
["How is an alkene converted to a diol?","Oxidise with cold, dilute acidified potassium manganate(VII). The purple colour disappears."],
["What is the test for an alkene?","Shake with bromine water. Orange to colourless."],
["Describe the mechanism of bromine adding to ethene.","The C=C induces a dipole in Br₂. The π electrons attack the δ+ bromine, forming a carbocation and Br⁻. The Br⁻ then attacks the carbocation."],
["Describe the mechanism of HBr adding to ethene.","HBr is polar. The π electrons attack the δ+ hydrogen, forming a carbocation and Br⁻. The Br⁻ attacks the carbocation."],
["What is Markovnikov’s rule?","When HX adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, H adds to the carbon with more hydrogens. The halogen goes to the carbon with more alkyl groups, giving the major product."],
["What is the major product when HBr adds to propene, and why?","2-bromopropane. It forms from the secondary carbocation, which is more stable than the primary one."],
["What is addition polymerisation?","Alkene monomers join into a long chain as their π bonds break and new σ bonds form. The atom economy is 100%."],
["What is a repeat unit?","The smallest part of a polymer that repeats to make the chain."],
["How can polymer life cycles be made more sustainable?","Make biodegradable polymers. Recycle polymers, including back into monomers."],
["How can problems from polymer disposal be reduced?","Develop biodegradable polymers. Remove toxic gases such as HCl from incinerator fumes."]],
"WCH12/energetics":[
["What is the heat change at constant pressure called?","The enthalpy change, ΔH."],
["What are standard conditions?","100 kPa, 298 K, and solutions at 1 mol dm⁻³."],
["What is an exothermic reaction?","A reaction that releases energy to the surroundings."],
["What is an endothermic reaction?","A reaction that takes in energy from the surroundings."],
["What sign does ΔH have for exothermic and endothermic reactions?","Exothermic: negative. Endothermic: positive."],
["Define the standard enthalpy change of reaction.","The enthalpy change when the molar amounts in the equation react under standard conditions, with all substances in their standard states."],
["Define the standard enthalpy change of formation.","The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states, under standard conditions."],
["Define the standard enthalpy change of combustion.","The enthalpy change when one mole of a substance burns completely in excess oxygen under standard conditions."],
["Define the standard enthalpy change of neutralisation.","The enthalpy change when an acid and a base react to form one mole of water under standard conditions."],
["Define the standard enthalpy change of atomisation.","The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms forms from the element in its standard state, under standard conditions."],
["Which equation gives the energy transferred in a calorimetry experiment?","q = mcΔT. m is the mass of the water or solution in g, c in J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, ΔT the temperature change."],
["Why are calorimetry reactions done in insulated containers?","To reduce heat loss to (or gain from) the surroundings."],
["State Hess’s law.","The total enthalpy change for a reaction is the same whatever route is taken."],
["What is mean bond enthalpy?","The average energy needed to break one mole of a particular covalent bond in the gas phase, averaged over many compounds."],
["How does bond enthalpy relate to bond strength?","A higher bond enthalpy means a stronger bond that needs more energy to break. Weaker bonds break first."]],
"WCH12/intermolecular-forces":[
["Name the three types of intermolecular force.","London forces (temporary dipoles), permanent dipole–dipole forces and hydrogen bonds."],
["How do London forces arise?","Electrons move unevenly, creating an instantaneous dipole. This induces a dipole in a neighbouring molecule, and the two attract."],
["What is a permanent dipole–dipole force?","Attraction between the δ+ end of one polar molecule and the δ− end of another."],
["What is a hydrogen bond?","Attraction between a δ+ hydrogen bonded to N, O or F and a lone pair on an N, O or F atom of another molecule."],
["Why does water have unusually high melting and boiling points?","Water molecules form hydrogen bonds, the strongest intermolecular forces, so a lot of energy is needed to separate them."],
["Why is ice less dense than liquid water?","Hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open lattice, further apart than in the liquid."],
["Why do boiling points of alkanes increase with chain length?","Longer chains have more electrons and more surface contact, so London forces are stronger."],
["Why does branching lower the boiling point of an alkane?","Branched molecules have less surface contact, so London forces are weaker."],
["What is volatility?","How easily a substance evaporates."],
["Why are alcohols less volatile than alkanes of similar size?","Alcohols form hydrogen bonds between molecules, which are stronger than London forces."],
["Why do boiling points of the hydrogen halides increase from HCl to HI?","The molecules have more electrons, so London forces are stronger."],
["How is the enthalpy of hydration of an ion related to solubility?","A more exothermic hydration enthalpy generally means the compound is more soluble."],
["Why do short-chain alcohols dissolve in water?","They form hydrogen bonds with water molecules."],
["Why don’t non-polar molecules dissolve in water?","They can’t form hydrogen bonds with water, so it isn’t worth breaking water’s hydrogen bonds."],
["How can you predict whether a substance will dissolve in a solvent?","It dissolves if its intermolecular forces are similar to those in the solvent (“like dissolves like”)."]],
"WCH12/redox-chemistry-groups-1-2-7":[
["What are the usual oxidation numbers of oxygen and hydrogen in compounds?","Oxygen −2. Hydrogen +1."],
["Give the oxidation numbers of the metals in NaOH, MgO and Al₂O₃.","Na +1, Mg +2, Al +3."],
["What is the oxidation number of oxygen in H₂O₂?","−1."],
["What is the oxidation number of hydrogen in metal hydrides such as NaH?","−1."],
["What is the oxidation number of iron in iron(III) chloride?","+3."],
["Which neutral compound contains sulfur at +6 and oxygen at −2?","SO₃."],
["Define reduction.","Gain of electrons. The oxidation number decreases."],
["Define oxidation.","Loss of electrons. The oxidation number increases."],
["What is a reducing agent?","A species that donates electrons, reducing something else. It is itself oxidised."],
["What is an oxidising agent?","A species that accepts electrons, oxidising something else. It is itself reduced."],
["What is a redox reaction?","A reaction where oxidation and reduction happen at the same time."],
["How are half-equations combined?","Multiply so the numbers of electrons are equal, add them, and cancel the electrons."],
["Combine Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻ with Fe³⁺ + e⁻ → Fe²⁺.","Zn + 2Fe³⁺ → Zn²⁺ + 2Fe²⁺."],
["Why does first ionisation energy decrease down Groups 1 and 2?","Atomic radius and shielding increase, so the outer electrons are held less strongly."],
["Why does reactivity increase down Group 1?","The outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily."],
["Why does reactivity increase down Group 2?","The two outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded, so they are lost more easily."],
["What forms when lithium reacts with water?","Lithium hydroxide and hydrogen."],
["Write the equation for sodium reacting with water.","2Na + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + H₂."],
["What mainly forms when potassium burns in oxygen?","Potassium superoxide, KO₂."],
["Write the equation for lithium burning in oxygen.","4Li + O₂ → 2Li₂O."],
["What forms when a Group 1 metal reacts with chlorine?","A metal chloride, e.g. 2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl."],
["Write the general equation for a Group 2 metal X burning in oxygen.","2X + O₂ → 2XO."],
["Write the general equation for a Group 2 metal X reacting with water.","X + 2H₂O → X(OH)₂ + H₂."],
["Write the general equation for a Group 2 metal X reacting with chlorine.","X + Cl₂ → XCl₂."],
["What forms when a Group 1 or 2 oxide or hydroxide reacts with a dilute acid?","A salt and water."],
["Write the equation for sodium hydroxide and hydrochloric acid.","NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O."],
["Write the equation for magnesium hydroxide and sulfuric acid.","Mg(OH)₂ + H₂SO₄ → MgSO₄ + 2H₂O."],
["Write the equation for lithium oxide and sulfuric acid.","Li₂O + H₂SO₄ → Li₂SO₄ + H₂O."],
["Write the equation for barium oxide and hydrochloric acid.","BaO + 2HCl → BaCl₂ + H₂O."],
["What forms when Group 1 and 2 oxides react with water?","Metal hydroxides (alkaline solutions)."],
["Write the equation for calcium oxide and water.","CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂."],
["How does the solubility of Group 2 hydroxides change down the group?","It increases."],
["How does the solubility of Group 2 sulfates change down the group?","It decreases."],
["How does the thermal stability of Group 1 and 2 carbonates change down the group?","It increases."],
["How does the thermal stability of Group 1 and 2 nitrates change down the group?","It increases."],
["Why do carbonates and nitrates become more stable down the group?","The cations get larger with the same charge, so their charge density falls. They polarise the anion less, so more energy is needed to decompose it."],
["What flame colour does sodium give?","Yellow-orange."],
["What flame colour does lithium give?","Red."],
["What flame colour does potassium give?","Lilac."],
["What flame colour does rubidium give?","Red-violet."],
["What flame colour does caesium give?","Blue-violet."],
["What flame colour does calcium give?","Brick red (orange-red)."],
["What flame colour does barium give?","Pale green."],
["What flame colour does strontium give?","Red (crimson)."],
["Why do some metal ions give coloured flames?","Heat excites electrons to higher energy levels. When they fall back they emit light of particular wavelengths."],
["How do you test for carbonate and hydrogencarbonate ions?","Add dilute acid. Bubble the gas through limewater, which turns cloudy if CO₂ is present."],
["How do you test for sulfate ions?","Add dilute hydrochloric acid then barium chloride solution. A white precipitate of barium sulfate forms."],
["How do you test for ammonium ions?","Warm with sodium hydroxide solution. Ammonia is given off, turning damp red litmus blue."],
["What colour is aqueous chlorine?","Very pale green, almost colourless."],
["What colour is aqueous bromine?","Orange (yellow-orange)."],
["What colour is aqueous iodine?","Brown."],
["What are the states of chlorine, bromine and iodine at room temperature, and why?","Gas, liquid, solid. Melting and boiling points increase down the group."],
["Why do melting and boiling points increase down Group 7?","The molecules have more electrons, so London forces are stronger."],
["How does electronegativity change down Group 7 and why?","It decreases. The atoms are larger and more shielded, so they attract bonding electrons less."],
["Why does reactivity decrease down Group 7?","The outer shell is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is harder to gain an electron."],
["When does a halogen displacement reaction happen?","When a more reactive halogen is added to a solution of a less reactive halogen’s halide ions."],
["What type of reaction is halogen displacement?","Redox."],
["Why can halogen A only be displaced by a halogen above it in the group?","Halogens higher in the group are more reactive (stronger oxidising agents)."],
["Which halogens can chlorine displace from solution?","Bromine and iodine."],
["Which halogens can’t bromine displace?","Chlorine and fluorine."],
["Why can’t iodine displace chloride or bromide ions?","Iodine is less reactive than chlorine and bromine."],
["Give the word equation for chlorine and potassium bromide.","Chlorine + potassium bromide → potassium chloride + bromine."],
["Write the equation for bromine reacting with potassium iodide.","Br₂ + 2KI → 2KBr + I₂."],
["What do you see when chlorine is added to potassium bromide solution?","The colourless solution turns orange as bromine forms."],
["Write the equation for sodium reacting with chlorine.","2Na + Cl₂ → 2NaCl."],
["What is disproportionation?","The same element is both oxidised and reduced in one reaction."],
["Write the equation for chlorine reacting with cold sodium hydroxide.","Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O."],
["Write the equation for chlorine reacting with water.","Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HCl + HClO."],
["How do chloride and fluoride ions react with concentrated sulfuric acid?","They are not strong enough reducing agents to reduce it. Only steamy fumes of HCl or HF form, e.g. NaCl + H₂SO₄ → NaHSO₄ + HCl."],
["How do bromide ions react with concentrated sulfuric acid?","They reduce it to sulfur dioxide, forming bromine: 2HBr + H₂SO₄ → Br₂ + SO₂ + 2H₂O."],
["How do iodide ions react with concentrated sulfuric acid?","They are strong reducing agents and reduce it to SO₂, S and H₂S, forming iodine."],
["How do you test for halide ions?","Add dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate. Chloride: white precipitate, dissolves in dilute ammonia. Bromide: cream, dissolves in concentrated ammonia. Iodide: yellow, insoluble."],
["How do hydrogen halides react with water and with ammonia?","They donate protons. HX + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + X⁻. HX + NH₃ → NH₄X."]],
"WCH12/introduction-to-kinetics-and-equilibria":[
["Name factors that affect the rate of a reaction.","Any four of: temperature; pressure (gases); surface area; concentration; catalyst."],
["Why does raising the temperature increase the rate?","More particles have energy at or above the activation energy. Collisions also happen more often."],
["How does increasing surface area increase the rate?","More particles are exposed, so there are more frequent successful collisions."],
["How does a catalyst increase the rate?","It provides an alternative route with a lower activation energy, so more collisions are successful."],
["Why does increasing concentration increase the rate?","There are more particles in the same volume, so successful collisions are more frequent."],
["How does increasing pressure affect the rate of a gas reaction?","Particles are closer together, so successful collisions are more frequent."],
["Define activation energy.","The minimum energy colliding particles need to react."],
["How is the rate of reaction calculated?","Amount of reactant used or product formed ÷ time."],
["Give three units for rate.","Any three of: g s⁻¹; cm³ s⁻¹; mol s⁻¹; mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹."],
["How do you find the rate at a particular time from a graph?","Draw a tangent at that time and find its gradient."],
["What is a reversible reaction?","A reaction where the products can react to form the reactants again."],
["What is dynamic equilibrium?","In a closed system the forward and reverse rates are equal, so concentrations stay constant."],
["State Le Chatelier’s principle.","If a change is made to a system at equilibrium, the position of equilibrium moves to oppose the change."],
["What happens to the position of equilibrium when temperature is increased?","It moves in the endothermic direction."],
["What happens when temperature is decreased?","The position moves in the exothermic direction."],
["What happens when pressure is increased in a gas equilibrium?","It moves to the side with fewer moles of gas."],
["What happens when more reactant is added?","The position moves to the right, forming more product."],
["Why are 450 °C and 200 atm a compromise for the Haber process?","A lower temperature gives more ammonia but is too slow, so a moderate temperature is used. Higher pressure gives more yield and rate but is expensive and dangerous."]],
"WCH12/organic-chemistry-halogenoalkanes-alcohols-and-spectra":[
["What is a nucleophile?","A species that donates a pair of electrons to form a covalent bond."],
["What are halogenoalkanes and how are they named?","Alkanes with at least one halogen atom. The prefixes chloro-, bromo- and iodo- show the halogen."],
["How are primary, secondary and tertiary halogenoalkanes distinguished?","By the number of carbons bonded to the carbon carrying the halogen: one (primary), two (secondary), three (tertiary)."],
["What forms when a halogenoalkane is heated with aqueous KOH, and what is the nucleophile?","An alcohol. The nucleophile is OH⁻."],
["What forms when a halogenoalkane is heated with ethanolic KOH, and what type of reaction is it?","An alkene. Elimination."],
["What forms when a halogenoalkane is heated with ammonia in ethanol under pressure?","An amine. Ammonia acts as the nucleophile."],
["What forms when a halogenoalkane is warmed with KCN in ethanol?","A nitrile. The cyanide ion is the nucleophile."],
["How can silver nitrate be used to identify the halogen in a halogenoalkane?","Warm with ethanol and aqueous silver nitrate. A silver halide precipitate forms."],
["What precipitate forms with chloroethane and silver nitrate?","White silver chloride."],
["What precipitate forms with bromoethane and silver nitrate?","Cream silver bromide."],
["What precipitate forms with iodoethane and silver nitrate?","Yellow silver iodide."],
["What is the mechanism for a halogenoalkane reacting with ammonia?","Nucleophilic substitution."],
["To make an alcohol rather than an alkene from a halogenoalkane, what must the KOH be dissolved in?","Water (aqueous KOH). Ethanolic KOH gives an alkene."],
["Put primary, secondary and tertiary halogenoalkanes in order of reactivity.","Tertiary > secondary > primary."],
["Why does reactivity increase from chloro- to bromo- to iodoalkanes?","The C–I bond has the lowest bond enthalpy, so it breaks most easily. C–Cl is strongest."],
["What is the functional group and general formula of alcohols?","The –OH group. CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH."],
["How are alcohols classed as primary, secondary or tertiary?","By the number of carbons attached to the carbon carrying the –OH: one, two or three."],
["How are alcohols named?","With the suffix -ol (e.g. ethanol), or the prefix hydroxy- when another group takes the suffix."],
["Write the equation for complete combustion of ethanol.","C₂H₅OH + 3O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O."],
["What is needed for complete combustion?","Excess oxygen."],
["Write the equation for incomplete combustion of ethanol to carbon monoxide.","C₂H₅OH + 2O₂ → 2CO + 3H₂O."],
["Which reagent converts an alcohol into a chloroalkane?","Phosphorus(V) chloride, PCl₅."],
["Write the equation for ethanol and PCl₅.","C₂H₅OH + PCl₅ → C₂H₅Cl + POCl₃ + HCl."],
["How is PCl₅ used to test for an alcohol?","Add PCl₅ to the dry liquid. Steamy fumes of HCl show an –OH group."],
["How can a bromoalkane be made from an alcohol?","Heat the alcohol with potassium bromide and concentrated sulfuric acid, which make HBr."],
["Give the two equations for making bromoethane from ethanol.","KBr + H₂SO₄ → KHSO₄ + HBr. C₂H₅OH + HBr → C₂H₅Br + H₂O."],
["What reagents convert an alcohol into an iodoalkane?","Red phosphorus and iodine (which form PI₃)."],
["Give the equations for making iodoethane from ethanol.","2P + 3I₂ → 2PI₃. 3C₂H₅OH + PI₃ → 3C₂H₅I + H₃PO₃."],
["How is an alcohol converted into an alkene?","Elimination (dehydration) by heating with concentrated phosphoric(V) acid."],
["Which oxidising agent is used for alcohols and what is the colour change?","Acidified potassium dichromate(VI). Orange to green."],
["How is a primary alcohol oxidised to an aldehyde?","Heat with acidified dichromate and distil the aldehyde off as it forms."],
["How is a primary alcohol oxidised to a carboxylic acid?","Heat under reflux with excess acidified dichromate."],
["Which alcohols oxidise to ketones?","Secondary alcohols."],
["What apparatus is used for distillation?","Any three of: round-bottomed flask; heat source; thermometer at the side-arm; sloping condenser with water in at the bottom; collecting flask."],
["What apparatus is used for reflux?","Any three of: round-bottomed flask with anti-bumping granules; vertical condenser with water in at the bottom; heat source such as a heating mantle or water bath."],
["What technique identifies functional groups in organic compounds?","Infrared spectroscopy."],
["How does infrared spectroscopy identify functional groups?","Bonds absorb infrared at characteristic frequencies as they vibrate. The absorption peaks identify the bonds present."]],
"WCH14/kinetics":[
["What is the rate of reaction?","The change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time."],
["Write the general rate equation.","Rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ, where m and n are the orders."],
["What is k in a rate equation?","The rate constant."],
["How do you work out the units of k?","Rearrange the rate equation for k, substitute the units and cancel."],
["Define the order with respect to a reactant and the overall order.","The order is the power of that reactant’s concentration in the rate equation. The overall order is the sum of the orders."],
["How does doubling a reactant’s concentration change the rate for zero, first and second order?","Zero: no change. First: doubles. Second: quadruples."],
["What is activation energy?","The minimum energy particles need to react."],
["What is a homogeneous catalyst?","A catalyst in the same state (phase) as the reactants."],
["What is a heterogeneous catalyst?","A catalyst in a different state from the reactants."],
["What is half-life?","The time for the concentration of a reactant to fall to half its value."],
["How do you find half-life from a concentration–time graph?","Read the time for the concentration to halve, and to halve again. They should be equal for a first-order reaction."],
["A reactant has a constant half-life. What is its order?","First order."],
["Give three ways of following the rate of a reaction.","Any three of: titration of samples; measuring gas volume; mass loss; colorimetry; a clock reaction (timing a colour change); pH or conductivity."],
["Describe the two main approaches to measuring rate.","Initial rates: repeat with different starting concentrations and measure the rate at the start. Continuous monitoring: follow concentration over time and plot a graph."],
["Which equation links k and half-life, and when does it apply?","k = ln 2 ÷ t½. Only for first-order reactions."],
["How do you find the rate at a given time from a concentration–time graph?","Draw a tangent at that time and find its gradient."],
["How do you find k from a rate–concentration graph for a first-order reactant?","The gradient of the straight line equals k."],
["How do you calculate a gradient?","Change in y ÷ change in x."],
["What is the rate-determining step?","The slowest step in a mechanism. Only species in it, or before it, affect the rate."],
["How are the orders in the rate equation linked to the rate-determining step?","Species in the rate equation take part in the rate-determining step. The order shows how many of each are involved."],
["What happens when iodine reacts with propanone in acid, and what is the rate equation?","CH₃COCH₃ + I₂ → CH₃COCH₂I + HI, and the brown colour fades. Rate = k[CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]: zero order in iodine."],
["Give the rate equations for hydrolysis of primary and tertiary halogenoalkanes.","Primary: rate = k[RX][OH⁻] (SN2). Tertiary: rate = k[RX] (SN1)."],
["Describe the SN2 mechanism for hydrolysis of bromoethane.","One step: OH⁻ attacks the δ+ carbon as the C–Br bond breaks, through a transition state. Both species are in the slow step."],
["Describe the SN1 mechanism for hydrolysis of 2-bromo-2-methylpropane.","Slow step: (CH₃)₃CBr → (CH₃)₃C⁺ + Br⁻. Fast step: the carbocation reacts with OH⁻ to give (CH₃)₃COH."],
["How do SN1 and SN2 differ in their first step?","SN1: only the halogenoalkane is in the slow step. SN2: the halogenoalkane and the hydroxide ion are both in it."],
["What happens in the slow step of SN1?","The halogenoalkane ionises to form a carbocation, which then reacts quickly with OH⁻."],
["Which equation is used to find activation energy from rate constants at different temperatures?","The Arrhenius equation, k = Ae^(−Ea/RT), using a graph of ln k against 1/T."],
["How do solid heterogeneous catalysts work in gas reactions?","They provide a surface where reactants adsorb, react and then desorb."]],
"WCH14/entropy-and-energetics":[
["Which two entropy changes decide whether a reaction is feasible?","The entropy change of the system and the entropy change of the surroundings."],
["Why can’t enthalpy change alone decide whether a reaction happens?","Some endothermic reactions happen spontaneously at room temperature."],
["What is entropy?","A measure of disorder: the number of ways particles and their energy can be arranged."],
["How does entropy change from solid to liquid to gas?","It increases, as particles become more disordered and free to move."],
["At what temperature does a perfect crystal have zero entropy?","0 K."],
["Give three ways to increase the entropy of a substance.","Raise its temperature. Change it from solid to liquid or liquid to gas. Give a gas more volume."],
["What sign is the entropy change for spontaneous processes such as a gas spreading?","Positive."],
["Why does entropy increase on melting or boiling?","Particles move more freely and randomly, so disorder increases."],
["Why does entropy usually increase when an ionic solid dissolves?","Ions fixed in a lattice become free to move in solution."],
["How is total entropy change calculated?","ΔStotal = ΔSsystem + ΔSsurroundings."],
["How is the entropy change of the system calculated?","ΣS(products) − ΣS(reactants)."],
["How is the entropy change of the surroundings calculated?","ΔSsurroundings = −ΔH ÷ T, with ΔH in J mol⁻¹ and T in K."],
["What decides whether a reaction is feasible?","The total entropy change must be positive. One term can be negative if the other outweighs it, and temperature changes the surroundings term."],
["What does the sign of the total entropy change say about stability?","Positive: products are thermodynamically more stable. Negative: reactants are more stable, so the reaction does not go."],
["Why might a thermodynamically feasible reaction not happen?","The activation energy is too high, so it is too slow (kinetic stability)."],
["What is electron affinity?","The enthalpy change when each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms gains an electron to form gaseous 1− ions."],
["Define lattice energy.","The enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid forms from its gaseous ions (exothermic)."],
["Define enthalpy change of atomisation.","The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous atoms forms from an element in its standard state."],
["Which cycle is used to find lattice energy from experimental data?","A Born–Haber cycle."],
["What does it mean if the experimental lattice energy is more exothermic than the theoretical value?","The compound has some covalent character."],
["Why does a small cation distort a large anion?","It strongly attracts the anion’s outer electrons (polarisation), giving covalent character."],
["Define enthalpy change of hydration.","The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous ions becomes hydrated (aqueous) ions."],
["Define enthalpy change of solution.","The enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid dissolves in water to form a very dilute solution."],
["What makes hydration enthalpy more exothermic?","Smaller ions and higher charge, which attract water molecules more strongly."],
["What makes lattice energy more exothermic?","Smaller ions and higher charges, which attract each other more strongly."]],
"WCH14/chemical-equilibria":[
["What is a reversible reaction?","A reaction where the products can react to form the reactants again."],
["What does Kc tell you?","It is the equilibrium constant. Its size shows whether products or reactants are favoured at equilibrium."],
["Write Kc for aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD.","Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ ÷ [A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ."],
["Write Kc for 3H₂ + N₂ ⇌ 2NH₃.","Kc = [NH₃]² ÷ [H₂]³[N₂]."],
["In a heterogeneous equilibrium, which species are left out of Kc and why?","Solids (and pure liquids), because their concentrations are constant."],
["Write Kc for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g).","Kc = [CO₂]."],
["How do you find the units of Kc?","Substitute concentration units into the expression and cancel."],
["What is Kp and when is it used?","The equilibrium constant in terms of partial pressures, used for gas equilibria."],
["How do you calculate mole fraction?","Moles of that gas ÷ total moles of gas."],
["How do you calculate partial pressure?","Mole fraction × total pressure."],
["Write Kp for H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI.","Kp = p(HI)² ÷ (p(H₂) × p(I₂))."],
["What are the units of Kp for H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI?","None: the units cancel."],
["Write Kp for CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g).","Kp = p(CO₂)."],
["Does changing concentration change Kc or Kp?","No. Only temperature changes them."],
["Does changing pressure change Kc or Kp?","No. The position may move, but the constant stays the same."],
["Why doesn’t a catalyst change K?","It speeds up forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster but its position is unchanged."],
["For an endothermic forward reaction, which way does equilibrium move when temperature rises?","To the right (forwards)."],
["How does increasing pressure affect N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃?","Equilibrium moves right, to fewer moles of gas, giving more ammonia."],
["How does lowering pressure affect H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI?","No effect: there are equal moles of gas on each side."],
["For an exothermic forward reaction, how does raising temperature change Kc?","Kc decreases, because the equilibrium moves left."],
["For an endothermic forward reaction, how does raising temperature change Kp?","Kp increases, because the equilibrium moves right."],
["How does K change as total entropy change increases?","K increases."],
["How are K and total entropy change related?","ΔStotal = R ln K."],
["How does temperature affect total entropy change?","It changes the surroundings term, −ΔH/T, which gets smaller as temperature rises."]],
"WCH14/acid-base-equilibria":[
["What is a Brønsted–Lowry acid?","A proton (H⁺) donor."],
["What is a Brønsted–Lowry base?","A proton (H⁺) acceptor."],
["Which particle is transferred in an acid–base reaction?","A proton, H⁺."],
["In HNO₂ + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + NO₂⁻, which species donates the proton and which accepts it?","HNO₂ donates the proton. H₂O accepts it."],
["What is a conjugate acid–base pair?","Two species that differ by one proton and turn into each other by gaining or losing H⁺."],
["What does pH measure, and what is the pH of a neutral solution?","How acidic or alkaline a solution is, based on [H⁺]. Neutral is pH 7 at 298 K."],
["How do you find the pH of a strong monoprotic acid?","[H⁺] equals the acid concentration. pH = −log[H⁺]."],
["How do you find [H⁺] from pH?","[H⁺] = 10^(−pH)."],
["What happens to a strong acid in water?","It ionises completely, e.g. HCl → H⁺ + Cl⁻."],
["What happens to a strong base in water?","It dissociates completely to release OH⁻, e.g. NaOH → Na⁺ + OH⁻."],
["How does a weak acid behave in water?","It only partly ionises, setting up an equilibrium, e.g. CH₃COOH ⇌ CH₃COO⁻ + H⁺."],
["How does a weak base behave in water?","It only partly reacts with water to form OH⁻ in an equilibrium, e.g. NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻."],
["What does Ka tell you, and what is its expression?","It measures the strength of an acid. Ka = [H⁺][A⁻] ÷ [HA]."],
["How do you calculate the pH of a weak acid from Ka and concentration?","Assume [H⁺] = [A⁻], so [H⁺] = √(Ka × [HA]). Then pH = −log[H⁺]."],
["What assumption is made about [HA] when finding the pH of a weak acid?","Its equilibrium concentration is taken as equal to its starting concentration, because so little dissociates."],
["What is pKa and why is it used?","pKa = −log Ka. It gives simpler numbers for comparing acid strength."],
["What is Kw and its value at 298 K?","Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶. Water is left out because its concentration is effectively constant."],
["What is pKw and its value at 298 K?","pKw = −log Kw. It equals 14 at 298 K."],
["How do you find the pH of a strong base?","[OH⁻] equals the base concentration, so [H⁺] = Kw ÷ [OH⁻]. Then pH = −log[H⁺]."],
["How do you produce a pH curve experimentally?","Pipette acid into a flask and measure its pH. Add base from a burette in small portions, recording the pH each time until it levels off. Plot pH against volume of base."],
["How does a pH curve help you choose an indicator?","The indicator’s colour-change range must lie within the steep vertical part of the curve. Weak acid–weak base titrations have no steep part, so no indicator works."],
["What colours is methyl orange in acid and alkali?","Red in acid, yellow in alkali."],
["What does blue litmus do in acid and alkali?","Turns red in acid; stays blue in alkali."],
["What does red litmus do in acid and alkali?","Stays red in acid; turns blue in alkali."],
["What colours is phenolphthalein in acid and alkali?","Colourless in acid, pink in alkali."],
["What is a buffer solution?","A solution that resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added."],
["How does an ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer respond to added acid?","The extra H⁺ combines with CH₃COO⁻ to form CH₃COOH. The equilibrium shifts left, so pH barely falls."],
["How does an ethanoic acid/ethanoate buffer respond to added alkali?","OH⁻ removes H⁺ as water. More CH₃COOH dissociates, shifting the equilibrium right, so pH barely rises."],
["How do you calculate the pH of a buffer made from a weak acid and its salt?","Rearrange Ka for [H⁺]: [H⁺] = Ka × [HA] ÷ [A⁻]. Then pH = −log[H⁺]."],
["How can the half-neutralisation point be used to find Ka?","At half-neutralisation [HA] = [A⁻], so Ka = [H⁺] and pKa = pH. Convert the measured pH to [H⁺]."],
["Why are buffers important in blood and foods?","Any 2 of: the carbonic acid/hydrogencarbonate buffer keeps blood pH steady; this protects proteins such as enzymes; buffers in food stop pH changes that cause spoilage."]],
"WCH14/organic-chemistry-carbonyls-carboxylic-acids-and-chirality":[
["What are optical isomers (enantiomers)?","Stereoisomers that are non-superimposable mirror images of each other."],
["What is a chiral centre?","A carbon atom bonded to four different groups."],
["What is optical activity?","The ability to rotate the plane of plane-polarised light."],
["How can the two enantiomers be told apart?","They rotate plane-polarised light by the same amount in opposite directions."],
["What is a racemic mixture?","A mixture with equal amounts of both enantiomers."],
["Why does a racemic mixture have no effect on polarised light?","The two enantiomers rotate light equally in opposite directions, so the effects cancel."],
["What is an aldehyde? Give an example.","A carbonyl compound with the C=O at the end of the chain (–CHO). Names end in -al, e.g. ethanal."],
["What is a ketone? Give an example.","A carbonyl compound with the C=O between two carbon groups. Names end in -one, e.g. propanone."],
["Why can’t aldehydes and ketones hydrogen bond to each other?","They have no hydrogen bonded to an oxygen atom."],
["Why do aldehydes and ketones have fairly low boiling points?","There is no hydrogen bonding between their molecules. Only London forces and permanent dipole forces act."],
["Why do small aldehydes and ketones dissolve in water?","Water’s δ+ hydrogens form hydrogen bonds with the lone pairs on the carbonyl oxygen."],
["How does Fehling’s (or Benedict’s) solution distinguish aldehydes from ketones?","Warmed with an aldehyde, the blue solution gives a brick-red precipitate. A ketone leaves it blue."],
["What does Tollens’ reagent show with aldehydes and ketones?","An aldehyde gives a silver mirror and is oxidised to a carboxylic acid. A ketone gives no change."],
["What happens when aldehydes and ketones are refluxed with acidified dichromate(VI)?","Aldehydes are oxidised to carboxylic acids and the solution turns orange to green. Ketones are not oxidised, so it stays orange."],
["What does LiAlH₄ reduce an aldehyde to?","A primary alcohol: CH₃CHO + 2[H] → CH₃CH₂OH."],
["What does LiAlH₄ reduce a ketone to?","A secondary alcohol: CH₃COCH₃ + 2[H] → CH₃CH(OH)CH₃."],
["Which reagent tests for a C=O group, and what is the positive result?","2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNPH). A yellow, orange or red precipitate forms."],
["How does HCN react with aldehydes and ketones?","Nucleophilic addition across the C=O. A hydroxynitrile forms, e.g. CH₃CHO + HCN → CH₃CH(OH)CN."],
["What is the iodoform test and its positive result?","Warm with iodine and sodium hydroxide. A pale yellow precipitate of CHI₃ shows a CH₃CO– group (or CH₃CH(OH)–)."],
["What is a carboxylic acid and why is it weak?","An organic acid containing –COOH. It only partly dissociates in water."],
["Name three carboxylic acids.","Any 3 of: methanoic acid; ethanoic acid; propanoic acid; butanoic acid; benzoic acid."],
["Describe the boiling points and solubility of carboxylic acids.","Any 2 of: high boiling points due to hydrogen bonding between molecules; small ones dissolve in water by hydrogen bonding; solubility falls as the chain gets longer."],
["Give two ways to make carboxylic acids.","Any 2 of: oxidising primary alcohols; oxidising aldehydes; hydrolysing nitriles with acid."],
["How is a carboxylic acid made from a primary alcohol?","Reflux with excess acidified dichromate(VI); orange to green. CH₃CH₂OH + 2[O] → CH₃COOH + H₂O."],
["How is a carboxylic acid made from an aldehyde?","Reflux with acidified dichromate(VI); orange to green. CH₃CHO + [O] → CH₃COOH."],
["How is a carboxylic acid made from a nitrile?","Reflux with dilute hydrochloric acid. CH₃CN + 2H₂O + HCl → CH₃COOH + NH₄Cl."],
["What does LiAlH₄ reduce a carboxylic acid to?","A primary alcohol: CH₃COOH + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂OH + H₂O."],
["What forms when a carboxylic acid reacts with sodium?","A salt and hydrogen gas: 2CH₃COOH + 2Na → 2CH₃COONa + H₂."],
["What forms when a carboxylic acid reacts with an alkali?","A salt and water: CH₃COOH + NaOH → CH₃COONa + H₂O."],
["What forms when a carboxylic acid reacts with a carbonate?","A salt, water and carbon dioxide (fizzing): 2CH₃COOH + Na₂CO₃ → 2CH₃COONa + H₂O + CO₂."],
["What forms when a carboxylic acid reacts with PCl₅?","An acyl chloride, POCl₃ and HCl: CH₃COOH + PCl₅ → CH₃COCl + POCl₃ + HCl."],
["How are esters made from carboxylic acids?","Warm the acid with an alcohol and a concentrated sulfuric acid catalyst. CH₃COOH + CH₃OH ⇌ CH₃COOCH₃ + H₂O."],
["What is an ester?","A compound with the –COO– group, where an alkyl group replaces the acid’s H. Usually made from an alcohol and a carboxylic acid, e.g. methyl ethanoate."],
["Describe acid hydrolysis of an ester.","Reflux with dilute acid to give a carboxylic acid and an alcohol. It is slow and reversible."],
["Describe alkaline hydrolysis of an ester.","Reflux with dilute alkali to give a carboxylate salt and an alcohol. It goes to completion."],
["How do polyesters form?","By condensation polymerisation, forming ester links and releasing water. Monomers are a diol and a dicarboxylic acid, or one molecule with both –OH and –COOH."],
["What is an acyl chloride? Give an example.","A carboxylic acid derivative where –OH is replaced by –Cl, e.g. ethanoyl chloride, CH₃COCl."],
["What forms when an acyl chloride reacts with water?","A carboxylic acid and HCl: CH₃COCl + H₂O → CH₃COOH + HCl."],
["What forms when an acyl chloride reacts with an alcohol?","An ester and HCl: CH₃COCl + CH₃OH → CH₃COOCH₃ + HCl."],
["What forms when an acyl chloride reacts with concentrated ammonia?","A primary amide and HCl: CH₃COCl + NH₃ → CH₃CONH₂ + HCl."],
["What forms when an acyl chloride reacts with a primary amine?","An N-substituted (secondary) amide and HCl: CH₃COCl + CH₃NH₂ → CH₃CONHCH₃ + HCl."],
["Why are polyesters useful?","They are used for plastic bottles and clothing fibres."],
["Which techniques are combined to find an organic structure?","Any 3 of: mass spectrometry gives the molar mass; infrared identifies functional groups; ¹³C NMR shows carbon environments; ¹H NMR shows hydrogen environments."],
["What happens when a molecule absorbs infrared radiation?","Bonds absorb IR at their own natural frequency. This makes them vibrate more, by stretching or bending."],
["How is IR used to identify functional groups?","Each bond absorbs at a characteristic wavenumber (cm⁻¹). For example, 1000–1300 cm⁻¹ suggests C–O."],
["What does a ¹H NMR spectrum tell you?","Any 4 of: chemical shift shows the type of proton environment; number of peaks shows the number of environments; relative peak areas show the ratio of protons; splitting shows the number of neighbouring protons."],
["How is NMR used to identify an unknown molecule?","It shows which fragments are present. These are pieced together to deduce the structure."],
["What is the n+1 rule?","A peak splits into n+1 lines, where n is the number of H atoms on neighbouring carbons."],
["What does a ¹³C NMR spectrum tell you?","Any 2 of: the number of peaks equals the number of carbon environments; chemical shift shows the type of carbon environment."],
["Name the first four ¹H NMR splitting patterns.","Any 4 of: singlet, no neighbouring H; doublet, one neighbouring H; triplet, two neighbouring H; quartet, three neighbouring H."],
["What are the height ratios in a doublet, triplet and quartet?","Any 3 of: doublet 1:1; triplet 1:2:1; quartet 1:3:3:1."],
["What is chromatography?","A way of separating a mixture using a mobile phase and a stationary phase."],
["Why does chromatography separate a mixture?","Components have different affinities for the mobile and stationary phases. So they move at different speeds."],
["What is an Rf value?","The distance moved by a substance divided by the distance moved by the solvent front."],
["How can chromatography show a known substance is in a mixture?","Run a pure sample alongside the mixture. A spot with the same Rf suggests it is present."],
["Write the formula for Rf.","Rf = distance moved by substance ÷ distance moved by solvent front."],
["What is HPLC?","High-performance liquid chromatography, with liquid pumped at high pressure through a packed column. Substances are identified by retention time."],
["What does gas chromatography separate?","Volatile substances, which are vaporised and carried through the column as gases."],
["How is gas chromatography carried out?","Any 4 of: the sample is injected and vaporised; an inert carrier gas moves it through the column; the column holds a high-boiling liquid on a solid support; substances move at different rates depending on solubility and volatility; a detector produces a chromatogram with a peak for each substance."],
["What is retention time?","The time from injection until a substance reaches the detector."],
["What is retention time used for?","Comparing it with known values to identify a substance."]],
"WCH15/redox-equilibria":[
["What is an oxidation number?","A number showing how many electrons an atom has lost or gained compared with the element."],
["What happens in oxidation?","Electrons are lost. The oxidation number increases."],
["What happens in reduction?","Electrons are gained. The oxidation number decreases."],
["What is the standard electrode potential of a half-cell?","The voltage of that half-cell measured against a standard hydrogen electrode under standard conditions."],
["What are the standard conditions for electrode potentials?","Any 3 of: 298 K; 100 kPa pressure for gases; 1.00 mol dm⁻³ for ions in solution."],
["Why is the standard hydrogen electrode used?","It is a common reference so electrodes can be compared. Its potential is defined as 0 V."],
["How is the standard electrode potential of zinc measured?","Connect a Zn/Zn²⁺ half-cell to a standard hydrogen electrode with a salt bridge and a high-resistance voltmeter."],
["Why are metal electrodes cleaned with sandpaper first?","To remove the oxide layer and give good electrical contact."],
["Which way do electrons flow in the external circuit of a cell?","From the negative electrode to the positive electrode."],
["What does a salt bridge do?","It completes the circuit by letting ions move. It keeps each solution electrically neutral."],
["Why must the salt in a salt bridge be unreactive?","A reactive salt could react with the solutions, changing concentrations and the cell potential."],
["What moves through a salt bridge?","Ions."],
["What sign must E°cell have for a reaction to be feasible?","Positive."],
["How is E°cell calculated?","E°cell = E°(positive electrode) − E°(negative electrode)."],
["Why may measured cell potentials differ from calculated ones?","The conditions may not be standard."],
["For Ag⁺/Ag (+0.80 V) and Cu²⁺/Cu (+0.34 V), give the overall reaction and E°cell.","2Ag⁺ + Cu → 2Ag + Cu²⁺. E°cell = 0.80 − 0.34 = +0.46 V."],
["Which half-cell is oxidised in an electrochemical cell?","The one with the more negative electrode potential."],
["How is E°cell related to the equilibrium constant?","E°cell is proportional to ln K."],
["How is E°cell related to the total entropy change?","E°cell is proportional to ΔStotal, because both are proportional to ln K."],
["What is uncertainty in a measurement?","The range within which the true value is expected to lie."],
["What is a fuel cell?","A cell that produces a voltage continuously while fuel and oxygen are supplied."],
["What is the only product of a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell?","Water."],
["How does a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell work?","Hydrogen and oxygen are fed to porous electrodes separated by an electrolyte. They react to produce electricity and water."],
["Give the electrode half-equations for an alkaline hydrogen fuel cell.","Negative: H₂ + 2OH⁻ → 2H₂O + 2e⁻. Positive: O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻."],
["What is the overall equation in a hydrogen fuel cell?","2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O."],
["Give two advantages of fuel cells.","Any 2 of: only water is produced at the point of use; they are more efficient than petrol engines; they run continuously while fuel is supplied."],
["Give two disadvantages of fuel cells.","Any 2 of: materials such as catalysts are expensive; hydrogen is hard and costly to store; high-pressure tanks are needed; hydrogen is often made using fossil fuels."],
["Name a fuel other than hydrogen for a fuel cell.","Methanol (or another hydrogen-rich fuel such as ethanol)."]],
"WCH15/transition-metals-and-their-chemistry":[
["What is a transition metal?","A d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with a partly filled d-subshell."],
["Which two d-block elements are not transition metals, and why?","Scandium and zinc. Their only ions (Sc³⁺, Zn²⁺) have empty or full d-subshells."],
["Which two elements have unusual electron configurations, and what are they?","Chromium [Ar]3d⁵4s¹ and copper [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹. A half-full or full d-subshell is more stable."],
["Give three typical properties of transition metals.","Any 3 of: variable oxidation states; form complex ions; form coloured ions; act as catalysts."],
["Give two pairs of ions showing variable oxidation states.","Any 2 of: Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺; Cu⁺ and Cu²⁺; Cr²⁺ and Cr³⁺; V²⁺ and V³⁺."],
["What is a ligand?","A molecule or ion with a lone pair that forms a dative bond to a central metal ion."],
["What bond joins a ligand to the metal ion?","A dative (coordinate) covalent bond. Both electrons come from the ligand."],
["What is a complex ion?","A central metal ion surrounded by ligands bonded by dative bonds."],
["What colour are most aqueous transition metal ions?","Coloured."],
["What is the coordination number of a complex?","The number of dative bonds to the central metal ion."],
["Why are transition metal complexes coloured?","Ligands split the d orbitals, and electrons absorb visible light to jump to the higher level. The remaining colours are transmitted or reflected, and that is what we see."],
["Why are some transition metal ions colourless?","They have no d electrons or a full d-subshell. So no d–d transitions can happen."],
["Give three things that change the colour of a complex.","Any 3 of: the oxidation state of the metal; the type of ligand; the coordination number."],
["What is a monodentate ligand? Give examples.","A ligand forming one dative bond, e.g. H₂O, NH₃, Cl⁻, OH⁻."],
["What is a bidentate ligand? Give an example.","A ligand forming two dative bonds, e.g. ethane-1,2-diamine, NH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂."],
["What is a hexadentate ligand? Give an example.","A ligand forming six dative bonds, e.g. EDTA⁴⁻."],
["Explain ligand exchange in haemoglobin.","Haemoglobin contains an iron(II) complex that binds oxygen. Carbon monoxide replaces the oxygen and binds more strongly, so less oxygen is carried."],
["Give the colours of vanadium in oxidation states +5 to +2.","Any 4 of: +5 yellow; +4 blue; +3 green; +2 violet."],
["How is dichromate(VI) reduced to Cr³⁺ and then Cr²⁺?","With zinc and acid."],
["How is Cr³⁺ oxidised to chromium(VI)?","Warm with hydrogen peroxide in alkali, giving yellow chromate(VI)."],
["Write the equilibrium between dichromate(VI) and chromate(VI).","Cr₂O₇²⁻ + H₂O ⇌ 2CrO₄²⁻ + 2H⁺. Adding alkali gives yellow chromate; adding acid gives orange dichromate."],
["What are the shape and bond angle of [CuCl₂]⁻?","Linear, 180°."],
["What are the shape and bond angle of [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂]?","Square planar, 90°."],
["What are the shape and bond angle of [CuCl₄]²⁻?","Tetrahedral, 109.5°."],
["What are the shape and bond angle of [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺?","Octahedral, 90°."],
["What are the shape and bond angle of [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺?","Octahedral, 90°."],
["What is cisplatin used for?","It is a square planar platinum complex used as an anti-cancer drug."],
["Write the equation for Cr(III) with excess ammonia.","[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 6NH₃ → [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺ + 6H₂O. The grey-green precipitate dissolves to a purple solution."],
["What happens when Cr(III) reacts with excess NaOH?","A grey-green precipitate of Cr(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ forms first. It dissolves in excess to give green [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻."],
["Write the equation for Mn(II) with ammonia.","[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2NH₃ → Mn(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ + 2NH₄⁺. A pale brown precipitate forms."],
["Write the equation for Mn(II) with NaOH.","[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Mn(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ + 2H₂O. A pale brown precipitate forms."],
["Write the equation for Fe(II) with NaOH.","[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Fe(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ + 2H₂O. A green precipitate forms."],
["Write the equation for Fe(II) with ammonia.","[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2NH₃ → Fe(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ + 2NH₄⁺. A green precipitate forms."],
["Write the equation for Fe(III) with ammonia.","[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3NH₃ → Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + 3NH₄⁺. An orange-brown precipitate forms."],
["Write the equation for Fe(III) with NaOH.","[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3OH⁻ → Fe(H₂O)₃(OH)₃ + 3H₂O. An orange-brown precipitate forms."],
["What happens when Ni(II) reacts with excess ammonia?","A green precipitate of Ni(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ forms first. It dissolves in excess to give blue [Ni(NH₃)₆]²⁺."],
["Write the equation for Ni(II) with NaOH.","[Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Ni(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ + 2H₂O. A green precipitate forms."],
["What happens when Cu(II) reacts with excess ammonia?","A pale blue precipitate of Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ forms first. It dissolves in excess to give deep blue [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺."],
["Write the equation for Cu(II) with NaOH.","[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ → Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂ + 2H₂O. A pale blue precipitate forms."],
["What does amphoteric mean?","Able to react as both an acid and a base."],
["What is ligand exchange? Give a copper example.","One ligand is replaced by another. [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 4H₂O."],
["What is seen when copper(II) ions react with ammonia, first a little then excess?","A little ammonia gives a pale blue precipitate. Excess gives a deep blue solution."],
["What happens when concentrated chloride ions are added to [Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺?","[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ → [CuCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O. The blue solution turns yellow-green."],
["What happens when concentrated chloride ions are added to [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺?","[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ + 4Cl⁻ ⇌ [CoCl₄]²⁻ + 6H₂O. The pink solution turns blue."],
["Why are complexes with bidentate or hexadentate ligands more stable?","Replacing monodentate ligands releases more particles, so ΔSsystem is positive. This makes the new complex more stable (the chelate effect)."],
["How do homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts differ?","Homogeneous: same phase as reactants, working through an intermediate. Heterogeneous: different phase, with reaction on its surface."],
["Why are transition metals good homogeneous catalysts?","Their variable oxidation states let them gain and lose electrons, oxidising and reducing reactants."],
["Why are transition metals good heterogeneous catalysts?","Their 3d and 4s electrons form weak bonds to reactants on the surface. This weakens bonds in the reactants and lowers activation energy."],
["How does a catalytic converter remove CO and NO?","The gases adsorb onto the metal surface and their bonds weaken. CO₂ and N₂ form and desorb."],
["What is the Contact Process?","The industrial manufacture of sulfuric acid. SO₂ is oxidised to SO₃ over a catalyst, then absorbed into concentrated sulfuric acid."],
["What are the conditions for the Contact Process?","Any 3 of: about 450 °C; vanadium(V) oxide catalyst; about 2 atm pressure."],
["How does V₂O₅ catalyse the oxidation of SO₂?","SO₂ + V₂O₅ → SO₃ + V₂O₄. V₂O₄ + ½O₂ → V₂O₅."],
["How do iron ions catalyse the reaction between peroxodisulfate and iodide?","S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺. 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂."]],
"WCH15/organic-chemistry-arenes":[
["How are the electrons in benzene’s ring arranged?","Each carbon’s p orbital overlaps sideways with those on both neighbours. This forms a ring of π electron density above and below the plane. The electrons are delocalised around the whole ring."],
["What enthalpy evidence supports the delocalised model of benzene?","The Kekulé model predicts an enthalpy of hydrogenation three times that of cyclohexene. The real value is less exothermic. So benzene is more stable than Kekulé suggests, due to delocalisation."],
["What X-ray diffraction evidence goes against the Kekulé structure?","Kekulé predicts two bond lengths, single and double. All the C–C bonds in benzene are the same length, between single and double."],
["Why is benzene much less reactive with bromine than alkenes are?","Its delocalised electrons are spread out, so electron density is lower than in a C=C bond. It cannot polarise a bromine molecule enough to react without a catalyst."],
["Describe the shape of benzene.","A flat (planar) regular hexagon. Each carbon is trigonal planar with 120° bond angles."],
["Write the equation for complete combustion of benzene and describe the flame.","2C₆H₆ + 15O₂ → 12CO₂ + 6H₂O. It burns with a very smoky flame."],
["What catalyst is needed for benzene to react with bromine?","A halogen carrier such as FeBr₃ or AlCl₃ (or iron filings)."],
["How is benzene sulfonated, and what is the electrophile?","Warm with fuming sulfuric acid. SO₃ acts as the electrophile."],
["What are the conditions for hydrogenating benzene?","Hydrogen with a nickel catalyst at about 150 °C."],
["What is needed for Friedel–Crafts acylation of benzene?","An acyl chloride with an AlCl₃ catalyst."],
["Why does phenol react with bromine water more easily than benzene?","A lone pair on the oxygen is partly delocalised into the ring. This increases electron density, so the ring polarises bromine more easily."]],
"WCH15/organic-nitrogen-compounds":[
["What is an amide and its general formula?","A carboxylic acid derivative where –OH is replaced by –NH₂. RCONH₂."],
["What is an amine and its general formula?","Ammonia with one or more hydrogens replaced by alkyl or aryl groups. RNH₂ for a primary amine."],
["What groups does an amino acid contain, and what is its general formula?","An amine group and a carboxylic acid group. H₂NCH(R)COOH."],
["Why does butylamine form an alkaline solution? Give the equation.","The lone pair on nitrogen accepts a proton from water, leaving OH⁻. C₄H₉NH₂ + H₂O ⇌ C₄H₉NH₃⁺ + OH⁻."],
["What forms when an amine reacts with an acid?","The amine acts as a base and forms a salt. CH₃CH₂NH₂ + HCl → CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻."],
["Write the equation for phenylamine reacting with sulfuric acid.","C₆H₅NH₂ + H₂SO₄ → C₆H₅NH₃⁺HSO₄⁻."],
["What happens when ethylamine reacts with excess bromoethane?","Repeated nucleophilic substitution gives secondary, then tertiary amines. The final product is a quaternary ammonium salt, (CH₃CH₂)₄N⁺Br⁻."],
["What forms when an amine reacts with ethanoyl chloride?","An N-substituted amide and HCl. The HCl reacts with more amine to form an ammonium salt."],
["Write the equations for propylamine and ethanoyl chloride.","CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ + CH₃COCl → CH₃CONHCH₂CH₂CH₃ + HCl. CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ + HCl → CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₃⁺Cl⁻."],
["What happens when methylamine is added to copper(II) ions, a little and then excess?","A little gives a pale blue precipitate of Cu(H₂O)₄(OH)₂. Excess gives a deep blue solution of [Cu(CH₃NH₂)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺."],
["Why are small amines soluble in water?","They form hydrogen bonds with water through the N–H group and the nitrogen lone pair."],
["Rank ammonia, primary aliphatic amines and phenylamine by base strength.","Primary aliphatic amine > ammonia > phenylamine."],
["Why are aliphatic amines stronger bases than ammonia, and phenylamine weaker?","Alkyl groups push electron density onto the nitrogen, making its lone pair more available. In phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the ring, so it is less available to accept H⁺."],
["How is a primary amine made from a halogenoalkane?","Heat with excess ammonia in ethanol under pressure. The ammonium salt formed first is deprotonated by more ammonia, releasing the amine."],
["Give the equations for bromoethane with excess ammonia.","CH₃CH₂Br + NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Br⁻. CH₃CH₂NH₃⁺Br⁻ + NH₃ → CH₃CH₂NH₂ + NH₄Br."],
["Which reducing agent turns nitriles into primary amines?","LiAlH₄ in dry ether."],
["Write the equation for reducing ethanenitrile to ethylamine.","CH₃CN + 4[H] → CH₃CH₂NH₂."],
["How is phenylamine made from nitrobenzene?","Heat under reflux with tin and concentrated hydrochloric acid. Then add sodium hydroxide to release the free amine."],
["How is phenylamine converted into a benzenediazonium ion?","React with nitrous acid, made from sodium nitrite and hydrochloric acid. Keep it below 10 °C (0–5 °C)."],
["How is an amide made from an acyl chloride?","React the acyl chloride with ammonia or an amine. This gives an amide and HCl."],
["What is condensation polymerisation?","Monomers with two functional groups join into a polymer. A small molecule such as water or HCl is lost at each link."],
["What type of polymer is nylon?","A polyamide."],
["What type of polymerisation makes nylon?","Condensation polymerisation."],
["How does a polyamide form?","A dicarboxylic acid (or diacyl chloride) reacts with a diamine. An amide link forms, with water (or HCl) lost each time."],
["How does a polyester form?","A dicarboxylic acid reacts with a diol. An ester link forms and water is lost each time."],
["How are proteins and nylon similar and different?","Both contain amide (peptide) links, –CONH–. Their repeat units are different."],
["What is addition polymerisation and why is its atom economy 100%?","Alkene monomers join as their C=C bonds open. The polymer is the only product, so atom economy is 100%."],
["Why is poly(ethenol) soluble in water, and what is it used for?","Its –OH groups hydrogen bond with water. It is used for soluble laundry bags."],
["What is a zwitterion?","An ion with both a positive and a negative charge but no overall charge. In an amino acid, H⁺ moves from –COOH to –NH₂."],
["What is the isoelectric point?","The pH at which an amino acid is mainly a zwitterion with no overall charge."],
["What happens to an amino acid when acid is added?","The –COO⁻ group gains H⁺. The amino acid becomes a positive ion."],
["What happens to an amino acid when alkali is added?","The –NH₃⁺ group loses H⁺ to OH⁻. The amino acid becomes a negative ion."],
["How does a peptide bond form?","By condensation between the –COOH of one amino acid and the –NH₂ of another, losing water. Two amino acids form a dipeptide."],
["Why are most amino acids optically active?","They have a chiral carbon. So they rotate plane-polarised light (glycine is the exception)."]],
"WCH15/organic-synthesis":[
["Why use the minimum volume of hot solvent in recrystallisation?","To make a saturated solution. So as much product as possible crystallises on cooling."],
["Why filter the hot solution during recrystallisation?","To remove insoluble impurities. Doing it hot stops crystals forming in the funnel."],
["Why cool the solution in ice during recrystallisation?","More product crystallises, increasing the yield."],
["Why wash the crystals with a little ice-cold solvent?","To remove soluble impurities on the surface without dissolving much product."],
["How are the crystals collected?","By filtration under reduced pressure using a Büchner funnel."],
["Give three reasons why percentage yield is below 100%.","Any 3 of: product lost during transfers; product left dissolved in the solvent; product stuck to filter paper or glassware; side reactions; incomplete reaction; losses during purification."],
["How are ground-glass (Quickfit) joints assembled?","Lightly grease the joints. Then push the pieces firmly together."],
["Why must cold water flow through the condenser in distillation?","To keep it cold, so the vapour condenses back into liquid."],
["How are two immiscible liquids separated?","Shake them in a separating funnel, releasing pressure now and then. Let the layers settle and run off the lower layer. Collect the organic layer and dry it."],
["Name two drying agents.","Any 2 of: anhydrous magnesium sulfate; anhydrous calcium chloride; anhydrous sodium sulfate."],
["How is an organic liquid dried?","Add anhydrous drying agent and swirl until it no longer clumps. Filter or decant off the dry liquid."],
["What is redistillation?","Distilling a liquid again to make it purer."],
["What are the main steps to purify an organic solid?","Filter under reduced pressure. Recrystallise. Check purity by melting point."]],
"WBI11/biomolecules":[
["Why is water a good solvent and transport medium?","Water molecules are polar (dipoles), so they surround and dissolve ions and other polar molecules, which can then be carried in solution."],
["What are monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides? Give an example of each.","Single sugar units (glucose), two units joined by a glycosidic bond (maltose, sucrose, lactose), and many units joined in a long chain (starch, glycogen)."],
["How is a glycosidic bond formed and broken?","Formed by a condensation reaction, releasing water. Broken by hydrolysis, using water."],
["Name the two components of starch and describe their structure.","Amylose: unbranched chain of α-glucose with 1,4 glycosidic bonds, coiled into a helix. Amylopectin: branched, with 1,4 and 1,6 glycosidic bonds."],
["Why are starch and glycogen good storage molecules?","They are compact and insoluble, so they have no osmotic effect. Branching gives many ends for rapid hydrolysis to release glucose."],
["What is a triglyceride made of, and what bond joins the parts?","One glycerol and three fatty acids, joined by ester bonds formed in condensation reactions."],
["What is the difference between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids?","Saturated: no carbon–carbon double bonds. Unsaturated: one or more C=C double bonds, which put kinks in the chain."],
["How do high LDL and HDL levels relate to cardiovascular disease risk?","High LDL raises blood cholesterol and the risk of atheroma. HDL carries cholesterol to the liver for removal, so a higher HDL level is linked to lower risk."]],
"WBI11/circulation":[
["Why do large multicellular organisms need a mass transport system?","Their surface area to volume ratio is small and diffusion distances are too long, so diffusion alone is too slow to supply cells."],
["Compare the structure of arteries and veins.","Arteries: thick walls with much muscle and elastic tissue, narrow lumen, no valves. Veins: thin walls, wide lumen, valves to prevent backflow."],
["How are capillaries adapted for exchange?","Walls one cell thick give a short diffusion distance; they are very numerous and narrow, giving a large surface area and slow blood flow."],
["Name the three stages of the cardiac cycle.","Atrial systole, ventricular systole, and diastole."],
["What makes the atrioventricular valves close?","Ventricular pressure rising above atrial pressure when the ventricles contract."],
["Outline how an atheroma develops.","The artery endothelium is damaged; an inflammatory response brings white blood cells; cholesterol builds up to form an atheroma; calcium salts and fibres harden it into a plaque, which narrows the artery and raises blood pressure."],
["Outline the blood clotting process.","Platelets stick to the damaged wall and release thromboplastin. With calcium ions this converts prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin, which forms a mesh that traps blood cells."],
["Why is the wall of the left ventricle thicker than the right?","It must generate a higher pressure to pump blood around the whole body, not just to the lungs."]],
"WBI11/health":[
["Name four risk factors for cardiovascular disease.","Any four of: high blood pressure, smoking, obesity, inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat or salt, genetics, age, sex."],
["What is the difference between correlation and causation?","Correlation: two variables change together. Causation: a change in one variable directly brings about the change in the other. Correlation alone does not prove causation."],
["How do you calculate body mass index?","BMI = mass in kg ÷ (height in m)²."],
["How do antihypertensives reduce risk, and give one drawback.","They lower blood pressure (e.g. ACE inhibitors, beta blockers, diuretics). Side effects can include dizziness, fainting or a cough."],
["How do statins reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease?","They inhibit an enzyme in the liver needed to make cholesterol, lowering blood LDL cholesterol."],
["How do anticoagulants and platelet inhibitors help, and what is the main risk?","They reduce blood clot formation (e.g. warfarin, aspirin). The risk is excessive bleeding."],
["What makes a health study valid and reliable?","A large, representative sample; control of other variables; a suitable control group; and results that are repeatable."],
["Why might people underestimate or overestimate a health risk?","Perception is affected by familiarity, whether the risk is voluntary, how far in the future the harm is, and media coverage, rather than by the actual probability."]],
"WBI11/gas-exchange-and-membrane-transport":[
["State Fick’s law in words.","Rate of diffusion is proportional to (surface area × concentration difference) ÷ thickness of the exchange surface."],
["How are the alveoli adapted for gas exchange?","Huge surface area; walls one cell thick; a dense capillary network and ventilation keep a steep concentration gradient."],
["Describe the fluid mosaic model of the cell membrane.","A phospholipid bilayer in which the molecules move freely (fluid), with proteins, cholesterol, glycoproteins and glycolipids scattered through it (mosaic)."],
["Define osmosis.","The net movement of free water molecules across a partially permeable membrane from a region of higher water potential (less solute) to lower water potential."],
["What is facilitated diffusion?","Passive movement of molecules down a concentration gradient through channel or carrier proteins. No ATP is needed."],
["How does active transport differ from diffusion?","It moves substances against their concentration gradient using carrier proteins and energy from ATP."],
["What are endocytosis and exocytosis?","Bulk transport using vesicles. Endocytosis takes material into the cell; exocytosis releases it when a vesicle fuses with the cell surface membrane. Both need ATP."],
["Why do phospholipids form a bilayer?","The phosphate heads are hydrophilic and face the water on each side; the fatty acid tails are hydrophobic and face inwards, away from water."]],
"WBI11/proteins-and-enzymes":[
["Draw in words the general structure of an amino acid.","A central carbon bonded to an amine group (NH₂), a carboxyl group (COOH), a hydrogen atom and a variable R group."],
["What is the primary structure of a protein, and what bond holds it together?","The sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain, joined by peptide bonds."],
["What are secondary and tertiary structure?","Secondary: folding into α-helices or β-pleated sheets, held by hydrogen bonds. Tertiary: further folding into a specific 3D shape, held by hydrogen, ionic and disulfide bonds and hydrophobic interactions between R groups."],
["Compare globular and fibrous proteins with examples.","Globular: compact, soluble, metabolically active (e.g. haemoglobin, enzymes). Fibrous: long chains, insoluble, strong and structural (e.g. collagen)."],
["How do enzymes speed up reactions?","They lower the activation energy by forming an enzyme–substrate complex at the active site."],
["Why are enzymes specific?","The active site has a specific shape that is complementary to only one substrate (or type of substrate)."],
["Explain the effect of increasing substrate concentration on the rate of an enzyme reaction.","Rate rises as more enzyme–substrate complexes form, then levels off when all active sites are occupied and enzyme concentration becomes limiting."],
["What happens to an enzyme at high temperature?","Bonds holding the tertiary structure break, the active site changes shape, and the substrate no longer fits: the enzyme is denatured."]],
"WBI11/nucleotides-and-nucleic-acids":[
["What are the three parts of a DNA nucleotide?","A deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group and a nitrogen-containing base (A, T, C or G)."],
["State the base-pairing rules in DNA and the bonds involved.","Adenine pairs with thymine (two hydrogen bonds); cytosine pairs with guanine (three hydrogen bonds)."],
["Give three differences between DNA and RNA.","DNA has deoxyribose, thymine and is double-stranded; RNA has ribose, uracil and is usually single-stranded."],
["What is meant by semi-conservative replication?","Each new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly made strand."],
["What do DNA helicase and DNA polymerase do?","Helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between bases to unzip the strands. DNA polymerase joins the new nucleotides together by forming phosphodiester bonds."],
["How did Meselson and Stahl’s experiment support semi-conservative replication?","Bacteria grown in heavy nitrogen and moved to light nitrogen produced DNA of intermediate density after one generation, then intermediate and light bands after two."],
["What is a gene?","A sequence of bases on a DNA molecule that codes for the sequence of amino acids in a polypeptide."],
["What is a mutation, and how can it affect a protein?","A change in the base sequence of DNA. It may change an amino acid and so the protein’s shape and function, as in cystic fibrosis."]],
"WBI11/gene-expression":[
["Describe three features of the genetic code.","Triplet (three bases code for one amino acid), non-overlapping (each base is read once), and degenerate (most amino acids have more than one codon)."],
["Outline transcription.","The DNA unwinds; RNA nucleotides pair with the template (antisense) strand; RNA polymerase joins them to form mRNA, which leaves the nucleus through a nuclear pore."],
["Outline translation.","mRNA attaches to a ribosome. tRNA molecules with complementary anticodons bring specific amino acids, which are joined by peptide bonds until a stop codon is reached."],
["What is the role of tRNA?","It carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome and its anticodon pairs with the complementary codon on mRNA."],
["Define genotype, phenotype and allele.","Genotype: the alleles an organism has. Phenotype: its observable characteristics. Allele: an alternative form of a gene."],
["What do homozygous, heterozygous, dominant and recessive mean?","Homozygous: two identical alleles. Heterozygous: two different alleles. Dominant: expressed even with one copy. Recessive: expressed only when no dominant allele is present."],
["How does the cystic fibrosis mutation cause thick, sticky mucus?","The faulty CFTR protein cannot transport chloride ions out of cells, so water does not move out by osmosis and the mucus is not diluted."],
["Name two methods of prenatal genetic screening and one ethical issue.","Amniocentesis and chorionic villus sampling. Issues include the small risk of miscarriage and decisions about abortion."]],
"WBI12/cell-structure-and-microscopy":[
["Give three differences between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.","Prokaryotes have no nucleus (circular DNA free in the cytoplasm), no membrane-bound organelles and smaller (70S) ribosomes; many have plasmids and a cell wall."],
["State the function of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus.","Rough ER: ribosomes on its surface make proteins, which it folds and transports. Golgi: modifies proteins and packages them into vesicles."],
["Describe the route of a protein that is secreted from a cell.","Made at ribosomes on the rough ER, carried in vesicles to the Golgi, modified, packaged into secretory vesicles, and released by exocytosis."],
["What is the function of mitochondria and of lysosomes?","Mitochondria: site of aerobic respiration, producing ATP. Lysosomes: contain digestive enzymes that break down unwanted material."],
["What is the difference between magnification and resolution?","Magnification is how many times bigger the image is than the object. Resolution is the ability to tell two close points apart."],
["Write the equation for magnification.","Magnification = image size ÷ actual size."],
["Why do electron microscopes show more detail than light microscopes?","Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light, giving a much higher resolution."],
["How are cells organised in a multicellular organism?","Similar cells form tissues; tissues form organs; organs work together in organ systems."]],
"WBI12/reproduction-and-cell-division":[
["Why is mitosis important?","It produces genetically identical cells for growth, repair and asexual reproduction."],
["Name the stages of mitosis in order and say what happens in metaphase and anaphase.","Prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase. Metaphase: chromosomes line up on the equator. Anaphase: centromeres divide and chromatids are pulled to opposite poles."],
["What happens during interphase?","The cell grows, makes new organelles and proteins, and replicates its DNA."],
["Give two ways meiosis produces genetic variation.","Crossing over between homologous chromosomes, and independent assortment of chromosomes."],
["How are sperm cells specialised?","A flagellum for swimming, many mitochondria for energy, and an acrosome containing enzymes to digest the zona pellucida; the nucleus is haploid."],
["What are the acrosome reaction and the cortical reaction?","Acrosome reaction: enzymes are released to digest a path through the zona pellucida. Cortical reaction: after one sperm enters, cortical granules make the zona pellucida harden, preventing other sperm entering."],
["What does haploid mean, and why must gametes be haploid?","Having one set of chromosomes. At fertilisation two haploid gametes fuse to restore the diploid number."],
["Describe double fertilisation in flowering plants.","The pollen tube delivers two male nuclei. One fuses with the egg to form the zygote; the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the triploid endosperm."]],
"WBI12/development-and-inheritance":[
["What is a stem cell?","An undifferentiated cell that can keep dividing and can differentiate into specialised cells."],
["Distinguish totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells.","Totipotent: can form every cell type, including placental cells. Pluripotent: can form almost any body cell type but not a whole organism. Multipotent: can form a limited range of cell types."],
["How do cells become specialised?","Different genes are switched on. Active genes are transcribed to mRNA, which is translated into proteins that change the cell’s structure and function."],
["What is epigenetic modification? Give two examples.","A change in gene expression without a change in the DNA base sequence, e.g. DNA methylation and histone modification."],
["What is polygenic inheritance and what kind of variation does it produce?","A characteristic controlled by several genes at different loci. It gives continuous variation."],
["Give an example of how genotype and environment interact to affect phenotype.","Examples: human height depends on genes and diet; fur colour in some animals depends on temperature; cancers arise from genes and environmental factors."],
["What are sex-linked characteristics?","Characteristics controlled by genes on the sex chromosomes, usually the X. They appear more often in males, who have only one X chromosome."],
["Give one argument for and one against using embryonic stem cells.","For: they could treat currently incurable conditions. Against: embryos are destroyed, which some see as destroying a potential life."]],
"WBI12/the-structure-and-function-of-plants":[
["Name three structures found in plant cells but not in animal cells.","Cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts, large permanent vacuole (also amyloplasts, plasmodesmata, middle lamella)."],
["Describe the structure of cellulose.","Long unbranched chains of β-glucose joined by 1,4 glycosidic bonds. Hydrogen bonds between chains form strong microfibrils."],
["Compare the structure and function of xylem vessels and phloem sieve tubes.","Xylem: dead, hollow, lignified vessels carrying water and mineral ions upwards and giving support. Phloem: living sieve tube elements with companion cells, carrying sugars and other organic solutes up and down."],
["What is the function of sclerenchyma fibres?","Support. They are dead cells with thick lignified walls."],
["Why do plants need nitrate, magnesium and calcium ions?","Nitrate: to make amino acids, proteins and DNA. Magnesium: to make chlorophyll. Calcium: to make calcium pectate in the middle lamella."],
["What makes plant fibres useful, and why are they more sustainable than oil-based products?","They are strong and flexible. Plants are renewable and their products are biodegradable."],
["Describe William Withering’s contribution and how modern drug testing differs.","He tested foxglove (digitalis) on patients by trial and error to find a safe dose. Modern testing uses pre-clinical tests then three phases of clinical trials with placebos and double-blind design."],
["Why are a placebo and a double-blind trial used?","A placebo shows whether the drug works better than no treatment. Double-blind means neither patients nor doctors know who has the drug, removing bias."]],
"WBI12/biodiversity":[
["Define species.","A group of organisms with similar characteristics that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring."],
["What is biodiversity, and what is species richness?","The variety of living organisms in an area. Species richness is the number of different species in a habitat."],
["What is the difference between a habitat and a niche?","Habitat: the place where an organism lives. Niche: the role of a species in its habitat, including how it uses resources and interacts."],
["Name three types of adaptation with an example of each.","Anatomical (thick fur), behavioural (migration), physiological (producing antifreeze proteins or venom)."],
["Outline natural selection.","A population shows genetic variation due to mutation. A selection pressure means individuals with advantageous alleles are more likely to survive, reproduce and pass those alleles on. Their frequency increases over generations."],
["What does the Hardy–Weinberg equation let you calculate?","Allele and genotype frequencies in a population: p + q = 1 and p² + 2pq + q² = 1."],
["What are the three domains, and what evidence supports this classification?","Bacteria, Archaea and Eukaryota. It is based on molecular evidence, such as differences in ribosomal RNA and membrane structure."],
["How can genetic diversity within a species be measured?","By the number of different alleles or by the heterozygosity index (the proportion of heterozygotes in the population)."]],
"WBI12/populations-and-conservation":[
["What is the difference between in-situ and ex-situ conservation?","In-situ: conserving species in their natural habitat. Ex-situ: conserving them outside it, e.g. zoos and seed banks."],
["How do zoos help conserve endangered species?","Captive breeding programmes, reintroduction into the wild, research and education."],
["Why are studbooks used in captive breeding?","To choose mates that are not closely related, which avoids inbreeding and keeps genetic diversity."],
["How do seed banks conserve plants, and why are seeds tested regularly?","Seeds are dried and stored cold so they stay viable for a long time. Samples are germinated regularly to check they are still viable."],
["Give two advantages of storing seeds rather than whole plants.","Any two of: less space needed; cheaper; many individuals stored so more genetic diversity; less vulnerable to disease or vandalism."],
["What is meant by endemism?","A species that is found in only one geographical area."],
["Why does a small population face a higher risk of extinction?","The gene pool is small, so there is low genetic diversity and inbreeding, and the population is less able to adapt to change."],
["What is sustainability in the use of biological resources?","Using resources in a way that meets present needs without reducing their availability for future generations."]],
"WBI14/Photosynthesis":[
["Where do the light-dependent and light-independent reactions take place?","Light-dependent: thylakoid membranes. Light-independent (Calvin cycle): stroma."],
["What are the products of the light-dependent reactions?","ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen."],
["What is photolysis?","The splitting of water using light energy into hydrogen ions, electrons and oxygen."],
["Describe how ATP is made in the light-dependent reactions.","Excited electrons pass along the electron transport chain, releasing energy that pumps H⁺ into the thylakoid space. H⁺ ions flow back through ATP synthase, which makes ATP from ADP and Pi (chemiosmosis)."],
["Outline the Calvin cycle.","CO₂ combines with RuBP (catalysed by RUBISCO) to give two GP. GP is reduced to GALP using ATP and reduced NADP. Most GALP regenerates RuBP using ATP; some is used to make glucose and other compounds."],
["What is the difference between cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation?","Cyclic uses only photosystem I and makes only ATP. Non-cyclic uses both photosystems and makes ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen."],
["What is a limiting factor? Name three for photosynthesis.","The factor in shortest supply that restricts the rate. Light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature."],
["How do an absorption spectrum and an action spectrum differ?","Absorption spectrum: how much light of each wavelength a pigment absorbs. Action spectrum: the rate of photosynthesis at each wavelength."]],
"WBI14/Ecosystems-and-biomass":[
["Define ecosystem, community and population.","Ecosystem: all the organisms and the non-living factors in an area. Community: all the populations of different species in a habitat. Population: all the organisms of one species in a habitat."],
["What is the difference between abiotic and biotic factors?","Abiotic: non-living factors such as light, temperature and pH. Biotic: living factors such as predation, competition and disease."],
["Define gross primary productivity and net primary productivity.","GPP: the rate at which producers convert light energy into chemical energy. NPP = GPP − energy lost in plant respiration; it is the energy available to the next trophic level."],
["Give three reasons why energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient.","Energy is lost as heat from respiration; some parts are not eaten; some food is not digested and is lost in faeces."],
["How do you calculate the efficiency of energy transfer?","(Energy transferred to the next trophic level ÷ energy available at the previous level) × 100."],
["What is succession?","The change in the species making up a community over time, from pioneer species to a climax community."],
["How do pioneer species make succession possible?","They colonise bare ground, and when they die and decompose they form soil and add nutrients, making conditions suitable for other species."],
["What is the role of microorganisms in the carbon cycle?","Decomposers break down dead organic matter and release carbon dioxide through respiration."]],
"WBI14/Changes-to-populations-and-the-environment":[
["Name three greenhouse gases and explain the greenhouse effect.","Carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour. They absorb infrared radiation emitted by the Earth’s surface and re-emit it, warming the atmosphere."],
["Give two types of evidence for climate change.","Any two of: temperature records; carbon dioxide levels from ice cores; pollen in peat bogs; tree-ring widths (dendrochronology)."],
["How can rising temperature affect organisms?","It changes the rate of enzyme reactions and development, alters distribution of species, and changes the timing of life cycles."],
["Why are predictions from climate models uncertain?","They extrapolate from current data, rely on assumptions about future emissions, and many factors interact."],
["What is allopatric speciation?","A population is split by a geographical barrier; different selection pressures and mutations change allele frequencies until the groups can no longer interbreed."],
["What is sympatric speciation?","A new species forms without geographical separation, through reproductive isolation such as different breeding seasons or behaviour."],
["How can biofuels and reforestation help reduce climate change?","Biofuels are close to carbon neutral, as the CO₂ released was recently absorbed in photosynthesis. Reforestation removes CO₂ from the atmosphere."],
["Why does the scientific community use peer review?","Other scientists check the methods and conclusions before publication, so the findings are valid and reliable."]],
"WBI14/microbiology":[
["What is aseptic technique and why is it used?","Methods such as flaming the loop and working near a flame that prevent contamination of cultures and the release of microorganisms."],
["Name the four phases of a bacterial growth curve.","Lag, exponential (log), stationary and death (decline) phases."],
["Why does a bacterial population enter the stationary phase?","Nutrients run out and toxic waste builds up, so the death rate equals the division rate."],
["Give two ways of measuring the growth of a microbial culture.","Any two of: cell counts with a haemocytometer; dilution plating to count colonies; turbidity using a colorimeter; dry mass."],
["How do bacteria and viruses differ in structure?","Bacteria are cells with a cell wall, cytoplasm, ribosomes and circular DNA. Viruses are not cells: nucleic acid in a protein coat (capsid), sometimes with an envelope, and they replicate only inside host cells."],
["What is the difference between bactericidal and bacteriostatic antibiotics?","Bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria. Bacteriostatic antibiotics stop them growing and reproducing."],
["How does antibiotic resistance develop in a bacterial population?","A random mutation gives resistance. When the antibiotic is used, resistant bacteria survive and reproduce, passing on the allele, so its frequency increases."],
["Give two ways hospitals reduce the spread of antibiotic-resistant infections.","Any two of: hand washing and hygiene codes; screening and isolating patients; prescribing antibiotics only when needed and completing the course."]],
"WBI14/disease-and-defence":[
["Name three non-specific defences against infection.","Any three of: skin, stomach acid, lysozyme in tears, inflammation, phagocytosis, interferon, fever."],
["Describe phagocytosis.","A phagocyte recognises and engulfs the pathogen into a vesicle (phagosome). Lysosomes fuse with it and enzymes digest the pathogen."],
["What is an antigen?","A molecule, usually a protein on a cell surface, that triggers an immune response."],
["What do T helper cells and T killer cells do?","T helper cells release cytokines that stimulate B cells and T killer cells to divide. T killer cells destroy infected body cells."],
["What do plasma cells and memory cells do?","Plasma cells secrete antibodies. Memory cells stay in the blood and give a faster, larger response if the same antigen is met again."],
["Compare active and passive immunity.","Active: the body makes its own antibodies and memory cells (infection or vaccination); long-lasting. Passive: antibodies are received from elsewhere (placenta, breast milk, injection); immediate but short-lived."],
["How does HIV weaken the immune system?","It infects and destroys T helper cells, so B cells and T killer cells are not activated effectively."],
["How does Mycobacterium tuberculosis evade the immune system?","It survives inside macrophages and can lie dormant within tubercles in the lungs, becoming active again if the immune system weakens."]],
"WBI14/forensic-techniques":[
["Name five things used to estimate time of death.","Body temperature, degree of muscle contraction (rigor mortis), extent of decomposition, forensic entomology and stage of succession."],
["How is body temperature used to estimate time of death, and what affects it?","The body cools after death in a predictable way. Cooling rate depends on body size, clothing, air temperature and position."],
["What causes rigor mortis?","After death, muscles run out of ATP, so the bonds between muscle proteins cannot be broken and the muscles stay contracted. It later passes off as the proteins break down."],
["How does forensic entomology help?","The species of insects present and the stage of their life cycle indicate how long the body has been dead."],
["Outline how a DNA profile is made.","DNA is extracted and amplified by PCR. Fragments are separated by size using gel electrophoresis and made visible, giving a pattern of bands."],
["Outline the polymerase chain reaction.","Heat to about 95 °C to separate the strands; cool to about 55 °C so primers bind; heat to about 70 °C so DNA polymerase builds new strands. The cycle is repeated many times."],
["How does gel electrophoresis separate DNA fragments?","DNA is negatively charged, so it moves towards the positive electrode. Smaller fragments move faster and further through the gel."],
["What are DNA profiles used for?","Identifying individuals in forensic cases, and showing genetic relationships between people or between species."]],
"WBI15/respiration":[
["Name the four stages of aerobic respiration and where each occurs.","Glycolysis (cytoplasm), link reaction and Krebs cycle (mitochondrial matrix), oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane)."],
["What are the net products of glycolysis from one glucose molecule?","Two pyruvate, two ATP (net) and two reduced NAD."],
["What happens in the link reaction?","Pyruvate is decarboxylated and dehydrogenated to form acetyl groups, which join coenzyme A to make acetyl CoA. CO₂ and reduced NAD are produced."],
["What is produced in each turn of the Krebs cycle?","Two CO₂, three reduced NAD, one reduced FAD and one ATP."],
["Explain how ATP is made by chemiosmosis in oxidative phosphorylation.","Electrons from reduced NAD and FAD pass along the electron transport chain. Energy released pumps H⁺ into the intermembrane space. H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase, making ATP."],
["What is the role of oxygen in aerobic respiration?","It is the final electron acceptor; it combines with electrons and H⁺ to form water."],
["What happens to pyruvate in anaerobic respiration in animals, and why?","It is reduced to lactate, which regenerates NAD so glycolysis can continue."],
["Define respiratory quotient.","RQ = volume of CO₂ produced ÷ volume of O₂ used in the same time. About 1.0 for carbohydrates and 0.7 for lipids."]],
"WBI15/cardiac-and-skeletal-muscle":[
["What does myogenic mean?","The heart muscle contracts without needing a nerve impulse from outside."],
["Describe how the heartbeat is coordinated.","The SAN sends a wave of depolarisation across the atria, which contract. The AVN delays it, then passes it down the bundle of His and Purkyne fibres, so the ventricles contract from the apex upwards."],
["What do the P wave, QRS complex and T wave on an ECG represent?","P: depolarisation of the atria. QRS: depolarisation of the ventricles. T: repolarisation of the ventricles."],
["How do you calculate cardiac output?","Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate."],
["Describe the sliding filament theory.","Calcium ions bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin and exposing binding sites on actin. Myosin heads bind, tilt to pull the actin along, then detach using ATP. The sarcomere shortens."],
["What is ATP needed for in muscle contraction?","To detach the myosin head from actin and return it to its original position, and to pump calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum."],
["Compare fast twitch and slow twitch muscle fibres.","Slow twitch: many mitochondria and capillaries, much myoglobin, aerobic, fatigue slowly. Fast twitch: few mitochondria, little myoglobin, anaerobic, powerful but fatigue quickly."],
["What are antagonistic muscle pairs?","Two muscles that work against each other at a joint: when one contracts the other relaxes, e.g. biceps and triceps."]],
"WBI15/homeostatic-principles-and-thermoregulation":[
["Define homeostasis.","The maintenance of a stable internal environment within narrow limits, despite changes outside."],
["What is negative feedback?","A change from the normal level is detected and triggers a response that reverses the change, returning the level to normal."],
["What is positive feedback?","A change triggers a response that increases the change further, e.g. during blood clotting."],
["Which part of the brain controls body temperature, and how does it detect change?","The hypothalamus. It monitors blood temperature and receives impulses from temperature receptors in the skin."],
["Give three responses to an increase in body temperature.","Sweating increases, vasodilation of skin arterioles increases blood flow near the surface, and hairs lie flat."],
["Give three responses to a decrease in body temperature.","Shivering, vasoconstriction of skin arterioles, hairs raised, and a higher metabolic rate."],
["Why must core body temperature be kept constant?","Enzymes work fastest at their optimum temperature; too high and they denature, too low and reactions are too slow."],
["How is heart rate increased during exercise?","Chemoreceptors detect a fall in blood pH (more CO₂). The cardiovascular control centre in the medulla sends impulses along the sympathetic nerve to the SAN, which fires more often."]],
"WBI15/the-kidneys-osmoregulation-and-DNA-control":[
["What is ultrafiltration and where does it happen?","High pressure in the glomerulus forces small molecules such as water, glucose, urea and ions into the Bowman’s capsule. Blood cells and large proteins stay in the blood."],
["What is selective reabsorption, and where does most of it occur?","Useful substances are taken back into the blood. All the glucose and most water and ions are reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule."],
["What is the role of the loop of Henle?","It creates a high solute concentration in the medulla, so water can be reabsorbed from the collecting duct by osmosis."],
["How does ADH control the water content of the blood?","When blood water potential falls, osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus cause the pituitary to release more ADH. It makes the collecting duct walls more permeable to water, so more is reabsorbed and urine is more concentrated."],
["Why is urea produced?","Excess amino acids cannot be stored. They are deaminated in the liver, and the ammonia formed is converted to less toxic urea."],
["How do steroid hormones such as oestrogen affect cells?","They pass through the cell membrane, bind to a receptor inside the cell, and the complex acts as a transcription factor that switches genes on or off."],
["How do peptide hormones affect cells?","They bind to receptors on the cell surface membrane, activating a second messenger such as cyclic AMP inside the cell."],
["What is a transcription factor?","A protein that binds to DNA and controls whether a gene is transcribed."]],
"WBI15/structure-and-function-of-nerve-tissue-and-responses-to-stimuli":[
["What is the resting potential and how is it maintained?","About −70 mV across the axon membrane. The sodium–potassium pump moves 3 Na⁺ out for 2 K⁺ in, and the membrane is more permeable to K⁺, which diffuses out."],
["Describe depolarisation and repolarisation in an action potential.","Depolarisation: voltage-gated sodium channels open and Na⁺ diffuses in. Repolarisation: sodium channels close, potassium channels open and K⁺ diffuses out."],
["What is the refractory period and why does it matter?","A short time after an action potential when another cannot be generated. It keeps impulses separate and travelling in one direction."],
["How does myelination speed up conduction?","Depolarisation occurs only at the nodes of Ranvier, so the impulse jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction)."],
["Outline transmission across a synapse.","An impulse opens calcium channels; Ca²⁺ enters; vesicles release neurotransmitter by exocytosis; it diffuses across the cleft and binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, opening sodium channels."],
["How do rod cells respond to light?","Light breaks rhodopsin into retinal and opsin. Sodium channels close, the rod cell hyperpolarises and releases less inhibitory neurotransmitter, so the bipolar cell depolarises."],
["Compare nervous and hormonal coordination.","Nervous: electrical impulses, fast, short-lived, localised. Hormonal: chemicals in the blood, slower, longer-lasting, widespread."],
["What is the pupil reflex and why is it useful?","In bright light the circular muscles of the iris contract and the pupil narrows; in dim light the radial muscles contract and it widens. It protects the retina and controls the light reaching it."]],
"WBI15/plant-hormones-coordination-and-the-brain":[
["How does auxin (IAA) cause a shoot to bend towards light?","Auxin moves to the shaded side, where it makes cells elongate more, so the shoot bends towards the light."],
["What is phytochrome and how does it control flowering?","A pigment with two forms. Red light converts Pr to Pfr; far-red light or darkness converts it back. The amount of Pfr tells the plant the night length, which triggers or inhibits flowering."],
["State the main function of the cerebrum, cerebellum, medulla and hypothalamus.","Cerebrum: thought, memory, voluntary movement. Cerebellum: balance and coordination. Medulla: heart rate and breathing. Hypothalamus: temperature and water balance."],
["Compare CT, MRI and fMRI scans.","CT uses X-rays to show structure. MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves for detailed images of soft tissue. fMRI shows brain activity by detecting changes in blood oxygen."],
["What is habituation?","A decreased response to a repeated harmless stimulus, as less neurotransmitter is released at the synapse."],
["What did Hubel and Wiesel’s work show?","There is a critical period in early life when visual stimulation is needed for the visual cortex to develop normally."],
["How does L-dopa treat Parkinson’s disease?","Parkinson’s involves a lack of dopamine. L-dopa crosses into the brain and is converted to dopamine."],
["How does MDMA (ecstasy) affect synapses?","It blocks the reuptake of serotonin, so serotonin stays in the synaptic cleft and keeps stimulating the postsynaptic neurone."]],
"WBI15/gene-technology":[
["What is recombinant DNA?","DNA made by joining DNA from two different sources."],
["What do restriction enzymes and DNA ligase do?","Restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific base sequences, often leaving sticky ends. Ligase joins DNA fragments together."],
["What is a vector in genetic modification? Give two examples.","Something that carries a gene into a cell, e.g. a plasmid or a virus."],
["How are bacteria that have taken up a recombinant plasmid identified?","A marker gene, such as antibiotic resistance or fluorescence, is included, so only transformed bacteria survive or glow."],
["What is a microarray used for?","To find which genes are active (expressed) in a cell, by seeing which labelled mRNA or cDNA binds to known DNA probes."],
["What is bioinformatics?","Using computers to store and analyse large amounts of biological data, such as DNA and protein sequences."],
["What are 'knockout' mice used for?","A gene is deactivated so its function can be studied from the effect on the animal."],
["Give one benefit and one risk of genetically modified crops.","Benefit: higher yield, better nutrition or pest resistance. Risk: transfer of genes to wild plants, reduced biodiversity, or unknown long-term effects."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-acceleration-of-a-freely-falling-object":[
["What does free fall mean?","The only force acting on the object is its weight. Resistive forces are negligible."],
["What does g represent in this experiment?","The gravitational field strength (acceleration of free fall) at the Earth’s surface."],
["Why can the suvat equations be used for the falling ball?","g is roughly constant near the Earth’s surface, so the acceleration is uniform."],
["How do you find g from a graph of t² against h?","From h = ½gt², t² = (2/g)h. So g = 2 ÷ gradient."],
["How do you find g from a graph of v² against h?","From v² = 2gh, the gradient is 2g. So g = gradient ÷ 2."],
["How can light gates be used to find g?","Drop a small ball or dowel through a known height. Light gates at each end start and stop the timer."],
["What safety precaution is needed with the clamp stand?","Clamp it to the bench or add a heavy weight to the base so it cannot topple."],
["How can you line up the light gates so the ball passes through them?","Use a plumb line to mark the vertical path, then position the gates along it."],
["Why use a small ball-bearing rather than a large ball?","Air resistance has less effect, so free fall is a better assumption."],
["Why leave a gap between the release point and the first light gate?","The ball is already moving quickly when it reaches the gate. This keeps the time spent blocking the gate small."],
["Why would the experiment be invalid if air resistance were not negligible?","It would not be free fall. Air resistance increases with speed, so acceleration would not be uniform and the suvat equations would not apply."],
["Give three reasons your value of g might differ from the accepted value.","Any three of: human reaction time with a stopwatch; air resistance; measuring height to different points on the ball; delay in releasing the ball."],
["Why are light gates better than a stopwatch?","They time more precisely, removing human reaction time and reducing uncertainty."],
["How can the results be made more reliable?","Repeat each height several times and average, ignoring anomalies. Measure height to the same point on the ball every time."],
["How do you estimate the uncertainty in the time readings?","Half the range of the repeat readings at each height."],
["How do you find the percentage uncertainty in t²?","Double the percentage uncertainty in t."],
["How should graph scales be chosen?","The plotted points should cover at least half the grid in each direction, using easy scales."],
["How many repeat readings should be taken at each height?","At least three, so anomalies can be spotted."],
["How do you calculate percentage uncertainty?","(Uncertainty ÷ mean value) × 100%."],
["How do you find the percentage difference from the accepted value of g?","(Your value − 9.81) ÷ 9.81 × 100%."],
["Would you expect your value of g to be higher or lower than the accepted value? Why?","Usually lower. Air resistance reduces the resultant downward force, so the measured acceleration is smaller."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-viscosity-of-a-liquid":[
["What is the main hazard in this practical and how is it controlled?","Spilled washing-up liquid or glycerol is very slippery. Clean up any spills straight away."],
["Why would a narrow tube give unreliable results?","Stokes’ law only applies to laminar flow. A narrow tube can disturb the flow around the ball."],
["Why are light gates unsuitable for timing the falling ball?","The ball may not break the beam cleanly, and the liquid can scatter or absorb the light."],
["Why might the flow become turbulent if the ball falls close to the wall?","The liquid speeds up in the narrow gap, so pressure falls there. This pulls the ball towards the wall and the flow becomes uneven."],
["Which forces act on a ball falling through a liquid?","Weight acts downwards. Drag (viscous force) and upthrust act upwards."],
["How is the uncertainty in r² related to the uncertainty in r?","The percentage uncertainty in r² is twice that in r."],
["How is the radius of a small ball measured accurately?","Measure the diameter with a micrometer, then halve it."],
["What is the force balance at terminal velocity?","The resultant force is zero. Weight = upthrust + drag."],
["How should times be taken as the ball passes marked bands?","Fix rubber bands round the tube at known heights. Read at eye level and use the lap function of a stopwatch at each band."],
["How can timing be made consistent?","The same person times every run, reading at eye level and stopping at the same point relative to each band."],
["Why is a steel ball useful in this practical?","It is magnetic, so a magnet can lift it out of the tube after each run."],
["Where should the top band be placed, and why?","Low enough that the ball has already reached terminal velocity before it reaches the band."],
["If the ball is at terminal velocity by the first band, how will speeds measured over two sections compare?","They will be the same, because the ball is no longer accelerating."],
["How far apart should the lower bands be?","Far enough apart that the time between them is long enough to measure precisely."],
["Give the equation for viscosity from terminal velocity.","η = 2r²g(ρsphere − ρliquid) ÷ 9v."],
["How can the density of a ball bearing be found?","Measure its diameter with a micrometer and calculate its volume. Measure its mass on a balance and divide mass by volume."],
["What is the SI unit of viscosity?","Pascal seconds (Pa s)."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-youngs-modulus-of-a-material":[
["What quantity describes a material’s stiffness, and how is it calculated?","The Young modulus. Tensile stress ÷ tensile strain."],
["How is stress calculated?","Stress = force ÷ cross-sectional area (σ = F/A)."],
["How is strain calculated?","Strain = extension ÷ original length."],
["What is the unit of stress?","The pascal, Pa (N m⁻²)."],
["Does strain have a unit? Why?","No. It is a ratio of two lengths, so the units cancel."],
["What is the unit of the Young modulus?","The pascal, Pa (N m⁻²)."],
["How do you find the cross-sectional area of a thin wire accurately?","Measure the diameter with a micrometer at several places and in different directions. Average, then use A = πd²/4."],
["What protective equipment is needed and why?","Safety goggles, in case the wire snaps under tension."],
["Why should room temperature stay constant?","Metals expand and contract with temperature, which would change the length and affect the extension readings."],
["Why is a small load added before taking readings?","To straighten the wire and remove kinks, so readings show only the extension caused by the load."],
["How do you find the Young modulus from a graph of extension against load?","Find the gradient of the straight section (extension ÷ load). E = L ÷ (A × gradient)."],
["The wire stays longer after the load is removed. What has happened?","It went past its elastic limit and deformed plastically."],
["How is a hanging mass converted into a force?","F = mg."],
["What precautions should be taken with the hanging masses?","Keep feet clear of the area below. Put a padded box or bucket underneath to catch them."],
["Describe a simple way to measure the extension.","Fix a marker such as tape to the wire and read its movement against a fixed ruler."],
["Why does the diameter of the wire matter?","A thick wire stretches too little to measure. A very thin wire may deform plastically before enough readings are taken."],
["Why does Searle’s apparatus use a second, unloaded reference wire?","It compensates for changes such as temperature, which affect both wires equally."],
["Why must the wire be long?","Extension is proportional to length (x = FL/AE), so a long wire gives a larger, more measurable extension."],
["How can the end of the wire be held so it doesn’t slip?","Clamp it between two wooden blocks fixed to the bench."],
["What tool helps read the marker against the ruler precisely?","A set square, to avoid parallax."],
["Why rotate the wire between diameter readings?","To check the wire is circular and average out any variation in its cross-section."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-speed-of-sound-in-air":[
["Which equation links wave speed, frequency and wavelength?","v = fλ."],
["How do you find frequency from an oscilloscope trace?","Measure the time period of one cycle from the time base. f = 1/T."],
["Which two signals are displayed on the oscilloscope?","The signal going to the loudspeaker. The signal received by the microphone."],
["What happens to the traces as the microphone is moved away from the speaker?","They shift horizontally relative to each other, because the phase difference changes."],
["What drives the loudspeaker, and what else is it connected to?","A signal generator. It is also connected to the oscilloscope."],
["How should the two traces be arranged for comparison?","One above the other, so a peak of one can be lined up with a trough (or peak) of the other."],
["How is the starting position set?","Move the speaker until a peak of one trace lines up with a trough of the other. Measure the distance with a metre ruler."],
["How do you find the wavelength by moving the speaker?","Move it until the traces line up in the same way again. The distance moved is one wavelength."],
["Give two sources of uncertainty.","Any two of: resolution of the oscilloscope scale; the thickness of the traces; judging exactly when the traces line up."],
["How should the frequency be chosen?","So the wavelength is easy to measure with a metre ruler, e.g. about 4 kHz."],
["How many cycles should each trace show?","About three complete cycles."],
["Why does a full-cycle shift mean the speaker moved one wavelength?","One cycle is one time period, and sound travels one wavelength in one period."],
["Why measure frequency on the oscilloscope rather than read the signal generator?","The oscilloscope gives a more precise, calibrated value."]],
"WPH13/standing-waves-on-a-vibrating-string":[
["How does a stationary wave differ from a progressive wave in terms of energy?","A stationary wave stores energy rather than transferring it."],
["How is a stationary wave formed on a string?","Two waves of the same frequency and wavelength travel in opposite directions and superpose."],
["What is a node?","A point that always has zero displacement, where the waves interfere destructively."],
["What is an antinode?","A point of maximum displacement, where the waves interfere constructively."],
["Where are the nodes and antinodes at the fundamental frequency?","Nodes at each end. One antinode in the middle."],
["What produces the wave on the string?","A vibration generator driven by a signal generator."],
["What is used to change the vibrating length of the string?","A movable bridge."],
["Why switch the signal generator on for a few minutes before use?","So its output frequency settles to a steady value."],
["How do you stop the clamp stand toppling?","Use a counterweight or clamp it to the bench."],
["How does increasing the vibrating length affect the fundamental frequency?","It decreases. Frequency is inversely proportional to length."],
["How does increasing mass per unit length affect the fundamental frequency?","It decreases."],
["How does increasing tension affect the fundamental frequency?","It increases."],
["Give the equation for the fundamental frequency of a string.","f = (1/2L)√(T/μ)."],
["How is the tension changed?","Add slotted masses to a hanger on the end of the string. T = mg."],
["Give two precautions when using hanging masses.","Any two of: don’t stand underneath; put a padded box or bucket below; keep the masses low."],
["Three 100 g masses hang on a 100 g hanger. Find the tension (g = 9.81 N kg⁻¹).","Total mass 0.4 kg. T = 0.4 × 9.81 ≈ 3.9 N."],
["How do you find the mass per unit length of a string?","Weigh a long length on a balance and divide by its length."],
["Why use a long sample to find mass per unit length?","It reduces the percentage uncertainty."],
["If the driving frequency is doubled from the first harmonic, what pattern appears?","The second harmonic, with two loops."],
["Which equation links wave speed, frequency and wavelength?","v = fλ."],
["At the fundamental, how does wavelength compare with the vibrating length?","λ = 2L."],
["Give the speed of a wave on a string in terms of tension.","v = √(T/μ)."],
["From a graph of 1/f against L at the fundamental, how do you find the wave speed?","Gradient = 2/v. So v = 2 ÷ gradient."],
["What protective equipment is needed if a wire might snap?","Safety goggles."],
["How can the actual frequency of the vibration generator be measured?","Connect it to an oscilloscope, measure the period and use f = 1/T."],
["Give two sources of uncertainty in this practical.","Any two of: nodes are blurred, so lengths are hard to measure; the signal generator dial may be poorly calibrated; judging the exact resonant frequency."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-wavelength-of-light":[
["What precautions are needed when using a laser?","Any two of: never look into the beam; remove reflective objects from its path; display a warning sign while it is on; keep the beam below eye level."],
["Why should the screen have a matt (non-shiny) surface?","It scatters the light instead of reflecting a strong beam, reducing the risk of eye damage."],
["What does monochromatic mean?","Light of a single wavelength (single frequency)."],
["What does it mean for two light sources to be coherent?","Same wavelength (frequency) and a constant phase difference."],
["Which equation links order, wavelength, grating spacing and angle?","nλ = d sin θ."],
["How do you find the slit spacing of a grating?","d = 1 ÷ (number of lines per metre)."],
["How do you find the angle to a maximum experimentally?","Measure the grating-to-screen distance D and the distance h from the central maximum to the order. tan θ = h/D."],
["How do grating fringes differ from double-slit fringes?","With many slits the maxima are much sharper and brighter."],
["Give a use of a diffraction grating instead of a prism.","Splitting light into a spectrum to measure wavelengths, e.g. in a spectrometer."],
["Where is the zero-order maximum?","Straight through, in line with the original beam."],
["Give three properties of laser light.","Monochromatic. Coherent. A narrow parallel (collimated) beam."],
["What pattern does white light give through a grating?","A white central maximum. Each order is a spectrum, with violet nearest the centre and red furthest out."],
["Why does white light give coloured spectra?","White light contains many wavelengths, and each is diffracted through a different angle."],
["What causes the bright fringes?","Waves from the slits arrive in phase and interfere constructively."],
["What causes the dark gaps between fringes?","Waves arrive in antiphase (half a cycle out) and interfere destructively."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-electrical-resistivity-of-a-material":[
["Which formula links resistivity, resistance, area and length?","ρ = RA/L."],
["How does decreasing a wire’s cross-sectional area affect its resistance?","Resistance increases. R is inversely proportional to area."],
["How does shortening a wire affect its resistance?","Resistance decreases. R is directly proportional to length."],
["How does a higher resistivity affect resistance?","Resistance increases in proportion."],
["What is the unit of resistivity?","Ohm metre (Ω m)."],
["How do you find the cross-sectional area of a thin wire accurately?","Measure the diameter with a micrometer at several points and average. A = πd²/4."],
["How can the length of wire in the circuit be varied?","Fix one end and move a crocodile clip (flying lead) along the wire."],
["How is the voltmeter connected?","In parallel across the length of wire being tested."],
["How is the ammeter connected?","In series with the wire."],
["Why switch off between readings?","So the wire does not heat up and its temperature stays constant."],
["Why must the temperature of the wire stay constant?","Resistance depends on temperature, so it is a control variable."],
["Why does a metal’s resistance increase when it gets hotter?","The ions vibrate more, so electrons collide with them more often."],
["How do you find resistivity from a graph of R against L?","Gradient = R/L. Resistivity = gradient × cross-sectional area."],
["Why use a small current?","To reduce heating of the wire."],
["How can you make sure lengths are measured from the same point every time?","Tape a metre rule beside the wire with its zero at the fixed end."],
["Give two sources of uncertainty in this practical.","Any two of: contact resistance at the clips and leads; zero error in the length; non-uniform diameter."],
["What single instrument could replace the ammeter and voltmeter?","A multimeter set to measure resistance (an ohmmeter)."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-emf-and-internal-resistance-of-an-electrical-cell":[
["What is internal resistance and how is it found from lost volts?","The resistance of the materials inside the source. r = lost volts ÷ current."],
["Define e.m.f.","The energy transferred by the source to each coulomb of charge. It equals the terminal p.d. when no current flows."],
["Write the equation linking e.m.f., current, load resistance and internal resistance.","ε = I(R + r)."],
["What is the difference between e.m.f. and terminal p.d. called?","Lost volts."],
["Write e.m.f. in terms of terminal p.d.","ε = V + Ir."],
["Show that V = ε − Ir is a straight-line equation.","From ε = IR + Ir and V = IR, V = −rI + ε. This has the form y = mx + c."],
["How is internal resistance found from a graph of V against I?","The gradient is −r, so r = −gradient."],
["How is e.m.f. found from a graph of V against I?","It is the intercept on the V axis."],
["Why disconnect the cell between readings?","To stop the cell and wires heating, which would change the resistances."],
["Why is a rechargeable cell unsuitable?","Its internal resistance is very small and hard to measure accurately."],
["Why use a fresh cell?","An old cell’s internal resistance changes during the experiment."],
["Give two safety precautions for this practical.","Any two of: disconnect when not taking readings; don’t touch bare connections; components may get hot; don’t short-circuit the cell."],
["How could you check your value of internal resistance?","Repeat with a different method or compare with a value from a meter or data sheet."],
["Why might the measured e.m.f. be slightly lower than the true value?","A real voltmeter does not have infinite resistance, so a small current flows and there are some lost volts."],
["Why are high-voltage lab supplies safe to use?","They have a very high internal resistance, so only a tiny current can flow even if you touch the terminals."]],
"WPH16/investigating-change-in-momentum":[
["Why is the runway tilted?","To compensate for friction on the trolley."],
["How does friction affect the results?","It reduces the resultant force, so the trolley is slower at the light gate and the gradient is lower."],
["How could friction be reduced further?","Use an air track."],
["How is the trolley’s start position set?","Pull it back until the hanger is just below the pulley, with the string taut."],
["How should the light gate and string be set up?","The interrupt card passes through the middle of the light gate. The hanger reaches the floor just before the card reaches the gate, with the string taut."],
["What makes up the total mass M of the system?","The trolley, any masses on it, the mass hanger, the slotted masses and the string."],
["Give two ways to measure the distance over which the force acts.","The distance the trolley travels before the hanger lands. The height the hanger falls."],
["How is the time T for which the force acts measured?","With a stopwatch, from release until the hanger hits the floor (or with a second light gate)."],
["How is the accelerating force calculated?","F = mg, where m is the hanging mass (hanger plus slotted masses)."],
["Give the momentum of the system at the light gate.","p = Mv."],
["How is the trolley’s speed at the light gate found?","v = length of interrupt card ÷ time to pass through the gate."],
["What is the change in momentum if the system starts from rest?","Mv, the final momentum."],
["What equation comes from impulse = change in momentum?","FT = Δp, so mgT = Mv."],
["How is the uncertainty in a timing found?","Half the range of the repeat readings."],
["Which graph tests the relationship, and what is its gradient?","Plot mT against v. The gradient is M/g, which can be compared with the expected value."],
["Give two safety precautions.","Any two of: secure the masses on the trolley; keep feet clear of the falling masses; stop the trolley at the end of the runway."],
["Describe the energy changes in the system.","Gravitational potential energy of the falling masses becomes kinetic energy of the system. Some is lost to friction and air resistance."],
["How would a second light gate improve the experiment?","It times T electronically, removing reaction-time error from the stopwatch."],
["How is the percentage uncertainty in a gradient found?","(Best gradient − worst gradient) ÷ best gradient × 100%. Some use half the difference between steepest and shallowest lines."]],
"WPH16/analysing-collisions-between-small-spheres":[
["What is mass × velocity, and is it conserved in collisions?","Momentum. It is conserved in collisions when no external forces act."],
["Which instrument measures the diameter of a small sphere precisely?","A micrometer screw gauge or vernier callipers."],
["How can momentum vectors show whether momentum was conserved?","Draw the momentum vectors head to tail. If the final momentum vectors add to the initial momentum (the triangle closes), momentum was conserved."],
["After video analysis gives the velocities, how do you get momentum?","Multiply each velocity by the mass of that sphere."],
["What must you record about each velocity besides its size?","Its direction, e.g. as an angle to a reference line."],
["Why is the surface tilted slightly rather than perfectly level?","To compensate for friction between the spheres and the surface."],
["What is the disadvantage of glass marbles for filming?","Their edges are hard to see clearly on video, so positions are less accurate."],
["Where should the camera be positioned?","Directly above, so it sees both spheres’ paths before and after the collision."],
["What makes a collision elastic?","Total kinetic energy is the same before and after."],
["How do you check conservation of momentum numerically in two dimensions?","Resolve into x and y components. Total momentum before equals total after in each direction separately."],
["How is the uncertainty in a velocity estimated from repeats?","Half the range of the repeat readings."],
["How do you find the speed from horizontal and vertical components?","Pythagoras: v = √(vx² + vy²)."],
["How do you find the direction from the components?","tan θ = vy ÷ vx."]],
"WPH16/capacitor-charging-and-discharging":[
["What must you check before connecting an electrolytic capacitor, and why?","That it is connected the correct way round (polarity). If reversed it can overheat and burst."],
["Give the time constant of an RC circuit.","τ = RC."],
["What does the time constant tell you?","The time to charge to 63% of the final value, or to discharge to 37% of the starting value."],
["Give the p.d. across a discharging capacitor at time t.","V = V₀e^(−t/RC)."],
["Which graph shows that discharge is exponential?","ln(V/V₀) (or ln V) against t gives a straight line."],
["How do you find RC from a graph of ln(V/V₀) against t?","Gradient = −1/RC. So RC = −1 ÷ gradient."],
["Why do a trial discharge first?","To choose sensible time intervals for the readings, depending on how fast it discharges."],
["Give the p.d. across a charging capacitor at time t.","V = V₀(1 − e^(−t/RC))."],
["What limit applies to the supply voltage?","It must not exceed the capacitor’s voltage rating."],
["How can an oscilloscope and stopwatch be used to find the time constant?","Use the lap function to record the times when the voltage reaches set values, then plot a graph."],
["Why is the lap function better than reading the stopwatch at each value?","You can keep watching the voltage without looking away, reducing timing uncertainty."],
["How could you measure an unknown resistance?","With an ohmmeter (multimeter)."],
["How should error bars for time be found?","Repeat readings at a mid-range voltage and use half their range."]],
"WPH16/calibrating-a-thermistor-in-a-potential-divider-circuit":[
["How does an NTC thermistor’s resistance change as it gets hotter?","It decreases."],
["Which equation links thermistor resistance and absolute temperature?","R = R₀e^(b/T), where R₀ and b are constants and T is in kelvin."],
["Why plot ln R rather than R?","It turns the exponential relationship into a straight line, which is easier to analyse."],
["Why heat the water slowly?","So the thermistor and thermometer keep up with the water’s actual temperature."],
["What is the benefit of slow heating?","The thermistor and thermometer are at the same temperature as the water when readings are taken."],
["Give safety precautions for using a Bunsen burner.","Any two of: tie back hair and loose clothing; use a heatproof mat; use the safety flame when not heating; don’t touch hot equipment."],
["What is the hazard of wires near a Bunsen flame?","The insulation can melt. Keep leads away from the flame and hot apparatus."],
["What does a potential divider do?","It gives an output voltage that depends on the ratio of two resistances."],
["How can readings near 0 °C be obtained?","Start with the thermistor in a beaker of ice and water, then heat to take readings up to 100 °C."],
["On a graph of ln R against 1/T, what is the intercept?","ln R₀, from ln R = b/T + ln R₀."],
["How do you make sure the water is at the same temperature throughout?","Stir it before each reading."],
["Give the output voltage when it is taken across the thermistor.","Vout = Vin × Rthermistor ÷ (Rthermistor + Rfixed)."],
["How should a thermometer be read?","At eye level with the liquid, to avoid parallax error."],
["What goes wrong if the fixed resistor is much larger than the thermistor?","The output voltage barely changes with temperature, so changes are hard to measure."],
["What goes wrong if the fixed resistor is too small?","The output range may not suit the voltmeter, and changes may be hard to read."]],
"WPH16/determining-the-specific-latent-heat-of-a-phase-change":[
["Define specific latent heat.","The energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without changing its temperature."],
["Which equation links energy, mass and specific latent heat?","Q = mL."],
["Define specific heat capacity.","The energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K, without a change of state."],
["Which equation gives the energy to change temperature?","Q = mcΔT."],
["When does the ice–water mixture reach its lowest temperature?","When the last of the ice has melted."],
["How do you find the mass of water in the beaker?","Weigh the empty beaker, then the beaker with water, and subtract."],
["Write the energy balance for the experiment.","Energy lost by the warm water cooling = energy to melt the ice + energy to warm the melted ice to the final temperature."],
["What happens to the calculated L if heat flows in from the surroundings?","L comes out too low, because some ice is melted by heat from the room."],
["How can heat gain from the room be reduced?","Insulate (lag) the beaker and use a lid."],
["Why must the ice be at 0 °C (melting) before it is added?","Otherwise some energy would warm the ice up to 0 °C, which isn’t in the calculation."],
["Why use crushed ice?","It melts faster, so there is less time for heat to come in from the surroundings."],
["How can ice be kept at 0 °C and separated from melted water?","Put it in a funnel over a beaker so meltwater drains away."],
["What happens to the uncertainty when you subtract two temperatures?","The absolute uncertainties add, so the uncertainty in the temperature change is larger."],
["Why is using too much ice a problem?","It takes longer to melt, so more heat enters from the surroundings."],
["How do you calculate the percentage difference from the accepted value?","(Your value − accepted value) ÷ accepted value × 100%."],
["Give two safety precautions.","Any two of: take care handling very cold ice; mop up spills to avoid slipping; take care with hot water."]],
"WPH16/investigating-the-pressure-and-volume-relationships-for-a-gas":[
["What does Boyle’s law say?","At constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume for a fixed mass of gas. pV = constant."],
["What is standard atmospheric pressure in kPa?","About 101 kPa."],
["What does a graph of 1/V against p look like?","A straight line through the origin, because 1/V is directly proportional to p."],
["Why must the volume be changed slowly?","So the gas temperature stays constant, which Boyle’s law requires."],
["Which instrument reads the pressure of the trapped gas?","A Bourdon gauge (pressure gauge)."],
["What could be used to increase the pressure on the oil?","A pump, such as a foot or tyre pump."],
["What happens to the trapped air when the pressure increases?","It is compressed, so its volume decreases."],
["How is the volume of air calculated from the tube?","Volume = length of air column × cross-sectional area. A = πr², with the diameter measured by a micrometer or callipers."],
["Why are readings less reliable when the pressure is decreased?","Oil sticks to the walls as the level falls, so the air column reading is inaccurate."],
["How should the oil level be read?","With your eye level with the surface, reading the bottom of the meniscus."],
["Why can the length of the air column be used instead of its volume?","V = AL and the area is constant, so volume is proportional to length."],
["How is the constant in pV = constant found?","Plot p against 1/V. The gradient is the constant."],
["Give two safety precautions.","Wear safety goggles. Use a safety screen in case the tube breaks under pressure."]],
"WPH16/investigating-the-absorption-of-gamma-radiation-by-lead":[
["Give safety precautions when using a radioactive source.","Any three of: keep exposure time short; keep at arm’s length; handle with long tongs; display warning signs; never point it at anyone."],
["How should a radioactive source be handled?","With long-handled tongs, keeping it at arm’s length from your body."],
["How should a source be stored when not in use?","In a lead-lined container, locked away, with a radiation hazard sign."],
["How does the intensity of gamma radiation from a point source vary with distance?","Inverse square law: I ∝ 1/d². Doubling the distance quarters the intensity."],
["What apparatus measures the count rate?","A Geiger–Müller tube connected to a counter."],
["Why is cobalt-60 a suitable source?","It emits gamma, is approved for school use, and has a half-life of about 5 years. Its activity is low enough to be safe but high enough to measure."],
["Why record counts over a longer time?","A larger count reduces the percentage uncertainty caused by random decay."],
["What should be measured before the source is brought into the room?","The background count."],
["How do you correct the counts for background?","Subtract the background count from each reading."],
["How do you get the count rate from a total count?","Divide the total count by the time."],
["What trial run could be done first?","Find the greatest lead thickness that still gives a count clearly above background."],
["Why might an old gamma source be unsuitable?","Its activity may have fallen too low to give useful counts."],
["Define half-thickness.","The thickness of absorber that halves the count rate."],
["Why can’t you predict when a nucleus will decay?","Decay is random: you can’t know which nucleus will decay or when."],
["Why take several readings at each thickness?","Decay is random, so averaging gives a more reliable count rate."],
["How is the uncertainty estimated from repeats?","Half the range of the repeat readings."],
["How do you measure the thickness of the lead sheets?","With a micrometer or vernier callipers at several places, then average."],
["Which graph should be plotted?","ln(corrected count rate) against thickness. It should be a straight line."],
["What does the gradient of that graph give?","−μ, where μ is the absorption coefficient."]],
"WPH16/determining-the-value-of-an-unknown-mass-using-the-resonant-frequencies":[
["State Hooke’s law in words.","The extension of a spring is proportional to the force applied, up to the limit of proportionality."],
["Write Hooke’s law as an equation.","F = kx. k is the spring constant in N m⁻¹ and x the extension in m."],
["How is a hanging mass linked to the extension?","Weight balances the spring force: mg = kx, so m = kx/g."],
["Give the period of a mass–spring oscillator.","T = 2π√(m/k)."],
["Does the length of the spring affect the period?","No. The period depends only on the mass and the spring constant."],
["How do you stop the clamp stand toppling?","Use a counterweight or G-clamp on the base."],
["Give safety precautions when adding masses to a spring.","Wear safety goggles in case the spring snaps. Keep feet clear of the masses."],
["How do free and forced oscillations differ?","Free: no driving force, oscillates at its natural frequency. Forced: a driving force supplies energy and sets the frequency."],
["How should the period be measured accurately?","Time 10 oscillations, repeat and average, then divide by 10."],
["What can be added to improve timing?","A fiducial marker (pointer) at the centre of the oscillation."],
["Which graph lets you find the unknown mass?","T² against mass for known masses. Measure T for the unknown mass, square it and read off the mass."],
["How could a data logger be used instead?","Put a motion sensor below the mass to record its motion, giving the period and extension."]],
"WCH13/finding-the-molar-volume-gas":[
["Write the equation for calcium carbonate reacting with ethanoic acid.","CaCO₃ + 2CH₃COOH → Ca(CH₃COO)₂ + CO₂ + H₂O."],
["Give two ways to collect and measure the gas produced.","In a gas syringe. Or over water in an upturned measuring cylinder."],
["Give safety precautions for the calcium carbonate and ethanoic acid experiment.","Any 3 of: wear eye protection; keep ethanoic acid away from flames as it is flammable; work in a ventilated room; make sure the delivery tube is not blocked; clear up spills and broken glass straight away."],
["Why must the bung go into the flask as quickly as possible?","So that as little gas as possible escapes before it is collected."],
["How do you calculate moles from mass?","Moles = mass ÷ molar mass (Mr)."],
["How does adding more calcium carbonate affect the volume of CO₂?","If calcium carbonate is limiting, more gives more CO₂. If it is already in excess, the volume does not change."],
["Which equation links moles, concentration and volume?","Moles = concentration (mol dm⁻³) × volume (dm³)."],
["How do you find moles of gas from volume at room temperature and pressure?","Moles = volume (dm³) ÷ 24. The molar volume is 24 dm³ mol⁻¹."],
["Give sources of error when using a gas syringe.","Any 3 of: the plunger can stick; gas escapes before the bung is in; the bung is not airtight; some solid is lost when transferring it."],
["Give sources of error when collecting over water.","Any 3 of: some CO₂ dissolves in the water; gas escapes before the bung is in; the bung is not airtight; some solid is lost when transferring it."],
["How can you stop gas escaping before the bung is fitted?","Keep the solid in a small tube inside the flask, separate from the acid. Fit the bung, then tip the tube over to mix them."]],
"WCH13/enthalpy-change-reaction-hess-law":[
["Why is Hess’s law used to find some enthalpy changes?","Some reactions cannot be measured directly, so two other reactions that can be measured are combined in an energy cycle."],
["Outline the calorimetry measurement.","Add a weighed solid to a measured volume of excess acid in a polystyrene cup and record the temperature change. Repeat for the second substance."],
["How do you calculate the enthalpy change for each reaction?","q = mcΔT, then ΔH = −q ÷ moles of the limiting solid."],
["Why is a polystyrene cup with a lid used?","It is a good insulator, which reduces heat exchange with the surroundings."],
["How can a more accurate temperature change be found?","Record temperature at regular intervals before and after mixing and extrapolate the cooling line back to the time of mixing."]],
"WCH13/titration-to-find-concentration-solution-hydrochloric-acid":[
["Outline how to carry out the titration.","Pipette a fixed volume of the standard alkali into a conical flask, add indicator, and add acid from a burette until the end point. Repeat until titres are concordant."],
["What are concordant titres?","Titres within 0.10 cm³ of each other; the mean is taken from these."],
["Why is a conical flask rinsed with water but a burette rinsed with the solution it will hold?","Water in the flask does not change the moles of alkali. Water in the burette would dilute the acid and give a larger titre."],
["Name a suitable indicator and its colour change for a strong acid–strong base titration.","Phenolphthalein (pink in alkali to colourless) or methyl orange (yellow in alkali to orange at the end point)."],
["How is the acid concentration calculated?","Moles of alkali = c × V; use the equation ratio to get moles of acid; concentration = moles ÷ mean titre in dm³."]],
"WCH13/preparation-of-a-standard-solution-and-titration":[
["How do you make a standard solution of sulfamic acid?","Weigh about 2.5 g by difference. Dissolve it in about 100 cm³ of distilled water in a beaker. Transfer to a 250 cm³ volumetric flask, rinsing the beaker in too. Make up to the mark with distilled water and invert to mix."],
["Which weighing method is used for the sulfamic acid?","Weighing by difference: weigh the boat with solid, transfer it, then reweigh the boat."],
["Give safety precautions for this titration.","Any 3 of: wear eye protection; avoid skin contact with the acid and alkali; fill the burette below eye level; clear spills straight away."],
["What colour is methyl orange in acid, alkali and at the end point?","Red in acid, yellow in alkali, orange at the end point."],
["Why use a pipette rather than a measuring cylinder?","A pipette measures a fixed volume much more precisely."],
["What is the end point?","The point at which the indicator first changes colour permanently."],
["Which titres are used for the mean?","Concordant titres, within 0.10 cm³ of each other."],
["Why is the rough titre not used in the mean?","It is only a quick guide and is less accurate."],
["To what precision are burette readings recorded?","To the nearest 0.05 cm³."],
["Write the equation for sulfamic acid reacting with sodium hydroxide.","H₂NSO₃H + NaOH → H₂NSO₃Na + H₂O."],
["Describe the titration of sodium hydroxide with sulfamic acid.","Pipette 25.0 cm³ of NaOH into a conical flask with a few drops of methyl orange. Fill the burette with acid and record the start reading. Do a rough titration, then accurate ones adding dropwise near the end point. Repeat until concordant titres are obtained."],
["Why swirl the conical flask?","To mix the reactants so they react fully."],
["Why is a 25 cm³ titre better than 10 cm³?","The percentage error in the reading is smaller."],
["Why use a white tile?","To see the colour change more clearly."],
["Why might a solution be diluted before titrating?","To give a larger titre and so a smaller percentage error."],
["Which equation links moles, concentration and volume?","n = c × V, with V in dm³."],
["How do you find the alkali concentration from the titration results?","Find moles of acid with n = c × V. Use the mole ratio to find moles of alkali. Divide by the alkali volume in dm³."],
["Why add only a few drops of indicator?","Indicators are weak acids, so too much would affect the titre."]],
"WCH13/rates-of-hydrolysis-of-halogenoalkanes":[
["What type of reaction is the hydrolysis of a halogenoalkane?","Nucleophilic substitution."],
["Why can halogenoalkanes undergo nucleophilic substitution?","The C–X bond is polar, so the carbon is δ+ and attracts nucleophiles."],
["What is the nucleophile in hydrolysis with water?","Water."],
["Why can water act as a nucleophile?","Its oxygen has lone pairs it can donate."],
["What affects the rate of hydrolysis of a halogenoalkane?","Any 2 of: which halogen it contains; whether it is primary, secondary or tertiary; temperature."],
["Give safety precautions for the halogenoalkane hydrolysis experiment.","Any 3 of: wear eye protection; keep halogenoalkanes and ethanol away from flames; avoid skin contact with silver nitrate; take care with hot water."],
["Describe how to compare the rates of hydrolysis of halogenoalkanes.","Mix a few drops of each halogenoalkane with ethanol in separate tubes and warm in a water bath at about 50 °C. Warm silver nitrate solution in the same bath. Add the silver nitrate and time how long a precipitate takes to appear."],
["Why is a water bath used?","To keep all tubes at the same controlled temperature. Warming also speeds up the reaction."],
["If NaOH is used for hydrolysis, what must be done before adding silver nitrate?","Neutralise the excess with dilute nitric acid. Otherwise brown silver oxide precipitates and hides the result."],
["Write the equation for 1-iodopropane reacting with water.","CH₃CH₂CH₂I + H₂O → CH₃CH₂CH₂OH + H⁺ + I⁻."],
["What precipitates do chloride, bromide and iodide give with silver nitrate?","AgCl white. AgBr cream. AgI yellow."],
["Why is ethanol used as a solvent?","Halogenoalkanes do not dissolve in water. Ethanol lets them mix with the water and silver nitrate."],
["How does the halogen affect the rate of hydrolysis?","The rate increases from chloro to bromo to iodo. The C–X bond enthalpy decreases, so the bond breaks more easily."],
["How does primary, secondary or tertiary structure affect the rate?","Tertiary is fastest and primary slowest, with secondary in between."]],
"WCH13/chlorination-2-methylpropan-2-ol":[
["Which reagent converts 2-methylpropan-2-ol to 2-chloro-2-methylpropane?","Concentrated hydrochloric acid, shaken with the alcohol at room temperature."],
["Why is the mixture shaken in a separating funnel with the tap opened from time to time?","To mix the layers and release the pressure that builds up."],
["Why is the organic layer washed with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution?","To neutralise and remove any remaining acid."],
["How is the product dried and purified?","Add an anhydrous salt (e.g. sodium sulfate) until the liquid is clear, decant, then distil and collect the fraction at the product’s boiling temperature."],
["How can you tell which layer is the organic layer?","Add a little water: the layer that increases in volume is the aqueous one. The less dense organic layer is usually on top."]],
"WCH13/oxidation-propan-1-ol-propanol-propanoic-acid":[
["Which oxidising agent is used and what colour change is seen?","Potassium dichromate(VI) acidified with dilute sulfuric acid. Orange to green."],
["How are the conditions changed to get propanal or propanoic acid?","Distil the product off as it forms, with limited oxidising agent, for propanal. Heat under reflux with excess oxidising agent for propanoic acid."],
["Why does distilling give the aldehyde?","Propanal has a lower boiling temperature than the alcohol (no hydrogen bonding between its molecules), so it leaves the mixture before it can be oxidised further."],
["Why are anti-bumping granules added?","To give smooth boiling and prevent the liquid boiling violently."],
["How could you test the product to show it is an aldehyde and not an acid?","Warm with Tollens’ reagent (silver mirror) or Fehling’s solution (red precipitate); an acid would instead fizz with sodium carbonate."]],
"WCH13/analysis-of-some-inorganic-and-organic-unknowns":[
["How do you test for carbonate ions?","Add dilute acid. Bubble the gas through limewater, which turns cloudy (CO₂). Hydrogencarbonates give the same result."],
["How do you test for sulfate ions?","Add dilute hydrochloric acid, then barium chloride solution. A white precipitate of BaSO₄ forms."],
["How do you test for halide ions?","Add dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate. Chloride: white precipitate, soluble in dilute ammonia. Bromide: cream, soluble in concentrated ammonia. Iodide: yellow, insoluble in ammonia."],
["In what order should anion tests be done, and why?","Carbonate, then sulfate, then halide. Carbonate gives white precipitates with barium and silver ions, which would give false positives."],
["How do you test for carbon dioxide?","Bubble it through limewater. It turns cloudy as CaCO₃ forms: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O."],
["How do you test for oxygen?","A glowing splint relights."],
["How is a flame test carried out?","Clean a nichrome wire in concentrated HCl and the flame. Dip it in the sample. Hold it in a blue Bunsen flame and note the colour."],
["What flame colour does lithium give?","Red (crimson)."],
["What flame colour does sodium give?","Yellow."],
["What flame colour does potassium give?","Lilac."],
["What flame colour does rubidium give?","Red-violet."],
["What flame colour does caesium give?","Blue-violet."],
["What flame colour does calcium give?","Orange-red (brick red)."],
["What flame colour does strontium give?","Red (crimson)."],
["What flame colour does barium give?","Pale green."],
["How do you test for an alkene?","Shake with bromine water. It decolourises from orange to colourless."],
["How do you identify the halogen in a halogenoalkane?","Warm with aqueous NaOH (or ethanol and water) to release halide ions. Acidify with dilute nitric acid and add silver nitrate. Use the precipitate colour and solubility in ammonia."],
["How does acidified dichromate distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols?","Primary and secondary alcohols turn it from orange to green. Primary gives an aldehyde or carboxylic acid, secondary a ketone. Tertiary alcohols do not react, so it stays orange."],
["How do you test for an aldehyde?","Any 2 of: warm with Fehling’s or Benedict’s solution, giving a brick-red precipitate; warm with Tollens’ reagent, giving a silver mirror."],
["How do you test for a carboxylic acid?","Add sodium carbonate or hydrogencarbonate. It fizzes, giving CO₂."],
["Which instrumental techniques help identify organic compounds?","Any 2 of: mass spectrometry; infrared spectroscopy; NMR spectroscopy."],
["How does mass spectrometry help identify a compound?","The molecular ion peak gives the relative molecular mass. Fragment peaks show pieces of the molecule."],
["How does infrared spectroscopy help identify a compound?","Absorptions at characteristic wavenumbers show which bonds are present. They are matched to data book values."]],
"WCH16/rate-of-iodine-propanone-reaction":[
["Write the equation for iodine reacting with propanone in acid.","CH₃COCH₃ + I₂ → CH₃COCH₂I + H⁺ + I⁻."],
["What is the catalyst in the iodine–propanone reaction?","H⁺ ions, supplied by sulfuric acid."],
["Why are propanone and acid used in large excess?","Their concentrations stay almost constant. So any change in rate is due to iodine alone."],
["How is the rate followed by titration?","Remove samples at timed intervals and quench them. Titrate the remaining iodine with sodium thiosulfate. Plot titre against time."],
["What is used to quench the samples?","Sodium hydrogencarbonate, which neutralises the acid catalyst."],
["Which indicator is used in the thiosulfate titration, and what is the colour change?","Starch. Blue-black to colourless."],
["What is the order with respect to iodine?","Zero order."],
["What are the orders with respect to H⁺ and propanone?","First order in each."],
["What is the rate equation for the iodine–propanone reaction?","Rate = k[CH₃COCH₃][H⁺]."],
["What happens to the rate if [I₂] is doubled?","No change, because it is zero order in iodine."],
["What happens to the rate if [H⁺] is doubled?","The rate doubles, because it is first order in H⁺."],
["What does the rate equation say about the mechanism?","The rate-determining step involves propanone and H⁺ only. Iodine reacts in a later, faster step."],
["Give safety precautions for the iodine–propanone experiment.","Any 3 of: keep propanone away from flames as it is highly flammable; avoid skin contact with propanone and iodine; sulfuric acid is corrosive, so wear goggles; work in a well-ventilated area."],
["What shape is the graph of titre against time?","A straight line with a negative gradient, showing zero order in iodine."],
["Write the equation for peroxodisulfate reacting with iodide.","S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂."],
["Write the equation for acidified hydrogen peroxide reacting with iodide.","H₂O₂ + 2H⁺ + 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2H₂O."],
["Write the equation for thiosulfate reacting with iodine.","I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻."],
["What is measured in an iodine clock reaction?","The time for the blue-black colour to appear."],
["A plot of 1/t against [I⁻] is a straight line through the origin. What is the order in I⁻?","First order, because 1/t is proportional to the rate."],
["In a clock reaction, what is the initial rate proportional to?","1/t, where t is the time for the colour to appear."],
["What must be kept constant when varying [I⁻] in a clock reaction?","Any 3 of: total volume; peroxodisulfate concentration and volume; thiosulfate amount; starch amount; temperature."],
["Give sources of error in an iodine clock reaction.","Any 2 of: judging when the colour appears; temperature changes; uncertainties in measuring volumes; delay in starting the timer."]],
"WCH16/finding-activation-energy-of-reaction":[
["Write the Arrhenius equation and say what each symbol means.","k = Ae^(−Ea/RT). A is the pre-exponential factor, Ea the activation energy in J mol⁻¹, R the gas constant and T the temperature in K."],
["Which function reverses e to the power of x?","The natural logarithm, ln."],
["How do you convert °C to K?","Add 273."],
["How do you find the gradient of a straight line?","Change in y ÷ change in x."],
["Write the Arrhenius equation in log form.","ln k = ln A − Ea/(RT)."],
["When ln k is plotted against 1/T, what do the gradient and intercept show?","The gradient is −Ea/R. The y-intercept is ln A."],
["The gradient of ln k against 1/T is −9.1 × 10³ K. What is Ea?","Ea = −gradient × R = 9.1 × 10³ × 8.31 ≈ 7.6 × 10⁴ J mol⁻¹ (76 kJ mol⁻¹)."],
["The intercept of ln k against 1/T is −0.7. What is A?","A = e^(−0.7) ≈ 0.50."],
["Outline how to find activation energy experimentally.","Measure the time for a fixed change at several temperatures. Use 1/t as a measure of k. Plot ln(1/t) against 1/T. Ea = −gradient × R."],
["If k is proportional to 1/t, what does a graph of ln(1/t) against 1/T give?","A straight line with gradient −Ea/R."],
["How is Ea found from a graph of ln t against 1/T?","The gradient is +Ea/R. So Ea = gradient × R."]],
"WCH16/finding-ka-value-for-weak-acid":[
["What gives an accurate measurement of pH?","A calibrated pH meter."],
["What is a weak acid?","An acid that only partly dissociates in water."],
["What colour change does phenolphthalein show when alkali is added to acid?","Colourless to pale pink at the end point."],
["Give two sources of error in a titration.","Any 2 of: uncertainty in the burette and pipette readings; judging the exact end point colour; air bubbles in the burette tip."],
["Why use a pH meter rather than an indicator?","Any 2 of: it gives a numerical, more precise value; it is not subjective; it does not add a substance to the solution."],
["Write the Ka expression for a weak acid HA.","Ka = [H⁺][A⁻] ÷ [HA]."],
["How can Ka be found experimentally?","Titrate the weak acid with strong alkali to find the end-point volume. Then add half that volume of alkali to a fresh sample of acid. Measure its pH with a calibrated meter: at half-neutralisation pH = pKa."],
["How is Ka calculated from the measured pKa?","Ka = 10^(−pKa)."],
["What does a large Ka mean?","A stronger acid, which dissociates more."],
["How is pKa calculated from Ka?","pKa = −log Ka."],
["How is the pH of a buffer related to pKa?","pH = pKa + log([A⁻] ÷ [HA]). When [A⁻] = [HA], pH = pKa."]],
"WCH16/investigating-electrochemical-cells":[
["What does each half-cell contain?","An element in two different oxidation states."],
["Define oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons.","Oxidation is loss of electrons. Reduction is gain of electrons."],
["Why are metal electrodes cleaned with sandpaper?","To remove the oxide layer and give good electrical contact."],
["Which way do electrons flow in the external circuit?","From the negative electrode to the positive electrode."],
["What does the salt bridge do?","It completes the circuit and keeps each solution electrically neutral."],
["Why must the salt bridge salt be unreactive?","So it does not react with the solutions and change the concentrations or cell potential."],
["What moves through the salt bridge?","Ions."],
["What are standard conditions for a cell?","Ion concentrations of 1 mol dm⁻³. Pressure of 100 kPa. Temperature of 298 K."],
["What is EMF?","Electromotive force: the cell potential measured when no current flows."],
["What sign must E°cell have for a reaction to be feasible?","Positive."],
["How is E°cell calculated?","E°cell = E°(positive electrode) − E°(negative electrode)."],
["Why is 1 mol dm⁻³ silver nitrate avoided in a school half-cell?","It is a strong oxidising agent and corrosive, so it is hazardous at that concentration (and expensive)."],
["Why can’t potassium be used as an electrode?","It reacts violently with water, giving hydrogen and potassium hydroxide."],
["Why may experimental cell potentials differ from theoretical values?","The conditions may not be standard."],
["For Ag⁺/Ag (+0.80 V) and Cu²⁺/Cu (+0.34 V), give the overall equation and E°cell.","2Ag⁺ + Cu → 2Ag + Cu²⁺. E°cell = +0.46 V."],
["Is the half-cell with the more negative E° oxidised or reduced?","Oxidised. It is the negative electrode."]],
"WCH16/carry-out-redox-titrations":[
["Define oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons.","Oxidation is loss of electrons. Reduction is gain of electrons."],
["Write the ionic equation for iron(II) reacting with manganate(VII) in acid.","MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺. Fe²⁺ is oxidised and MnO₄⁻ is reduced."],
["Why is no indicator needed in a manganate(VII) titration?","Manganate(VII) is purple and its reduced form Mn²⁺ is almost colourless. The end point is the first permanent pale pink, when MnO₄⁻ is in excess."],
["Why is the manganate(VII) titration done in acid?","The reaction needs H⁺ ions."],
["Why isn’t potassium manganate(VII) a primary standard?","Its solutions are not stable. It slowly decomposes to form MnO₂."],
["Why isn’t hydrated iron(II) sulfate a primary standard?","Fe²⁺ is slowly oxidised to Fe³⁺ by air, so its composition changes."],
["Why use a pipette, not a measuring cylinder, for the iron(II) solution?","A pipette measures volume much more precisely."],
["Write the ionic equation for iodine reacting with thiosulfate.","I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻. Iodine is reduced and thiosulfate is oxidised."],
["To what precision should burette readings be recorded?","To the nearest 0.05 cm³."],
["What is the end point of a titration?","The point where the colour change first becomes permanent."],
["Which titres are used to calculate the mean?","Concordant titres, within 0.10 cm³ of each other."],
["Why is the rough titre left out of the mean?","It is only a quick estimate and is less accurate."],
["Describe how to titrate iron(II) solution with potassium manganate(VII).","Pipette 25.0 cm³ of Fe²⁺ solution (with dilute sulfuric acid) into a conical flask. Fill the burette and read the start volume. Do a rough titration, then repeat adding dropwise near the end point. Continue until concordant titres are obtained."],
["Why swirl the flask during a titration?","To mix the reactants so they react completely."],
["Why is a titre of about 25 cm³ better than 10 cm³?","The percentage error in the reading is smaller."],
["Why place a white tile under the flask?","To see the colour change more clearly."],
["How does a redox titration differ from an acid–base titration?","Acid–base titrations are neutralisation reactions. Redox titrations involve electron transfer."],
["Which equation links moles, concentration and volume?","n = c × V, with V in dm³."],
["Which equation links moles, mass and molar mass?","n = m ÷ M."],
["What is the colour change at the end point of an iodine–thiosulfate titration?","Starch is added near the end: blue-black to colourless. Without starch, pale yellow to colourless."]],
"WCH16/preparation-transition-metal-complex":[
["What is a transition metal?","A d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with a partly filled d-subshell."],
["What is a ligand?","A molecule or ion that donates a lone pair to a metal ion, forming a dative bond."],
["What is a complex?","A central metal ion surrounded by ligands joined by dative bonds."],
["How is the mass of a solid measured accurately?","By weighing by difference."],
["What are the hazards of concentrated ammonia and how is it handled?","It is corrosive and its fumes irritate. Use it in a fume cupboard with gloves and goggles."],
["How are the crystals collected?","Filtration under reduced pressure with a Büchner funnel."],
["Write the equation for hydrated copper(II) sulfate reacting with ammonia.","CuSO₄·5H₂O + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄]SO₄·H₂O + 4H₂O."],
["Why might the yield of the complex be below 100%?","Any 3 of: incomplete reaction; product left in solution; losses during transfer; losses during washing."],
["Why might a yield above 100% be calculated?","Any 2 of: crystals not fully dry; impurities present."],
["Why use a warm water bath to dissolve copper(II) sulfate?","Warming makes it dissolve faster."],
["Why cool the product mixture in ice?","Solubility is lower when cold, so more crystals form."],
["How do you recover product left in the beaker?","Rinse it with a little cold solvent and pour this into the Büchner funnel."],
["Why wash the crystals in the funnel?","To remove soluble impurities from their surface."],
["How is percentage yield calculated?","Actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100."],
["Why must the crystals be dry before weighing?","Leftover solvent adds mass and makes the yield seem too high."]],
"WCH16/analysis-of-some-inorganic-and-organic-unknowns":[
["How do you test for carbonate ions?","Add dilute acid. Bubble the gas through limewater, which turns cloudy (CO₂). Hydrogencarbonates give the same result."],
["How do you test for sulfate ions?","Add dilute hydrochloric acid, then barium chloride solution. A white precipitate of BaSO₄ forms."],
["How do you test for halide ions?","Add dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate. Chloride: white precipitate, soluble in dilute ammonia. Bromide: cream, soluble in concentrated ammonia. Iodide: yellow, insoluble in ammonia."],
["In what order should anion tests be done, and why?","Carbonate, then sulfate, then halide. Carbonate gives white precipitates with barium and silver ions, which would give false positives."],
["How do you test for carbon dioxide?","Bubble it through limewater. It turns cloudy as CaCO₃ forms: Ca(OH)₂ + CO₂ → CaCO₃ + H₂O."],
["How do you test for oxygen?","A glowing splint relights."],
["How is a flame test carried out?","Clean a nichrome wire in concentrated HCl and the flame. Dip it in the sample. Hold it in a blue Bunsen flame and note the colour."],
["What flame colour does lithium give?","Red (crimson)."],
["What flame colour does sodium give?","Yellow."],
["What flame colour does potassium give?","Lilac."],
["What flame colour does rubidium give?","Red-violet."],
["What flame colour does caesium give?","Blue-violet."],
["What flame colour does calcium give?","Orange-red (brick red)."],
["What flame colour does strontium give?","Red (crimson)."],
["What flame colour does barium give?","Pale green."],
["How do you test for an alkene?","Shake with bromine water. It decolourises from orange to colourless."],
["How do you identify the halogen in a halogenoalkane?","Warm with aqueous NaOH (or ethanol and water) to release halide ions. Acidify with dilute nitric acid and add silver nitrate. Use the precipitate colour and solubility in ammonia."],
["How does acidified dichromate distinguish primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols?","Primary and secondary alcohols turn it from orange to green. Primary gives an aldehyde or carboxylic acid, secondary a ketone. Tertiary alcohols do not react, so it stays orange."],
["How do you test for an aldehyde?","Any 2 of: warm with Fehling’s or Benedict’s solution, giving a brick-red precipitate; warm with Tollens’ reagent, giving a silver mirror."],
["How do you test for a carboxylic acid?","Add sodium carbonate or hydrogencarbonate. It fizzes, giving CO₂."],
["Which instrumental techniques help identify organic compounds?","Any 2 of: mass spectrometry; infrared spectroscopy; NMR spectroscopy."],
["How does mass spectrometry help identify a compound?","The molecular ion peak gives the relative molecular mass. Fragment peaks show pieces of the molecule."],
["How does infrared spectroscopy help identify a compound?","Absorptions at characteristic wavenumbers show which bonds are present. They are matched to data book values."]],
"WCH16/preparation-of-aspirin":[
["What by-product forms when 2-hydroxybenzoic acid reacts with ethanoic anhydride?","Ethanoic acid."],
["What type of bond forms in aspirin synthesis?","An ester bond."],
["Why might the measured yield of aspirin be too high?","Any 2 of: the product is not fully dry; impurities remain in it."],
["How does melting point show purity?","A pure substance melts sharply at its known melting point. Impurities lower it and make it melt over a range."],
["Give three safety precautions when making aspirin.","Any 3 of: use a fume cupboard or good ventilation; wear goggles and a lab coat; avoid skin contact with ethanoic anhydride and concentrated sulfuric acid; handle hot apparatus with care; clear up spills straight away."],
["What is the role of concentrated sulfuric acid in making aspirin?","It is a catalyst."],
["Why heat with a water bath rather than a Bunsen burner?","Any 2 of: steadier, controlled temperature; less risk of igniting flammable chemicals; less risk of overheating the mixture or cracking glassware."],
["Why cool the mixture in ice?","Aspirin is less soluble when cold, so more crystallises."],
["How do you reduce product loss when filtering?","Rinse the flask with a little cold solvent. Pour the washings into the Büchner funnel."],
["Why wash the aspirin crystals with ice-cold water?","To remove soluble impurities. Cold water dissolves very little product."],
["Which functional groups react to make the ester in aspirin?","The phenol –OH group of 2-hydroxybenzoic acid. It reacts with the acid anhydride group."],
["How are the aspirin crystals separated?","Filtration under reduced pressure with a Büchner funnel."],
["How is percentage yield calculated?","Actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100."],
["Why might the percentage yield of aspirin be below 100%?","Any 3 of: incomplete reaction; product left dissolved in the solution; losses during transfer; losses during recrystallisation."],
["Why might a percentage yield above 100% be calculated?","Any 2 of: crystals not fully dry; impurities in the crystals."],
["How is atom economy calculated?","Mr of desired product ÷ total Mr of reactants × 100."],
["Why is ethanoic anhydride used industrially instead of ethanoyl chloride?","Any 2 of: ethanoyl chloride gives off toxic, corrosive HCl; it reacts violently with water; the anhydride is cheaper and safer; the ethanoic acid by-product is useful."]],
"WBI13/benedicts-test":[
["How is the Benedict’s test carried out?","Add Benedict’s solution to the sample and heat in a water bath."],
["What is a positive result?","The blue solution changes through green, yellow and orange to a brick-red precipitate, depending on how much reducing sugar is present."],
["How do you test for a non-reducing sugar such as sucrose?","After a negative test, boil a fresh sample with dilute hydrochloric acid, neutralise with sodium hydrogencarbonate, then repeat the Benedict’s test."],
["How can the test be made quantitative?","Make a calibration curve with glucose solutions of known concentration using a colorimeter, then read the unknown from the curve."],
["What must be kept the same for a fair comparison?","Volume of sample and of Benedict’s solution, temperature and heating time."]],
"WBI13/vitamin-c-content":[
["Which reagent is used to measure vitamin C and what is the end point?","DCPIP. The blue solution turns colourless when it is reduced by vitamin C."],
["Outline the method.","Add the juice drop by drop to a fixed volume of DCPIP, shaking gently, until the blue colour just disappears. Record the volume added."],
["How is the vitamin C concentration worked out?","First find the volume of a standard vitamin C solution of known concentration needed to decolourise the same volume of DCPIP, then compare."],
["Why should the tube be shaken gently and for the same time?","Vigorous shaking adds oxygen, which can re-oxidise the DCPIP and turn it blue again."],
["Give one limitation of the method.","The end point is hard to judge in coloured juices."]],
"WBI13/investigating-membrane-properties":[
["Why is beetroot used to investigate membrane permeability?","Its cells contain a red pigment (betalain) that leaks out when membranes are damaged."],
["Outline the method for the effect of temperature.","Cut equal-sized pieces, rinse, and place them in equal volumes of water at different temperatures for the same time. Remove the pieces and measure the absorbance of each solution with a colorimeter."],
["Why are the pieces rinsed first?","To remove pigment released from cells that were cut open."],
["Explain why more pigment leaks at high temperature.","Membrane proteins denature and the phospholipids move more and become more fluid, so the membrane becomes more permeable."],
["What effect does ethanol have, and why?","It increases permeability because it dissolves the lipids in the membrane."]],
"WBI13/enzyme-reactions":[
["What is meant by the initial rate of reaction, and why is it measured?","The rate at the very start, found from the gradient of the tangent at time zero. Substrate is not yet limiting, so results can be compared fairly."],
["Name two ways of following an enzyme reaction.","Measure the volume of gas produced (e.g. oxygen from catalase), or use a colorimeter to follow a colour change."],
["Which variables must be controlled when investigating enzyme concentration?","Temperature, pH (with a buffer), substrate concentration and the volumes used."],
["What does the graph of initial rate against enzyme concentration look like when substrate is in excess?","A straight line through the origin: rate is directly proportional to enzyme concentration."],
["Why is a buffer solution used?","To keep the pH constant, because pH changes alter the shape of the active site."]],
"WBI13/microscopy":[
["How do you calibrate an eyepiece graticule?","Line it up with a stage micrometer and work out how many micrometres one eyepiece division represents at that magnification."],
["Why must the graticule be recalibrated for each objective lens?","The size each division represents changes with the magnification."],
["State the equation linking magnification, image size and actual size.","Actual size = image size ÷ magnification."],
["Give three rules for a good biological drawing.","Use a sharp pencil with clear, continuous lines; no shading; label with ruled lines that do not cross; include a title and magnification or scale."],
["Why are thin specimens and stains used?","Thin sections let light pass through. Stains make particular structures visible by increasing contrast."]],
"WBI13/stages-mitosis":[
["Which part of a plant is used to observe mitosis, and why?","The root tip (e.g. of garlic or onion), because it contains a meristem where cells are dividing."],
["Why is the root tip treated with warm hydrochloric acid?","To break down the middle lamella holding the cells together, so they can be spread into a single layer."],
["Name a suitable stain and say what it does.","Acetic orcein or toluidine blue. It stains the chromosomes so they are visible."],
["Why is the tip squashed under the coverslip?","To spread the cells into one thin layer so light passes through and individual cells can be seen."],
["How is the mitotic index calculated?","Number of cells in mitosis ÷ total number of cells counted."]],
"WBI13/tensile-strength-plant-fibres":[
["How are fibres extracted from plant stems?","Soak the stems in water for about a week (retting) to soften the tissue, then pull out and dry the fibres."],
["Outline how tensile strength is measured.","Clamp a fibre of known length between two supports and add masses to its centre one at a time until it breaks. Record the breaking mass."],
["Which variables must be controlled?","Length and diameter of the fibre, the humidity and how the masses are added."],
["Why are repeats needed?","Fibres vary naturally, so repeats and a mean give a more reliable result."],
["Give one safety precaution.","Wear eye protection and keep feet clear of falling masses."]],
"WBI13/antimicrobial-properties-plants":[
["Outline how to test a plant extract for antimicrobial properties.","Spread bacteria evenly over an agar plate, place paper discs soaked in the extract on the surface, incubate, and measure the clear zones around the discs."],
["What does a clear zone show?","Bacteria have been killed or prevented from growing there. A wider zone means a more effective extract."],
["What control is needed?","A disc soaked only in the solvent used to make the extract, to show that the solvent is not responsible."],
["Give three aseptic techniques.","Flame the neck of the culture bottle, sterilise the spreader and forceps, work near a Bunsen flame, and lift the lid as little as possible."],
["Why are the plates incubated at about 25 °C and not sealed completely?","To avoid growing pathogens that thrive at body temperature, and to let oxygen in so harmful anaerobes do not grow."]],
"WBI16/factors-affecting-photosynthesis":[
["Outline a method for investigating light intensity and the rate of photosynthesis.","Place pondweed in water with sodium hydrogencarbonate and a lamp at a measured distance. Count bubbles or measure the volume of oxygen collected in a set time, then change the distance."],
["How is light intensity related to distance from the lamp?","Light intensity is proportional to 1/distance²."],
["Why is sodium hydrogencarbonate added?","To supply carbon dioxide so that it is not a limiting factor."],
["How is temperature kept constant?","Stand the tube in a water bath or put a heat shield (a tank of water) between the lamp and the plant."],
["Why is the plant left for a few minutes after each change?","To let it adjust to the new conditions so the rate is steady before measuring."]],
"WBI16/ecology-habitat":[
["When would you use random sampling and when a transect?","Random sampling for a uniform area. A transect when there is a gradient of conditions across the habitat."],
["How are quadrats placed at random?","Lay out a grid with tape measures and use a random number generator to give coordinates."],
["Name three ways of recording abundance in a quadrat.","Counting individuals (density), percentage cover, and frequency of occurrence."],
["Name three abiotic factors you might measure and the equipment used.","Light intensity (light meter), soil pH (pH probe), temperature (thermometer), soil moisture (moisture probe)."],
["Which statistical test shows whether abundance is correlated with an abiotic factor?","Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient."]],
"WBI16/temperature-development-organisms":[
["Outline a method to investigate temperature and the hatching of brine shrimp.","Put equal numbers of eggs into equal volumes of salt water at a range of temperatures. Count the number of hatched larvae each day."],
["Which variables must be controlled?","Salt concentration, volume, pH, light, oxygen supply and the number of eggs."],
["How can the larvae be counted easily?","Shine a bright light at one side; the larvae swim towards it and can be pipetted out and counted."],
["Why does the hatching rate rise with temperature and then fall?","Enzyme reactions speed up as temperature increases, but above the optimum the enzymes denature."],
["State one ethical consideration.","Treat living organisms with care, use only as many as needed, and return surviving animals to suitable conditions."]],
"WBI16/microorganisms-liquid-culture":[
["Name two ways to measure the growth of microorganisms in liquid culture.","Count cells with a haemocytometer, measure turbidity with a colorimeter, or do dilution plating."],
["What is a serial dilution and why is it done?","Repeatedly diluting the culture by a fixed factor, so the cells are few enough to count accurately."],
["How is turbidity related to cell number?","More cells scatter and absorb more light, so absorbance is higher. A calibration curve converts absorbance to cell number."],
["What is the difference between a total count and a viable count?","A total count includes living and dead cells; a viable count includes only living cells that can form colonies."],
["Name two factors to control when growing the culture.","Temperature, pH, nutrient concentration and oxygen supply (shake the flask)."]],
"WBI16/effect-different-antibodies":[
["Outline the method for comparing antibiotics.","Spread bacteria evenly on agar, place discs containing different antibiotics on the surface, incubate, and measure the diameter of the clear zones."],
["What is the clear area called and what does its size show?","The zone of inhibition. A larger zone means the bacteria are more sensitive to that antibiotic."],
["Why might there be no clear zone around a disc?","The bacteria are resistant to that antibiotic."],
["Why should the bacteria be spread as an even lawn?","So growth is uniform and the zones can be compared fairly."],
["How should the plates be disposed of?","Sterilise them in an autoclave before throwing them away."]],
"WBI16/respiration-yeast":[
["Outline a method for investigating the rate of respiration in yeast.","Mix a yeast suspension with a sugar solution at a set temperature and measure the volume of carbon dioxide produced in a fixed time, or the time for an indicator such as methylene blue to lose its colour."],
["Why does methylene blue turn colourless?","It accepts hydrogen removed during respiration and is reduced, so faster respiration decolourises it sooner."],
["Why is the mixture left to reach the bath temperature before timing?","So the yeast and its enzymes are at the temperature being tested from the start."],
["Which variables must be controlled?","Concentration and volume of yeast and of sugar, pH and time."],
["Why is a layer of oil sometimes added on top?","To keep oxygen out so that respiration is anaerobic."]],
"WBI16/rate-respiration-rq":[
["What does a respirometer measure?","The volume of oxygen taken up by living organisms in a given time."],
["What is the soda lime (or potassium hydroxide) for?","It absorbs the carbon dioxide produced, so the movement of the liquid shows only oxygen uptake."],
["Why is a control tube with glass beads needed?","To correct for changes in volume caused by temperature or pressure changes."],
["How is the volume of oxygen calculated?","Distance moved by the liquid × cross-sectional area of the capillary tube (πr²)."],
["How can the respirometer be used to find RQ?","Repeat without the carbon dioxide absorber to find the net volume change, work out the CO₂ produced, and divide by the oxygen used."]],
"WBI16/effects-exercise":[
["What does a spirometer trace show?","Changes in lung volume with time, from which tidal volume, breathing rate and oxygen consumption can be found."],
["How are tidal volume and breathing rate read from the trace?","Tidal volume: the height of one peak above its trough at rest. Breathing rate: the number of peaks per minute."],
["How is minute ventilation calculated?","Tidal volume × breathing rate."],
["How is oxygen consumption found from the trace?","From the downward slope of the trace: the fall in volume per unit time, as oxygen is used and carbon dioxide is absorbed by soda lime."],
["State two safety precautions.","Use a sterilised mouthpiece, fresh soda lime, medical-grade oxygen, and stop if the person feels unwell."]],
"WBI16/production-amylase-cereal-grains":[
["What triggers amylase production in a germinating cereal grain?","The embryo releases gibberellin, which makes the aleurone layer produce amylase."],
["How is amylase activity detected on a starch agar plate?","Flood the plate with iodine solution. A clear zone where starch has been digested appears around the seed against a blue-black background."],
["Why are the seeds cut in half and only some halves contain the embryo?","To show that without the embryo no gibberellin is made, so no amylase is produced unless gibberellin is added."],
["How does gibberellin switch on amylase production?","It causes the genes for amylase to be transcribed, by removing a repressor of the transcription factor."],
["Why are the seeds surface-sterilised first?","To kill microorganisms that would also digest the starch."]]};
